Module 1: Research Methodology & CALA/SBP Essentials

Primary students utilize these foundational terms when planning, executing, and writing their Continuous Assessment Learning Activities (CALA) and School-Based Projects (SBPs).
• Continuous Assessment Learning Activity (CALA): A holistic student assessment framework focusing on practical, research-oriented tasks alongside formal exams.
• School-Based Project (SBP): A hands-on, community-focused research assignment where students solve a local real-world problem using local resources.
• Data Collection: The systematic gathering of specific facts, measurements, or observations using interviews, question sheets, or physical counts.
• Target Population: The specific group of people, animals, or objects being studied in a project (e.g., local poultry farmers in the village).
• Data Presentation: The process of organizing research findings visually using bar graphs, tally charts, tables, or pictograms.
• Problem Statement: A concise sentence written by the student highlighting an issue requiring a solution (e.g., “Soil erosion is reducing vegetable yields in our school garden.”).
• Recommendations: Actionable steps proposed by the student at the conclusion of their research to solve the stated problem.
• Plagiarism: The act of copying another student's work or taking a resource directly from the internet without giving credit.
• Interview Guide: A listed sequence of conversational questions prepared beforehand to ask an elder, local farmer, or community expert.
• Observation Method: Collecting research information carefully by watching and recording events without actively modifying the situation.

Module 2: Heritage Studies, Social Sciences & National Identity

This domain focuses heavily on indigenous knowledge systems, historical geography, civic structures, and Unhu/Vumunhu (Ubuntu) values.
• Unhu / Vumunhu: The deep-rooted African philosophical code of ethics that dictates communal responsibility, self-respect, integrity, and hospitality.
• Totem (Mitupopo / Iziduko): A symbolic emblem, typically an animal, representing a specific ancestral lineage, heavily researched for its role in preventing intermarriage and preserving historical identities.
• Extended Family: A structural family system encompassing parents, siblings, aunts, uncles, cousins, and grandparents living or working together.
• Mbuya Nehanda : A legendary spiritual leader and heroine of the First Chimurenga (1896-1897) who serves as a primary icon for heritage projects.
• Sekuru Kaguvi : A prominent spiritual medium and leader who, along with Nehanda, spearheaded early historical resistance against colonial occupation.
• Great Zimbabwe: An ancient stone city located near Masvingo that was constructed from dry-stone walls between the 11th and 15th centuries.
• Khami Ruins   : A historical monument situated near Bulawayo featuring complex stone terraces, serving as the capital of the Torwa State.
• Matobo Hills  : An area of dramatic granite landscapes south of Bulawayo containing a dense concentration of ancient San rock art and spiritual shrines.
• Zimbabwe Bird: The carved soapstone national emblem derived from Great Zimbabwe, representing the ancestral link between spirits and the living world.
• Flame Lily: The national flower of Zimbabwe, distinct for its bright yellow and red petals, protected by law as a heritage asset.
• Chindunduma: A historical school model developed during the liberation war to provide holistic education to young freedom fighters and refugees.
• The National Flag: The supreme civic symbol of the nation, containing multi-colored bands that represent natural resources, blood shed during the liberation war, and national peace.
• The National Anthem: The patriotic song titled “Simudzai Mureza wedu weZimbabwe” (Lift High the Flag of Zimbabwe), researched for its historical and ideological value.
• Chinyakare: A comprehensive term denoting traditional ways of living, performing arts, folklore, and historical customs of indigenous Zimbabweans.
• Lobola / Roora: The traditional practice of bride price payment, researched by students to understand its role in unifying two family networks.
• Chief (Mambo / Isiphathi): The traditional, institutional leader of a rural community responsible for land distribution, local law, and ancestral rituals.
• Headman (Sabhuku / Umlisa): The village-level leader who coordinates with the chief and keeps the village register or records for community development.
• Local Governance: The administrative system run by Rural District Councils (RDCs) and urban municipalities to maintain roads, clean water, and local schools.
• Judiciary System: The legal branch composed of traditional primary courts (village courts) up to the modern Magistrates and Supreme courts.
• Constitution: The supreme written law book of the Republic of Zimbabwe that defines the rights and obligations of every citizen.

Module 3: Agriculture, Soil Science & Climate-Smart Farming

As a foundation of the Zimbabwean economy, students investigate smallholder farming practices, conservation, and livestock production.
• Pfumvudza / Intwasa: A high-yield, climate-smart conservation agriculture system that relies on precise planting holes, heavy mulching, and targeted fertilization.
• Mulching: Covering the topsoil with dried grass, leaves, or crop residues to prevent soil moisture from escaping and to suppress weed growth.
• Soil Erosion: The carrying away of topsoil by natural forces like wind or fast-flowing rainwater, often caused by overgrazing or bad plowing.
• Siltation: The choking up of local rivers, dams, and lakes with mud and sand deposits carried down from eroded farmlands.
• Crop Rotation: The practice of growing different types of crops (e.g., maize followed by groundnuts) in the same field across successive seasons to keep the soil fertile.
• Legumes: Nitrogen-fixing plants such as groundnuts, round nuts (bambara nuts), cowpeas, and soybeans that improve soil nutrients.
• Cereal Crops: Grasses cultivated for their edible grains, forming the backbone of regional food supplies (e.g., maize, sorghum, pearl millet, finger millet).
• Cash Crops: Commercial plants grown purposely to be sold for local income or international export, such as tobacco, cotton, and sugarcane.
• Organic Fertilizer: Plant nutrients derived from natural materials like composted livestock manure, leaf mold, or decomposed kitchen waste.
• Inorganic Fertilizer: Commercially manufactured chemicals added to the soil to supply instant nitrogen, phosphorus, and potassium (e.g., Compound D, Ammonium Nitrate).
• Intercropping: The practice of growing two or more different types of crops simultaneously in the same field space (e.g., planting pumpkins beneath maize stalks).
• Drought-Resistant Crops: Hardy grains like sorghum (mapfunde) and millet (mhunga) that require very little rainfall to mature successfully.
• Overgrazing: Keeping too many livestock animals on a single piece of grazing land (bani) for too long, destroying the grass cover and causing erosion.
• Ruminant Animals: Herbivorous livestock like cattle, goats, and sheep that have a specialized four-compartment stomach to digest tough grass.
• Broiler Production: The commercial raising of fast-growing chickens specifically optimized for meat production within 4 to 6 weeks.
• Layer Production: The systematic management of specialized poultry varieties raised primarily for high-volume egg production over several months.
• Deciduous Fruit Trees: Fruit-bearing trees that shed all their leaves annually during the dry winter season (e.g., peaches, plums).
• Citrus Fruit Trees: Evergreen trees that thrive in warm regions and yield acidic fruits rich in vitamin C (e.g., Mazowe oranges, lemons).
• Acreage: A traditional and modern unit used to measure the physical surface area of agricultural land.
• Knapsack Sprayer: A backpack-mounted manual pumping device used by farmers to spray pesticides or liquid fertilizers onto crop fields.

Module 4: Science, Technology & Environmental Management

This section captures terms students lookup regarding local ecosystems, health hazards, sanitation, and sustainable energy alternative technologies.
• Waterborne Diseases: Highly contagious infections, such as Cholera and Typhoid, contracted by consuming water contaminated by untreated sewage.
• Vector: A living organism—most commonly a mosquito, tsetse fly, or freshwater snail—that carries and transmits infectious diseases to humans.
• Malaria: A life-threatening blood disease caused by parasites transmitted through the bite of an infected female Anopheles mosquito.
• Schistosomiasis (Bilharzia): A chronic parasitic disease caused by tiny flatworms that breed inside freshwater snails, entering humans through skin contact with contaminated river water.
• Deforestation: The uncontrolled cutting down of natural woodlands for firewood, household building poles, or wood to cure tobacco.
• Afforestation: The proactive practice of planting new forests on land areas where there were previously no trees growing.
• Reforestation: Replanting trees in a forest area where numbers have been depleted due to logging, clearing, or wildfires.
• Renewable Energy: Clean power harvested from endless natural sources like sunlight (solar), wind, or moving water (hydroelectric).
• Biogas: A clean-burning gas composed mostly of methane, produced by breaking down animal dung and organic waste inside an airtight underground pit (digester).
• Solar Energy: Electrical or thermal energy generated from sunlight using photovoltaic panels, heavily researched for rural lighting solutions.
• Ecosystem: A community where living organisms (plants, insects, animals) interact directly with non-living elements (soil, rocks, water, weather).
• Photosynthesis: The chemical process through which green plants use sunlight, carbon dioxide, and water to manufacture their own food while releasing oxygen.
• Pollution: The introduction of harmful materials, plastics, or poisonous chemical emissions into air, soil, or natural water systems.
• Biodegradable Materials: Substances that can be broken down naturally by soil bacteria and fungi within a short time (e.g., food scraps, paper).
• Non-biodegradable Materials: Tough synthetic matter like plastic bottles, glass, and aluminum tins that cannot rot or break down naturally.
• Information and Communication Technology (ICT): The overarching field of computing hardware, internet connectivity, and mobile applications used for learning.
• Hardware: The physical parts of a computer system that a student can touch, including the central processing unit (CPU), keyboard, monitor, and mouse.
• Software: A collection of digital programs, operational instructions, and educational web packages that tell computer hardware exactly what tasks to run.
• Internet: A global network linking millions of computer devices together, allowing students to access digital textbooks and search for remote information.
• E-Learning: An educational framework that utilizes electronic media, internet platforms, and digital tablets to deliver school lessons.

