• Verb (Chiito / Isenzo): A core grammatical word used to describe an action, state of being, or physical occurrence.
• Adjective (Chipauro / Isiphawulo): A descriptive word that modifies, clarifies, or gives extra details about a noun.
• Comprehension: The active cognitive ability to read a passage of text, understand its core meaning, and accurately answer analytical questions about it.
• Composition: A multi-paragraph piece of original writing where a student constructs a story, descriptive essay, or formal letter.
• Synonym: A word that has the exact same or an exceptionally close meaning to another word within the same language.
• Antonym: A word that expresses the polar opposite meaning of another given word.
• Homophone: Words that sound identical when spoken aloud but differ completely in their spelling, origin, and definitions (e.g., hear and here).
• Prefix (Chivakashure / Isiqalo): A letter or group of letters fixed firmly to the beginning of a root word to alter its meaning or grammatical class.
• Suffix (Chivakashure chekumashure / Isijeskelo): A structural element added to the tail end of a root word to determine tense, plurality, or function.
• Direct Speech: The exact words spoken by a person, enclosed tightly within quotation marks during writing.
• Indirect Speech: Reporting what someone else said without repeating their exact words, removing quotation marks and shifting verb tenses backward.
• Punctuation: A system of standard marks (periods, commas, question marks, exclamation points) used to separate sentences and make text readable.
• Simile: A literary comparison device used to show similarity between two distinct things using the connecting words “like” or “as”.
• Metaphor: A direct figure of speech that states one thing is another thing to highlight a shared characteristic, without using "like" or "as."
• Dialogue: A written conversation unfolding between two or more characters in a story or educational play script.

