• Intercropping: The practice of growing two or more different types of crops simultaneously in the same field space (e.g., planting pumpkins beneath maize stalks).
• Zero Tillage: A farming method where the soil is not plowed or turned over, keeping organic matter intact and reducing erosion risks.
• Compost: A rich organic fertilizer made from layers of decomposed plant material, dry leaves, and kitchen scraps left to rot over time.
• Manure: Animal waste (cow dung, chicken droppings) collected and dried out to be used as an organic fertilizer to enrich field nutrients.
• Compound D: A widely used commercial inorganic fertilizer in Zimbabwe that supplies essential Nitrogen, Phosphorus, and Potassium (NPK) to young crops.
• Ammonium Nitrate (AN): A chemical top-dressing fertilizer rich in nitrogen, applied to crops mid-season to encourage green growth and high yields.
• Legumes: Plants like groundnuts, round nuts, and cowpeas that harbor beneficial root bacteria to fix nitrogen directly into the soil.
• Cereal Crops: Cultivated grasses grown for their edible grains, forming regional food supplies (e.g., maize, sorghum, pearl millet, finger millet).
• Cash Crops: Commercial plants grown purposely to be sold for local income or international export, such as tobacco, cotton, and sugarcane.
• Drought-Resistant Crops: Hardy grains like sorghum (mapfunde) and millet (mhunga) that require very little rainfall to mature successfully.
• Weeding: The physical pulling or hoeing out of unwanted plants that compete with target crops for nutrients, sunlight, and water.
• Thinning: Removing excess weak seedlings from a planting hole to allow the strongest remaining plants enough space to grow properly.
• Transplanting: Moving a young, delicate seedling from a protected nursery bed into its permanent growing spot in a field or garden.
• Siltation: The choking up of local rivers, dams, and lakes with mud and sand deposits carried down from eroded farmlands.
• Soil Erosion: The washing away of fertile topsoil by heavy rain or wind, often caused by overgrazing or poor plowing habits.
• Overgrazing: Keeping too many livestock animals on a single piece of grazing land (bani) for too long, destroying the grass cover and causing erosion.

Livestock & Horticulture

• Ruminant: An animal with a specialized four-compartment stomach (like cattle, sheep, and goats) that enables it to digest tough grass.
• Non-Ruminant: An animal with a simple, single-compartment stomach system (such as pigs and poultry) requiring concentrated feeds.
• Broiler Production: Raising fast-growing chickens optimized for commercial meat production within 4 to 6 weeks.
• Layer Production: The management of poultry varieties raised primarily for high-volume egg production over several months.
• Vaccination: Giving livestock medicine via injections or water additives to protect them against deadly outbreaks like Newcastle disease or Anthrax.
• Diaster / Dipping: The regular driving of cattle through a chemical-filled water trench (dip tank) to kill disease-carrying ticks.
• Tick-Borne Diseases: Deadly cattle illnesses, such as January Disease (Theileriosis), transmitted through the bites of infected ticks.
• Horticulture: The intensive cultivation of fruits, vegetables, flowers, and ornamental plants for food or income.
• Deciduous Fruit Trees: Fruit-bearing trees that shed all their leaves annually during the dry winter season (e.g., peaches, plums).
• Citrus Fruit Trees: Evergreen trees that thrive in warm regions and yield acidic fruits rich in vitamin C (e.g., Mazowe oranges, lemons).
• Nursery Bed: A small, carefully prepared, sheltered patch of soil used to raise delicate seeds until they are strong enough to be transplanted.
• Knapsack Sprayer: A backpack-mounted manual pumping device used by farmers to spray pesticides or liquid fertilizers onto crop fields.
• Pruning: The careful cutting away of dead, diseased, or overgrown plant branches to encourage healthy fruit growth.
• Irrigation: Supplying water to dry crop fields using man-made pipes, sprinklers, or channels rather than relying only on rainfall.
• Drip Irrigation: A water-saving method that delivers slow, precise drops of water directly to the root zone of plants through plastic pipes.
• Silage: High-moisture animal fodder made from green crops like maize, packed airtight into a pit to ferment for winter livestock feed.
• Poultry Run: A secure, enclosed wire or brick shelter built to protect chickens from bad weather and predators like eagles or foxes.
• Forage: Grasses and herbaceous plants eaten by livestock animals while grazing in open fields.
• Broadcasting: An old farming method of scattering seeds or fertilizer over a large field surface by hand.
• Row Planting: Sowing seeds in straight, measured lines to make weeding, fertilizing, and harvesting easier.

MODULE 5: SCIENCE, TECHNOLOGY & HEALTH


Public Health, Sanitation, & Pathogens

• Waterborne Diseases: Highly contagious infections, such as Cholera and Typhoid, contracted by consuming water contaminated by untreated sewage.
• Vector: A living organism—most commonly a mosquito, tsetse fly, or freshwater snail—that carries and transmits infectious diseases to humans.
• Malaria: A life-threatening blood disease caused by parasites transmitted through the bite of an infected female Anopheles mosquito.
• Schistosomiasis (Bilharzia): A chronic parasitic disease caused by tiny flatworms that breed inside freshwater snails, entering humans through skin contact with contaminated river water.
• Pathogen: A scientific term for any microscopic organism (virus, bacteria, fungus, or parasite) capable of causing disease.
• Sanitation: The hygienic maintenance of clean living spaces through safe disposal of human waste and trash, preventing disease spread.
• Contamination: The introduction of dangerous bacteria, chemicals, or waste into clean water, food, or environments.
• Sterilization: The process of killing all living microorganisms on an object using extreme heat, boiling water, or strong chemicals.
• Dehydration: A dangerous condition where the human body loses more water than it takes in, often caused by severe diarrhea or vomiting.
• Oral Rehydration Solution (ORS): A simple life-saving mixture of clean water, salt, and sugar given to patients to restore lost body fluids.
• Kwashiorkor: A form of malnutrition caused by a lack of protein in a child's diet, showing symptoms like a swollen belly and thinning hair.
• Marasmus: Severe malnutrition caused by a lack of overall calories and energy, making a child look thin and wasted away.
• Balanced Diet: A meal containing the correct amounts of carbohydrates, proteins, fats, vitamins, minerals, water, and fiber needed for health.
• Immunity: The body's natural or vaccine-assisted ability to resist and fight off infections and diseases.
• Personal Hygiene: Regular actions like bathing, washing hands with soap, and brushing teeth to maintain health and prevent illness.
• First Aid Kit: A portable box containing bandages, antiseptics, and medical tools used to give emergency care right after an injury.

Matter, Energy, & Ecosystems

• Ecosystem: A community where living organisms (plants, insects, animals) interact directly with non-living elements (soil, rocks, water, weather).
• Photosynthesis: The chemical process through which green plants use sunlight, carbon dioxide, and water to manufacture their own food while releasing oxygen.
• Deforestation: The uncontrolled cutting down of natural woodlands for firewood, household building poles, or wood to cure tobacco.
• Afforestation: The proactive practice of planting new forests on land areas where there were previously no trees growing.
• Reforestation: Replanting trees in a forest area where numbers have been depleted due to logging, clearing, or wildfires.
• Renewable Energy: Clean power harvested from endless natural sources like sunlight (solar), wind, or moving water (hydroelectric).
• Biogas: A clean-burning gas composed mostly of methane, produced by breaking down animal dung and organic waste inside an airtight underground pit (digester).
• Solar Energy: Electrical or thermal energy generated from sunlight using photovoltaic panels, heavily researched for rural lighting solutions.
• Biodegradable: Substances that can be broken down naturally by soil bacteria and fungi within a short time (e.g., food scraps, paper).
• Non-biodegradable: Tough synthetic matter like plastic bottles, glass, and aluminum tins that cannot rot or break down naturally.
• Pollution: The introduction of harmful materials, plastics, or poisonous chemical emissions into air, soil, or natural water systems.
• Global Warming: The long-term rise in Earth's temperature caused by greenhouse gases trapping heat in the atmosphere.
• Water Cycle: The continuous movement of water from Earth's surface into the sky through evaporation, turning into clouds, and returning as rain.
• Evaporation: The physical process where heat turns a liquid substance into an invisible gas or vapor.
• Condensation: The process where gas cools down and turns back into a liquid, creating rain clouds.
• Precipitation: Any form of water falling from the sky to Earth's surface, including rain, hail, or snow.
• Habitat: The specific natural home or environment where an animal, insect, or plant species naturally lives and grows.
• Adaptation: A special physical or behavioral feature that helps an organism survive in its specific environment (e.g., a camel's hump).
• Matter: Anything that has weight and takes up physical space, existing as a solid, liquid, or gas.
• Gravity: The invisible pulling force that drags all physical objects downward toward the center of the Earth.

Information & Communication Technology (ICT)

• Information and Communication Technology (ICT): The overarching field of computing hardware, internet connectivity, and mobile applications used for learning.
• Hardware: The physical parts of a computer system that a student can touch, including the central processing unit (CPU), keyboard, monitor, and mouse.
• Software: A collection of digital programs, operational instructions, and educational web packages that tell computer hardware exactly what tasks to run.
• Internet: A global network linking millions of computer devices together, allowing students to access digital textbooks and search for remote information.
• E-Learning: An educational framework that utilizes electronic media, internet platforms, and digital tablets to deliver school lessons.
• Browser: A software application used to open, view, and navigate websites on the World Wide Web (e.g., Google Chrome).
• Search Engine: An online tool that sifts through internet databases to find relevant web pages when a student types in research keywords.
• Keyboarding: The physical skill of typing text accurately and efficiently using the keys of a computer or laptop keyboard.
• Data Security: Protecting digital files, school projects, and personal information from unauthorized access, hacking, or corruption.
• Virus (Digital): A malicious program written to infect a computer, delete files, steal data, or slow down system performance.
• Anti-Virus: A protective software tool used to scan, detect, and delete digital viruses from a computer system.
• Word Processor: A software program used to type, edit, format, and print written text documents on a computer (e.g., Microsoft Word).
• Spreadsheet: A grid-based software program used to organize numbers, calculate formulas, and create charts (e.g., Microsoft Excel).
• File: A digital unit of stored information, such as a text document, photo, song, or video.
• Folder: A digital storage container used to organize multiple computer files into clear groups.
• Mouse: A hand-held pointing device used to move the cursor and click on items on a computer screen.
• Monitor: The electronic display screen that shows visual text, images, and videos generated by a computer.
• CPU (Central Processing Unit): The main microchip or "brain" of a computer that processes data and carries out software instructions.
• Storage Device: Hardware items like USB flash drives or hard disks used to save files and data so they aren't lost when a computer turns off.
• Digital Literacy: Having the skills to confidently navigate computers, use software, and evaluate online research safely.

MODULE 6: GEOGRAPHY & OUTDOOR STUDIES


Physical Topography & Water Systems

• Landlocked Country: A country surrounded entirely by land with no direct access to an ocean, sharing borders with multiple neighbors (Zimbabwe borders Zambia, Mozambique, South Africa, and Botswana).
• The Highveld: A high-altitude ridge running southwest to northeast across Zimbabwe that enjoys a mild, pleasant climate.
• The Middleveld: The plateau flanking both sides of the Highveld, sitting at an altitude between 900 and 1,200 meters above sea level.
• The Lowveld: Hot, dry lowland regions located in the southern and northern borders of Zimbabwe, known for vast wildlife areas and sugarcane estates.
• The Eastern Highlands: The mountainous border region with Mozambique, featuring Mount Nyangani (Zimbabwe's highest peak) and Vumba.
• Mosi-oa-Tunya (Victoria Falls): Meaning "The Smoke That Thunders," it is one of the world's largest waterfalls, situated on the Zambezi River.
• Lake Kariba: The largest man-made lake in the world by volume, providing hydroelectric power to Zimbabwe and Zambia.
• Zambezi River: A major African river forming Zimbabwe's northern border with Zambia, feeding Victoria Falls and Lake Kariba.
• Limpopo River: The river forming Zimbabwe's southern border with South Africa, flowing through dry Lowveld ecosystems.
• Save River: A major domestic river draining the eastern and southeastern parts of the country, heavily researched for issues regarding siltation.
• Catchment Area: An area of land where all falling rainfall collects and drains off into a common river, lake, or reservoir.
• Water Table: The upper underground level where the soil and rocks are completely soaked with water, tapped by digging wells and boreholes.
• Borehole: A deep, narrow well drilled by machines into the ground to pump clean, filtered underground water up to communities.
• Dam: A thick concrete or earthen wall built across a flowing river to block and store water for town use, cattle, or crop irrigation.
• Plateau: A large, flat area of land that sits elevated high above sea level.
• Mountain Range: A continuous line of connected mountains or steep hills running closely together.
• Valley: A low-lying stretch of land nestled safely between hills or mountain ridges, often carved out by a river over time.
• Tributary: A smaller river or freshwater stream that flows into and joins a larger main river.
• Estuary: The wide mouth of a river where its fresh water meets and mixes with the salty water of an ocean.
• Silt: Fine sand, clay, or soil particles carried by moving river waters and dropped as sediment at the bottom of dams or pools.

Climate, Weather, & Conservation

• Rainfall Season (Chizhumishe): The warm, rainy period in Zimbabwe stretching from mid-November to March, crucial for national crop production.
• Winter Season (Chando): The dry, cold period stretching from late May to August, dominated by clear skies and cold nights.
• Drought: A long period of time with little to no rainfall, leading to water shortages, crop failures, and livestock deaths.
• El Niño: A natural climate pattern causing warmer ocean waters that frequently brings severe droughts to Southern Africa, including Zimbabwe.
• Meteorology: The scientific study of weather patterns, atmosphere changes, and forecasting techniques.
• Rain Gauge: A marked cylinder tool used to collect and measure the amount of rain that falls over a specific time.
• Thermometer: A medical or meteorological tool used to measure temperatures in degrees Celsius.
• Anemometer: A weather instrument featuring rotating cups used to measure the speed of moving wind.
• Wind Vane: A spinning arrow tool placed high up to show the directional origin of blowing wind (North, South, East, West).
• Barometer: A scientific instrument used to measure atmospheric air pressure, helping predict sudden weather changes.
• National Parks: Protected wildlife areas run by ZimParks to conserve animals and environments while hosting tourists (e.g., Hwange National Park).
• The Big Five: The group of large African animals heavily researched in conservation studies: Lion, Leopard, Elephant, Cape Buffalo, and Rhinoceros.
• Poaching: The illegal hunting, trapping, or killing of protected wild animals in game reserves or national parks.
• Camouflage: A natural coloring or pattern that allows an animal to blend into its surroundings to hide from predators or prey.
• Endangered Species: Animals or plants at a high risk of going extinct soon due to poaching, habitat loss, or climate changes.
• Game Reserve: A large area of wild land set aside specifically for protecting and breeding wild animals.
• Savanah: A wide, open grassland ecosystem dotted with scattered trees, covering most of Zimbabwe’s landscape.
• Woodland: An area of land covered thick with trees and shrubs, providing firewood and wildlife homes.
• Natural Region I: High-altitude, cool areas in Zimbabwe receiving very high rainfall, suited for forestry, tea, coffee, and fruit farming.
• Natural Region V: Hot, low-lying areas receiving low, erratic rainfall, suited only for cattle ranching and wild game management.

MODULE 7: PHYSICAL EDUCATION, ARTS & MASS DISPLAYS


Movement, Sports, & First Aid

• Mass Display: A highly choreographed performance blending dance, gymnastics, music, and formations to celebrate national identity and health.
• Gymnastics: A physical sport involving structured body movements that build agility, balance, core strength, and flexibility.
• Aerobics: Active rhythmic exercises performed to upbeat music to increase heart rates and improve cardiovascular health.
• Agility: The physical ability to change the direction of the body rapidly and smoothly while maintaining complete balance.
• Coordination: The ability to move different parts of the body together smoothly and efficiently to complete a task (e.g., catching a ball).
• Endurance: The physical stamina to keep exercising or playing a sport for a long time without getting overly tired.
• Flexibility: The range of motion allowed around your body joints, built through regular stretching exercises.
• Cardiovascular Fitness: How efficiently your heart and lungs can deliver oxygen to active muscles during long workouts.
• Warm-Up: Gentle, slow exercises done right before intense sports to prepare muscles and avoid painful injuries.
• Cool-Down: Easy movements and slow stretches done at the end of a workout to help heart rates return to normal safely.
• Sprain: A painful injury occurring when body ligaments around a joint are stretched or torn during sports.
• Fracture: A medical term for a partial or complete break in a bone, requiring immediate stabilization and casting.
• Resuscitation (CPR): An emergency first-aid procedure combining chest compressions and rescue breaths to help a person whose heart has stopped.
• Splint: A rigid support piece made of wood or plastic tied to a broken limb to keep it from moving while seeking a doctor.
• Antiseptic: A mild liquid chemical used to clean cuts and scrapes to kill germs and prevent infections.
• Tourniquet: A tightly wrapped band used in medical emergencies to stop severe bleeding from a deep wound or limb injury.
• Dehydration (Sports): Losing too much body water through heavy sweating during physical games, fixed by drinking clean water.
• Heat Stroke: A dangerous health condition where the body overheats from playing too long under hot, direct Zimbabwean sunlight.
• Sportsmanship: Following rules, playing fair, treating teammates and opponents with respect, and being a graceful winner or loser.
• Referee / Umpire: The official on-field judge tasked with enforcing game rules fairly and ensuring player safety.

Traditional Arts & Musical Instruments

• Mbira: An indigenous musical instrument made of rowed metal keys fixed to a wooden soundboard, played inside a large calabash resonator.
• Marimba: A musical instrument made of tuned wooden bars arranged over resonators, struck with soft mallets to create melodic sounds.
• Hosho: Traditional percussion shakers made from dried gourd shells filled with small seeds, used to keep time in traditional music.
• Ngoma / Isungu: A traditional skin-covered wooden drum struck with hands or sticks to provide deep rhythms for community dances.
• Jerusarema / Mbende Dance: A traditional Shona dance featuring quick foot movements and acrobatics, honored as an important piece of cultural heritage.
• Muchongoyo Dance: A powerful, rhythmic dance traditionally performed by warriors to build spirit before battle or celebrate victories.
• Isitshikitsha Dance: A traditional Ndebele dance performed at social gatherings and weddings, featuring graceful upper body movements.
• Visual Arts: Creative art fields meant to be enjoyed with your eyes, including clay pottery, stone sculpting, wood carving, and painting.
• Pottery (Hari / Izimbiza): The traditional craft of molding wet clay into pots, bowls, and jars, which are then baked hard in a fire.
• Sculpture: The world-famous Zimbabwean art form of carving beautiful figures, animals, or human stories out of hard stone blocks.
• Weaving: Interlocking long strips of reeds, grasses, or fibers together to create functional sleeping mats (bonde), baskets, or hats.
• Beadwork: The creative craft of sewing tiny colored beads onto fabric or string to make traditional headbands, necklaces, and ceremonial belts.
• Folksong: A traditional cultural song passed down through generations, sung during farming, festivals, or funerals.
• Choreography: The process of designing and arranging a sequence of dance steps and movements for a performance or mass display.
• Tempo: The speed or pace at which a piece of music is played or sung (e.g., fast, slow, steady).
• Rhythm: A strong, regular, repeated pattern of sounds or movements that forms the heartbeat of music and dance.
• Melody: A pleasing sequence of musical notes played or sung in a specific order to create a memorable tune.
• Harmony: Two or more different musical notes sung or played at the same time to create a pleasing sound.
• Audition: A short performance given by a student to show their skills when trying out for a school play, choir, or dance group.
• Artifact: An object made by a human being, typically one of cultural or historical interest, that tells a story about how ancestors lived.

MODULE 8: RESEARCH ESSENTIALS & DATA COMPILATION

• Continuous Assessment Learning Activity (CALA): A holistic student assessment framework focusing on practical, research-oriented tasks alongside formal exams.
• School-Based Project (SBP): A hands-on, community-focused research assignment where students solve a local real-world problem using local resources.
• Data Collection: The systematic gathering of specific facts, measurements, or observations using interviews, question sheets, or physical counts.
• Target Population: The specific group of people, animals, or objects being studied in a project (e.g., local poultry farmers in the village).
• Data Presentation: The process of organizing research findings visually using bar graphs, tally charts, tables, or pictograms.
• Problem Statement: A concise sentence written by the student highlighting an issue requiring a solution (e.g., “Soil erosion is reducing vegetable yields in our school garden.”).
• Recommendations: Actionable steps proposed by the student at the conclusion of their research to solve the stated problem.
• Plagiarism: The act of copying another student's work or taking a resource directly from the internet without giving credit.
• Interview Guide: A listed sequence of conversational questions prepared beforehand to ask an elder, local farmer, or community expert.
• Observation Method: Collecting research information carefully by watching and recording events without actively modifying the situation.

Physics, Mechanics, & Energy Dynamics

• Velocity:The speed of an object measured in a specific vector direction over a unit of time.
• Acceleration:The rate of change of an object's velocity over time, measured in meters per second squared.
• Inertia:  The natural resistance of any physical object to any change in its current state of rest or motion.
• Friction:  The resistive force that opposes the sliding or rolling motion of two solid surfaces touching each other.
• Terminal Velocity:  The constant maximum speed achievable by a falling object when the force of gravity matches the air resistance.
• Kinetic Energy:  The mechanical energy possessed by an object or body by virtue of being in active motion.
• Potential Energy:  The stored energy possessed by an object due to its structural position or configuration.
• Law of Conservation of Energy:  A fundamental rule stating that energy cannot be created or destroyed, only transformed from one state to another.
• Conduction:  The transfer of heat energy or electricity directly through a solid material from molecule to molecule.
• Convection:  The movement of heat through liquids or gases as warmer, less dense fluids rise and colder fluids sink.
• Radiation:  The emission or transfer of energy in the form of electromagnetic waves or particles through a vacuum or space.
• Refraction: The bending of light waves when passing from one transparent medium to another of different density.
• Refraction Index: :  A mathematical ratio measuring how much a ray of light bends when entering a specific medium.
• Total Internal Reflection:  The complete reflection of a light ray back into its original medium when hitting a boundary at a steep angle.
• Focal Length:  The exact physical distance between the center of a curved lens or mirror and its focal point.
• Direct Current (DC):  An electric current that flows continuously in one single direction through a circuit.
• Alternating Current (AC): An electric current that reverses its direction of flow at regular intervals within a power grid.
• Ohm's Law:  A rule stating that electric current is directly proportional to voltage and inversely proportional to resistance.
• Electrical Resistance:  The structural property of a component or material that opposes the free flow of electric current.
• Transformer: A device used to increase or decrease the voltage of an alternating electrical current.

Chemistry, Matter, & Periodicity

• Atom:  The smallest indivisible particle of a chemical element that retains all the structural properties of that element.
• Molecule:  A group of two or more chemically bonded atoms forming the smallest fundamental unit of a compound.
• Element:  A pure chemical substance consisting entirely of one single type of atom that cannot be broken down simpler.
• Compound:  A chemical substance composed of two or more separate elements bonded together in a fixed weight ratio.
• Mixture:  A material system made up of two or more different substances which are physically mixed but not chemically combined.
• Isotope:  Atoms of the exact same chemical element that have the same number of protons but different numbers of neutrons.
• Allotrope:  Structurally different physical forms of the same chemical element (e.g., diamond and graphite are allotropes of carbon).
• Ionic Bonding:  A type of chemical link formed through the complete transfer of electrons from a metal atom to a non-metal atom.
• Covalent Bonding:  A chemical link formed when two non-metal atoms share pairs of valence electrons to gain stability.
• Metallic Bonding:  The chemical binding force that holds metal atoms together within a shared pool of free-moving electrons.
• Valency:  A numerical value representing the combining power of an element based on the electrons it can lose, gain, or share.
• Exothermic Reaction:  A chemical process or reaction that releases thermal energy into its surrounding environment.
• Endothermic Reaction:  A chemical reaction that absorbs heat energy from its surroundings to proceed.
• Catalyst:  A substance that increases the speed of a chemical reaction without being consumed or permanently altered itself.
• Oxidation:  A chemical process involving the loss of electrons or the gain of oxygen by an atom or molecule.
• Reduction: A chemical process involving the gain of electrons or the loss of oxygen by an atom or molecule.
• Electrolysis:  The passage of a direct electric current through an electrolyte solution to trigger a chemical decomposition.
• Anode:  The positively charged electrode in an electrical cell toward which anions move during electrolysis.
• Cathode:  The negatively charged electrode in an electrical cell toward which cations migrate.
• Saturated Solution:  A chemical solution that contains the maximum possible amount of dissolved solute at a specific temperature.

Secondary & Higher Mathematics

• Algebraic Expression:  A mathematical phrase combining numbers, variables, and operational symbols without an equals sign.
• Quadratic Equation:  A polynomial equation of the second degree, written in the standard form ax² + bx + c = 0.
• Simultaneous Equations: A set of two or more equations containing common variables that must be solved at the same time.
• Matrix:  A rectangular grid array of numbers or variables organized neatly into rows and columns.
• Determinant: A scalar numeric value calculated from a square matrix that helps determine if it has an inverse.
• Vector:  A geometric quantity that possesses both a defined numerical magnitude and a specific physical direction.
• Probability: A mathematical ratio measuring the likelihood or chance that a specific event will occur, from 0 to 1.
• Hypotenuse:  The longest side of a right-angled triangle, always located directly opposite the 90-degree angle.
• Pythagoras' Theorem:  A geometric rule stating that in a right triangle, the square of the hypotenuse equals the sum of the squares of the other sides (a² + b² = c²).
• Trigonometr: The branch of mathematics dealing with the specific relationships between the side lengths and angles of triangles.
• Standard Deviation:  A statistical measure calculating how spread out a set of numbers is from their calculated average.
• Arithmetic Progression (AP):  A mathematical sequence of numbers where the difference between consecutive terms remains constant.
• Geometric Progression (GP):  A sequence of numbers where each term after the first is found by multiplying the previous term by a fixed number.
• Differentiation:  The calculus process of finding the exact rate of change or gradient of a function at any given point.
• Integration:  The calculus process of calculating the total area enclosed under a function's curve.
• Locus:  A set of points whose location is determined by a specific mathematical rule or geometric condition.
• Polygon:  A closed, flat, two-dimensional geometric shape bounded completely by three or more straight lines.
• Scalar:  A physical quantity that possesses magnitude or size but has no defined direction (e.g., mass, time).
• Symmetrical Component: A geometric element that matches perfectly across an axis of reflection or rotation.
• Inequality:  A mathematical statement comparing two expressions that are unequal, using signs like greater than (>) or less than (<).

MODULE 10: HUMAN BIOLOGY, GENETICS, & HEALTH ADAPTATIONS

• Cell Membrane:  The flexible outer boundary of a cell that controls which substances enter and exit.
• Cytoplasm:  The jelly-like fluid fluid filling the inside of a cell where most chemical activities take place.
• Nucleus:  The master control center of a cell housing genetic blueprints and coordinating activities like growth.
• Mitochondrion:  The rod-shaped cell organelle responsible for carrying out respiration to generate cellular energy.
• Ribosome:  A tiny cellular structure responsible for manufacturing proteins by linking amino acids together.
• Chloroplast:  An organelle found inside green plant cells containing chlorophyll to capture light for photosynthesis.
• Mitosis:  A type of cell division resulting in two identical daughter cells, used for physical growth and tissue repair.
• Meiosis:  A specialized type of cell division that reduces chromosome numbers by half to create reproductive sperm or egg cells.
• Enzyme:  A biological catalyst composed of protein that accelerates metabolic chemical reactions inside organisms.
• Peristalsis:  The wave-like muscular contractions of the digestive tract that push food downward through the system.
• Hemoglobin:  The iron-rich protein molecule inside red blood cells responsible for transporting oxygen throughout the body.
• Artery:  A thick-walled, elastic blood vessel that carries oxygenated blood away from the heart to body tissues.
• Vein:  A thin-walled blood vessel equipped with valves that carries deoxygenated blood back toward the heart.
• Capillary:  A microscopic blood vessel with single-cell walls where nutrients, gas, and waste exchange occurs.
• Alveolus:  A tiny, thin-walled air sac inside human lungs where oxygen enters the blood and carbon dioxide leaves.
• Nephron:  The microscopic functional filtering unit inside human kidneys responsible for cleansing blood and forming urine.
• Homeostasis:  The internal self-regulating process by which an organism maintains a stable environment despite shifting outside conditions.
• Hormone:  A chemical messenger produced by endocrine glands that travels through blood to regulate target organs.
• Pathogen Transmission:  The mechanisms through which harmful microbes spread from an infected person or animal to a healthy individual.
• Antigen:  Any foreign substance or protein that triggers an active immune response and antibody production inside the body.

MODULE 11: GEOGRAPHY, GEOMORPHOLOGY, & URBAN PLANNING

• Folding:  The bending of rock layers within Earth's crust caused by immense horizontal compression forces.
• Faulting:  The fracturing and displacement of rock strata along a line of weakness due to extreme crustal tension or compression.
• Rift Valley:  A steep-sided valley formed by the downward sliding of land blocks between parallel faults (e.g., East African Rift System).
• Weathering:  The mechanical breaking down or chemical decomposition of rocks exposed at Earth's surface by weather elements.
• Exfoliation:  A mechanical weathering process where outer rock layers peel away due to repeated expansion and contraction from temperature shifts.
• Mass Wasting:  The downward movement of loose soil, rock, and debris down a slope driven primarily by gravity.
• Oxbow Lake:  A crescent-shaped pool formed when a looping river meander is cut off from the main flowing river channel.
• River Rejuvenation:  The process where a river gains fresh erosive energy due to a drop in sea level or an uplift of the land landscape.
• Urbanization:  The continuous migration of human populations from rural locations to live in towns and cities.
• Rural-Urban Migration:  The movement of people away from rural villages to cities in search of jobs, better schools, and amenities.
• Squatter Settlement:  An informal residential area built from scrap materials on land occupied illegally without municipal permits.
• Central Business District (CBD):  The commercial, financial, and retail core of a city characterized by high land values and high-rise offices.
• Industrial Inertia:  The tendency of a factory or industry to remain located in its original spot even after its initial location advantages have disappeared.
• Subsistence Farming:  A small-scale farming model where families grow food primarily to feed themselves, with little left to sell.
• Commercial Agriculture:  Large-scale farming managed with machinery and modern inputs aimed purely at maximizing crop profits for market sales.
