• Denominator: The bottom number in a fraction, indicating the total number of equal parts into which a whole has been divided.
• Density: A physical property defined as the mass of a substance per unit volume.
• Depreciation: The systematic allocation of the cost of a tangible fixed asset over its useful economic life due to wear and tear or obsolescence.
• Desertification: The gradual transformation of fertile agricultural or grazing land into dry desert terrain due to drought, deforestation, or land mismanagement.
• Determinant: A scalar numeric value derived from a square matrix that characterizes its properties, such as whether an inverse matrix exists.
• Dialogue: A written conversation unfolding between two or more characters in a story, essay, or script.
• Differentiated Learning: A teaching method that adapts lessons to match the unique learning styles, speeds, and academic abilities of individual students.
• Differentiation: A calculus operation that determines the exact rate of change, derivative, or instantaneous gradient of a function at a specific point.
• Diffusion: The net movement of particles from a region of higher concentration to a region of lower concentration down a concentration gradient due to random thermal motion.
• Digital Data: Information that is converted and stored into discrete numeric values, typically binary digits, for electronic processing.
• Digital Literacy: Having the practical skills to confidently navigate computers, use software applications, and evaluate online research safely.
• Dip Tank: A deep, narrow concrete trench filled with water and liquid chemical acaricides through which cattle are driven to kill ticks.
• Direct Current (DC): An electric current that flows continuously and uniformly in one single direction through an electrical circuit.
• Direct Rule: A colonial administrative framework where the occupying European power replaces local indigenous authorities with foreign officials.
• Direct Speech: The exact words spoken by a person, enclosed tightly within quotation marks during writing.
• Displacement: A vector quantity indicating the straight-line distance and direction from an object's initial reference position to its final position.
• Divergent Boundary: A tectonic boundary where two crustal plates are moving away from each other, creating new crust as magma rises.
• Division: The mathematical operation of splitting a number into equal parts or groups to find the quotient.
• DNA (Deoxyribonucleic Acid): The double-helix molecule found inside cell nuclei that carries the primary genetic codes and instructions for living organisms.
• Domain: The complete set of all possible independent input values for which a mathematical function is validly defined.
• Dominant Allele: A genetic variant that is physically expressed in an organism's phenotype even if only one copy is inherited.
• Draught Animal: A domesticated livestock animal, such as an ox or donkey, bred and trained to perform heavy physical labor like plowing fields.
• Drip Irrigation: A water-conserving irrigation system that delivers slow, precise drops of water directly to the root zone of crops through plastic pipes.
• Drought: A long period of time with little to no rainfall, leading to water shortages, crop failures, and livestock deaths.
• Drought-Resistant Crop: A hardy crop variety capable of enduring prolonged periods of low soil moisture and erratic rainfall while still producing a harvest.
• Duties of a Citizen: Constitutional responsibilities like paying taxes, voting, obeying local laws, and protecting national property.
• Dynasty: A succession of hereditary rulers belonging to the exact same family line who maintain political power across multiple generations.


Section E

• E-Commerce: The buying, selling, trading, or marketing of physical goods and digital services over internet platforms and applications.
• E-Learning: An educational framework utilizing electronic media, internet platforms, digital devices, and software apps to deliver instructional lessons remotely.
• Egestion: The biological process of discharging undigested, unabsorbed food residues and waste materials from the digestive tract of an animal.
• Efficiency: A dimensionless ratio measuring the useful energy output of a machine relative to the total energy input, expressed as a percentage.
• Efflorescence: The loss of water of crystallization from a hydrated crystalline salt when exposed to dry air, leaving a powdery residue on the surface.
• El Niño: A climate pattern characterized by abnormal warming of surface waters in the eastern Pacific Ocean that frequently triggers severe droughts in Southern Africa.
• Elder: A senior member of a community respected for their wisdom, historical knowledge, and role in passing down oral traditions.
• Electrical Resistance: The structural property of a component or material that opposes the free flow of electric current.
• Electrolysis: A chemical process that uses direct electrical current to drive a non-spontaneous oxidation-reduction reaction and decompose an electrolyte solution.
• Electrolyte: A liquid substance or molten salt compound that contains free-moving ions and conducts electricity through chemical migration.
• Electromagnetic Spectrum: The complete range of all types of electromagnetic radiation arranged according to wavelength or frequency.
• Element: A pure chemical substance consisting entirely of one single type of atom that cannot be broken down simpler.
• Empirical Formula: A chemical formula showing the simplest whole-number ratio of the atoms of each element present in a compound.
