• Molarity: A unit of concentration defined as the number of moles of a specific solute dissolved per liter of liquid solution.
• Modem: A hardware device that converts digital data from a computer into analog signals for transmission over telephone lines, and vice versa.
• Mode: The specific value or category that appears with the highest frequency within a data set.
• Molecule: A group of two or more chemically bonded atoms forming the smallest fundamental unit of a compound.
• Mole: The SI unit for amount of substance, containing exactly 6.022 × 10²³ elementary entities.
• Molecular Formula: A chemical formula that states the exact number of atoms of each element present in a single molecule of a compound.
• Moment of a Force: A quantitative measure of the turning or rotational effect of a force applied around a fixed pivot point.
• Monitor: The electronic display screen that shows visual text, images, and videos generated by a computer.
• Mouse: A hand-held pointing device used to move the cursor and click on items on a computer screen.
• Muchongoyo: A traditional warrior dance performed with drums and sticks to build stamina, demonstrate military prowess, and celebrate victories.
• Mulching: The practice of covering topsoil with organic material like dried grass, leaves, or crop residues to suppress weeds and retain moisture.
• Multiple: The product result of multiplying a given whole number by any other whole number.
• Multiplication: The shorthand operation of repeated addition, scaling one number by another to find the product.
• Municipality: An urban local government council tasked with delivering clean water, waste disposal, and housing in towns and cities.
• Mutation: A permanent, structural alteration in the nucleotide sequence of the genomic DNA of an organism that can introduce new traits.

Section N

• National Flag: The supreme civic banner containing seven horizontal stripes and a white triangle housing the Zimbabwe Bird and a red star.
• Nationalism: An ideology and political movement centered on the shared identity, culture, and interests of a specific group of people, driving their struggle for sovereignty.
• Natural Region I: High-altitude, cool areas in Zimbabwe receiving very high rainfall, suited for forestry, tea, coffee, and fruit farming.
• Natural Region V: Hot, low-lying areas receiving low, erratic rainfall, suited only for cattle ranching and wild game management.
• Neocolonialism: The geopolitical practice of using economic globalization, corporate investments, and political pressure to control or influence a developing nation instead of direct military occupation.
• Nephron: The microscopic, functional filtration unit located within the kidney cortex and medulla that filters blood, reabsorbs essential nutrients, and generates urine.
• Neutralization: A chemical reaction between an acid and a base that produces a neutral salt and water.
• Newton: The standard SI unit used to measure physical force, named after scientist Isaac Newton.
• Ngano / Inganekwane: Traditional fireside folktales told orally by elders to children to pass down historical morals, usually featuring talking animals.
• Ngoma: A traditional drum made from a hollowed wooden log covered with a taut animal skin, forming the rhythmic base of regional music.
• Nhetembo / Izimbongi: Cultural poetry and clan praise poetry spoken aloud to honor family lineages, heroic leaders, or national identity.
• Non-biodegradable: Tough synthetic matter like plastic bottles, glass, and aluminum tins that cannot rot or break down naturally over time.
• Non-Ruminant: An animal with a simple, single-compartment stomach system (such as pigs and poultry) requiring concentrated feeds.
• Normal Distribution: A continuous, symmetrical bell-shaped probability distribution centered around its mean.
• Noun: A fundamental part of speech that names a specific person, place, physical object, or abstract idea.
• Noun Classes: The grammatical grouping system in Bantu languages that categorizes nouns based on their prefixes.
• Nuclear Family: A family unit consisting strictly of two parents and their immediate biological or adopted children.
• Nucleus: The master control center of a cell housing genetic blueprints and coordinating activities like growth.
• Numerator: The top number in a fraction that indicates how many equal parts of a whole are being considered.
• Nursery Bed: A small, carefully prepared, sheltered patch of soil used to raise delicate seeds until they are strong enough to be transplanted.

Section O

• Observation Method: Collecting research information carefully by watching and recording events without actively modifying the situation.
• Obtuse Angle: Any geometric angle that measures strictly greater than 90 degrees but less than 180 degrees.
• Ohm's Law: A principle stating that the electric current passing through a metallic conductor is directly proportional to the potential difference across it, provided temperature remains constant.
• Operating System (OS): The core system software that manages a computer's hardware resources, memory allocation, storage files, and provides common services for apps.
• Oral Rehydration Solution (ORS): A simple life-saving mixture of clean water, salt, and sugar given to patients to restore lost body fluids from dehydration.
• Oral Tradition: The systematic preservation and transmission of historical narratives, cultural values, genealogies, folklore, and legal codes across generations through spoken word.
• Organic Chemistry: The scientific study of the structure, properties, composition, reactions, and synthesis of carbon-containing chemical compounds.
• Organelle: A specialized microscopic structure located inside a cell that performs a specific functional task.
• Osmoregulation: The physiological process by which an organism maintains a constant water balance and osmotic pressure within its body fluids.
• Osmosis: The net movement of water molecules from a region of higher water potential to a region of lower water potential across a semi-permeable membrane.
• Overgrazing: Keeping too many livestock animals on a single piece of grazing land for too long, destroying the grass cover and causing erosion.
• Oxbow Lake: A crescent-shaped pool formed when a looping river meander is cut off from the main flowing river channel.
• Oxidation: A chemical process involving the loss of electrons, an increase in oxidation state, or the gain of oxygen.
• Oxygen: A vital gaseous chemical element required by most living organisms to execute aerobic cellular respiration.

Section P

• Parabola: A U-shaped symmetrical curve generated by a quadratic function or the intersection of a cone with a plane parallel to its side.
• Parallel Lines: Two or more straight lines running side-by-side that always stay the same distance apart and never cross.
• Pasture: A managed field or grassland area set aside specifically for livestock animals to graze on.
• Paternalism: An administrative style where an occupying or governing authority manages a population in a manner that limits their freedoms, justifying it as acting in their best interest.
• Pathogen: A scientific term for any microscopic organism (such as bacteria or a virus) capable of causing disease.
• Pathogen Transmission: The mechanisms through which harmful microbes spread from an infected person or animal to a healthy individual.
• Patrilineal System: A societal system where lineage, family names, inheritance, and ancestral rights are passed down exclusively through the father's side.
• Pedagogy: The formal method, practice, and science of teaching, particularly as an academic subject or theoretical study.
• Percentage: A ratio or fractional value expressed out of a fixed base of 100, symbolized by the % sign.
• Perimeter: The total distance or continuous path length around the outside edge of a two-dimensional shape.
• Periodicity: The repeating pattern of physical and chemical properties of elements across the periodic table when arranged by increasing atomic number.
• Peristalsis: Rhythmic, wave-like muscular contractions and relaxations of the muscles in the walls of the alimentary canal that push food downward.
• Permutation: A mathematical arrangement of a set of items or values where the specific sequence or order is important.
• Perpendicular Lines: Lines that cross or meet at a perfect 90-degree right angle.
• Personal Hygiene: Regular actions like bathing, washing hands with soap, and brushing teeth to maintain health and prevent illness.
• Pesticide: A chemical substance or biological agent used to deter, incapacitate, kill, or manage organisms deemed harmful to crops or livestock.
• Pfumvudza / Intwasa: A high-yield, climate-smart conservation agriculture system that relies on precise planting holes, heavy mulching, and targeted fertilization.
• Phenotype: The observable physical traits, biochemical properties, or behavioral characteristics of an organism, determined by both its genotype and environmental influences.
• Phishing: A cybercrime deceptive practice where targets are contacted by email or message by someone posing as a legitimate institution to lure them into providing sensitive data.
• Phloem: A specialized vascular tissue in plants responsible for transporting soluble organic nutrients, primarily sucrose, from the leaves to growth and storage organs.
• Photosynthesis: The biochemical process through which green plants utilize chlorophyll to convert carbon dioxide, water, and radiant sunlight into glucose and oxygen.
• Phylum: A primary rank of biological classification that sits directly below a Kingdom and above a Class.
• Pictogram: A data visualization chart that employs simple pictures or symbolic icons to represent quantities of items collected.
• Plagiarism: The act of copying another student's work or taking a resource directly from the internet without giving credit.
• Plasmolysis: The shrinking of the cytoplasm away from the rigid cell wall of a plant cell when placed in a hypertonic solution due to water loss via osmosis.
• Plateau: A large, flat area of land that sits elevated high above sea level.
• Poaching: The illegal hunting, trapping, or killing of protected wild animals in game reserves or national parks.
• Pollution: The introduction of harmful materials, plastics, or poisonous chemical emissions into air, soil, or natural water systems.
• Polygon: A closed, flat, two-dimensional geometric shape bounded completely by three or more straight lines.
• Polymerization: A chemical process where small repeating molecular units called monomers react together to form a long, complex chain molecule.
• Potential Difference: The work done per unit charge in moving a positive electrical charge between two specific points in an electric circuit.
• Potential Energy: The stored energy an object possesses due to its relative structural position, stress, or configuration within a field.
• Pottery: The traditional ceramic craft of shaping wet clay into vessels or functional containers, which are then hardened at high temperatures.
• Poultry Run: A secure, enclosed wire or brick shelter built to protect chickens from bad weather and predators.
• Power: A scalar quantity measuring the rate at which work is performed or energy is converted or transferred over time, measured in Watts.
• Precipitate: An insoluble solid substance that separates from a liquid solution during a chemical displacement or double-decomposition reaction.
• Precipitation: Any form of water falling from the sky to Earth's surface, including rain, hail, or snow.
• Prefix: A letter or group of letters fixed firmly to the beginning of a root word to alter its meaning.
• Preposition: A word showing the spatial, temporal, or logical relationship of a noun to other words in a sentence.
• Pressure: The continuous force exerted perpendicular to a surface per unit area over which that force is distributed, measured in Pascals.
• Prime Number: A whole number greater than 1 that cannot be divided evenly by any number except 1 and itself.
• Probability: A mathematical measure ranging from 0 to 1 that quantifies the likelihood of a specific event occurring.
• Problem Statement: A concise sentence written by a student highlighting an issue requiring a research solution.
• Profit: The financial gain made when the money earned from selling an item is higher than the original cost to make or buy it.
• Pronoun: A word used to replace a noun to prevent repetitive phrasing in a sentence.
• Protectorate: An autonomous territory that is formally protected and politically directed by a more powerful sovereign state in exchange for control over foreign policy.
• Protocol: A standardized set of digital rules that govern how data is formatted, transmitted, received, and processed across a network link.
• Proverb: A wise, brief traditional saying that conveys a deep life lesson or moral truth using figurative language.
• Pruning: The horticultural cutting away of selective dead, overgrown, or diseased plant branches to improve air circulation and encourage fruit growth.
• Punctuation: A system of standard marks used to separate sentences and clarify written text.
• Pythagoras' Theorem: A geometric theorem stating that in any right triangle, the square of the hypotenuse equals the sum of the squares of the other two sides (a² + b² = c²).

Section Q

• Quadratic Equation: A polynomial equation of the second degree, written in the standard form ax² + bx + c = 0.
• Quadrilateral: Any flat, two-dimensional geometric polygon that possesses exactly four straight sides and four vertices.
• Qualitative Data: Research information expressed in descriptive words or categories rather than numerical measurements.
• Quantitative Data: Research findings expressed as numbers, counts, or statistics that can be measured mathematically.
• Quotient: The mathematical result or answer obtained by dividing one number by another.

Section R

• Radiation: The emission and propagation of energy through space or a material medium in the form of electromagnetic waves or subatomic particles.
• Radical: An atom, molecule, or ion that possesses an unpaired valence electron, making it highly reactive.
• Rain Gauge: A marked cylinder tool used to collect and measure the amount of rain that falls over a specific time.
• RAM: Volatile primary computer memory that provides temporary, high-speed storage for data and programs currently being processed by the CPU.
• Range: The complete set of all possible dependent output values that a function can produce from its valid domain inputs.
• Ratio: A mathematical comparison of two numeric values showing the relative size of one value to another.
• Recommendations: Actionable steps proposed by a student at the conclusion of their research to solve the stated problem.
• Red star: Located on the national flag, it represents internationalism, bright future prospects, and state development values.
• Reduction: A chemical process involving the gain of electrons, a decrease in oxidation state, or the loss of oxygen.
• Referee: The official on-field judge tasked with enforcing game rules fairly and ensuring player safety.
• Reflection: The abrupt rebounding of a wave train, light ray, or sound wave off a boundary surface between two different media back into its original medium.
• Reforestation: The deliberate replanting of trees in forest areas that have been depleted due to logging, clearing, or wildfires.
• Refraction: The bending or change in direction of a wave ray when it passes obliquely from one medium to another of different optical density.
• Refraction Index: A mathematical ratio measuring how much a ray of light bends when entering a specific medium.
• Register: The choice of vocabulary, structure, and tone a speaker uses based on their social relationship with the listener.
• Renewable Energy: Clean power harvested from endless natural sources like sunlight, wind, or moving water.
• Resistance: A measure of the opposition a component or material system presents to the free flow of an electric current through it.
• Resuscitation (CPR): An emergency first-aid procedure combining chest compressions and rescue breaths to help a person whose heart has stopped.
• Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG): An AI system architecture that searches an external database for facts before generating a response to ensure accurate answers.
• Reversible Reaction: A chemical reaction where the conversion of reactants into products occurs simultaneously with the conversion of products back into reactants.
• Rhythm: A strong, regular, repeated pattern of sounds, beats, or movements that forms the structural backbone of music and dance.
• Riddle: A puzzling question or statement phrased as a word game to test a child's critical thinking and vocabulary.
• Rift Valley: A steep-sided valley formed by the downward sliding of land blocks between parallel faults.
• Right Angle: An exact 90-degree angle formed when two perpendicular lines intersect perfectly.
• ROM: Non-volatile primary computer memory that contains permanent, unchangeable boot instructions needed to turn on a device.
• Rote Learning: A learning technique focusing on memorizing facts through pure repetition rather than building deep understanding.
• Router: A network hardware device that forwards data packets between separate computer networks, directing internet traffic.
• Row Planting: Sowing seeds in straight, measured lines to make weeding, fertilizing, and harvesting easier.
• Rudd Concession: A written agreement signed in 1888 between King Lobengula and agents of Cecil Rhodes, granting exclusive mining privileges over all minerals.
• Ruminant: An herbivorous mammal with a specialized four-compartment stomach that allows it to digest tough plant matter.
• Rural-Urban Migration: The movement of people away from rural villages to cities in search of employment and social amenities.

Section S

• Sadza: The staple carbohydrate food of Zimbabwe, made of thick boiled maize-meal or grain porridge.
• San: The indigenous hunter-gatherer people of Southern Africa, celebrated for their historical rock art across rock shelters.
• Sanitation: The hygienic maintenance of clean living spaces through safe disposal of human waste and trash, preventing disease spread.
• Saturated Compound: An organic chemical compound whose carbon atoms are linked entirely by single covalent bonds.
• Saturated Solution: A chemical solution that contains the maximum possible amount of dissolved solute at a specific temperature.
• Savannah: A wide, open grassland ecosystem dotted with scattered trees, covering most of Zimbabwe’s landscape.
• Scaffolding: Temporary instructional support structures provided by teachers to help students master complex skills step-by-step.
• Scalar: A physical quantity that is completely defined by its numerical magnitude or size alone, possessing no direction.
• School-Based Project (SBP): A hands-on, community-focused research assignment where students solve a local real-world problem using local resources.
• Schistosomiasis (Bilharzia): A chronic parasitic disease caused by tiny flatworms that breed inside freshwater snails, entering humans through skin contact with contaminated water.
• Science: The systematic study of the physical and natural world through structured observation, experimentation, and profiling.
• Scramble for Africa: The rapid, competitive invasion, annexation, division, and colonization of African territories by European empires between 1881 and 1914.
• Sculpture: The three-dimensional art form of shaping, carving, or modeling figures, animals, or abstract forms out of stone, wood, or metal.
• Search Engine: An online software tool that sifts through internet databases to find relevant web pages when a user types in research keywords.
• Second Chimurenga: The liberation war (1960s–1979) fought by nationalist forces to overturn colonial minority rule and achieve independence.
• Sector: A wedge-shaped portion of a circle bounded by two radii and an outer connecting arc.
• Sediment: Mineral or organic matter deposited by water, wind, or ice that accumulates at the base of land or water bodies.
• Sekuru Kaguvi: A prominent spiritual medium who alongside Nehanda led the resistance in central regions during the First Chimurenga.
• Shona: One of the principal indigenous languages and cultural groups spoken widely across Zimbabwe.
• Silage: High-moisture animal fodder made from green forage crops packed airtight in a pit to undergo anaerobic fermentation for winter feed.
• Siltation: The accumulation of fine sand, clay, or soil sediments at the bottom of rivers, dams, and lakes, reducing their water storage capacity.
• Simile: A literary comparison device used to show similarity between two distinct things using the connecting words like or as.
• Simultaneous Equations: A collection of two or more equations sharing common unknown variables that must be satisfied by the same set of values.
• Socialization: The lifelong process through which individuals learn the values, language, behaviors, and social expectations of their society.
• Software: A collection of digital instructions, data programs, operating scripts, and applications that tell computer hardware exactly how to perform tasks.
• Soil Erosion: The wearing away and displacement of the fertile topsoil layer by natural forces like wind or fast-flowing rainwater.
• Solar Energy: Electrical or thermal energy generated from sunlight using photovoltaic panels.
• Solute: The solid, liquid, or gaseous component that is dissolved into a solvent to create a homogeneous solution.
• Sovereignty: The supreme, independent authority and power of a nation-state to govern itself and establish its own laws without external interference.
• Specific Heat Capacity: The amount of thermal energy required to raise the temperature of exactly one kilogram of a substance by one degree Celsius.
• Sprain: A painful joint injury that occurs when supportive ligaments are stretched beyond their normal limits or torn during physical activity.
• Spreadsheet: An interactive grid-based application software used to organize, calculate, analyze, and graph structured numeric data in rows and columns.
• Squatter Settlement: An informal residential area built from scrap materials on land occupied illegally without municipal permits.
• Standard Deviation: A statistical measure that quantifies the amount of variation, dispersion, or spread of a set of data values around their mean.
• Star Topology: A network layout where all individual computer workstations are linked directly to a central hub, switch, or router.
• Stem Subjects: An educational acronym grouping together the fields of Science, Technology, Engineering, and Mathematics.
• Sterilization: The process of killing all living microorganisms on an object using extreme heat, boiling water, or strong chemicals.
• Storage Device: Hardware items like USB flash drives or hard disks used to save files and data so they aren't lost when a device power cuts.
• Subsistence Farming: A small-scale farming model where families grow food crops primarily to satisfy their own household consumption needs.
• Subtraction: The operation of taking one number away from another to find the difference between them.
• Suffix: A structural language element added to the tail end of a root word to determine tense or plurality.
• Summative Assessment: Final evaluations like end-of-term exams or national ZIMSEC tests used to score a student's total academic achievement.
• Suzerainty: A political situation where a dominant state exercises control over the foreign policy of a tributary state while permitting it internal autonomy.
• Symmetry: A geometric property where a shape can be divided by a line into two matching halves that are mirror images of each other.
• Synapse: The microscopic gap or junction across which electrical nerve impulses are transmitted chemically via neurotransmitters from one neuron to another.
• Synonym: A word that has the exact same or an exceptionally close meaning to another word within the same language.
• System Software: Programs designed to run a computer's hardware and application programs directly, acting as an intermediary layer.

Section T

• Taboo: Social or religious customs that strictly prohibit certain actions, words, or foods, often used to preserve morals or protect environment assets.
• Tally Chart: A data collection method using groups of four vertical marks crossed by a fifth diagonal stroke to count observations quickly.
• Target Population: The specific group of people, animals, or objects being studied in a research project.
• Tempo: The absolute speed, pace, or rate at which a musical piece is played, sung, or executed during a routine.
• Tense: The grammatical form of a verb that indicates whether an action happened in the past, is happening now, or will happen in the future.
• Terminal Velocity: The constant, maximum speed achieved by a falling object when the downward force of gravity matches the upward resistive force of air drag.
• Thermometer: A medical or meteorological tool used to measure temperatures in degrees Celsius.
• Thinning: Removing excess weak seedlings from a planting hole to allow the strongest remaining plants enough space to grow properly.
• Tick-Borne Diseases: Deadly livestock illnesses, such as January Disease, transmitted through the bites of infected ticks.
• Total Internal Reflection: An optical phenomenon that occurs when a light ray traveling in a denser medium hits a boundary at an angle greater than the critical angle, reflecting entirely back into the denser medium.
• Totem: An animal or natural element representing a specific ancestral lineage, researched for its role in preventing incest and building identity.
• Tourniquet: A tightly bound band or device wrapped around a limb to stop severe, life-threatening arterial bleeding in an emergency.
• Transformer: A device used to increase or decrease the voltage of an alternating electrical current.
• Transplanting: Moving a young, delicate seedling from a protected nursery bed into its permanent growing spot in a field or garden.
• Treaty of Versailles: The peace treaty signed in 1919 that formally ended World War I, imposing harsh financial reparations on Germany.
• Tributary: A smaller river or freshwater stream that flows into and joins a larger main river.
• Trigonometry: A branch of mathematics exploring the specific geometric relationships between the side lengths and angles of triangles.
• Triumvirate: A political regime or administrative alliance dominated or ruled equally by three powerful leaders.
• Trophic Level: The specific position or feeding stage that a group of organisms occupies within an ecological food chain or pyramid.
• Turgor Pressure: The hydrostatic pressure exerted by the fluid contents of a plant cell against its rigid cell wall, providing structural support.
• Typhoid: A life-threatening waterborne bacterial infection causing severe fever and diarrhea, spread through water contaminated with sewage.

Section U

• Unhu / Vumunhu: The foundational socio-philosophical ethical code of Zimbabwe, dictating communal responsibility, mutual respect, integrity, and good moral character.
• Unity Day: Celebrated on December 22nd to mark the signing of the 1987 Unity Accord, which united major political liberation movements.
• Universal Suffrage: The constitutional right of all adult citizens to vote in democratic elections regardless of race, gender, or wealth.
• Urbanization: The continuous migration of human populations from rural locations to live in towns and cities.
• URL: A web address that specifies the exact location of a digital resource on the internet.

Section V

• Vaccination: Giving livestock or humans medicine via injections or oral routes to build biological immunity against deadly diseases.
• Valency: A numerical value representing the combining power of an element based on the outer electrons it can lose, gain, or share.
• Valley: A low-lying stretch of land nestled safely between hills or mountain ridges, often carved out by a river over time.
• Vasoconstriction: The narrowing of the lumen of subcutaneous blood vessels near the skin surface, reducing blood flow to minimize thermal energy loss.
• Vasodilation: The widening of subcutaneous blood vessels near the skin surface, increasing blood flow to maximize heat loss into the environment.
• Vassal State: A country or kingdom that is subordinate to a more powerful neighboring empire, paying financial tribute in exchange for security.
• Vector: A physical or mathematical quantity that requires both a defined numerical magnitude and a specific geometric direction to be fully described.
• Vector (Biology): A living organism—most commonly an insect—that carries and transmits infectious pathogens to hosts.
• Vein: A thin-walled blood vessel equipped with internal valves that carries deoxygenated blood back toward the heart.
• Velocity: A vector quantity that measures the exact displacement of an object per unit of time in a specified direction.
• Verb: A core action or doing word that expresses what a subject is performing, experiencing, or being.
• Virus: A submicroscopic infectious agent that replicates only inside the living cells of an organism, causing disease.
• Virus (Digital): A malicious program written to infect a computer, delete files, steal data, or slow down system performance.
• Visual Arts: A broad class of art forms focused on creating works that are primarily visual in nature, such as painting, drawing, and sculpting.
• Volume: The total amount of three-dimensional space occupied by a solid object or fluid container, measured in cubic units.

Section W

• WAN: A telecommunications network that extends over a large geographical distance, interconnecting smaller network nodes across cities or countries.
• Warm-Up: A series of light exercises and stretches performed right before intense physical activity to increase blood flow and prevent injury.
• Water Cycle: The continuous movement of water from Earth's surface into the sky through evaporation, turning into clouds, and returning as rain.
• Water Scarcity: The critical lack of sufficient clean water resources to meet household, agricultural, and industrial needs in a community.
• Water Table: The upper underground level where the soil and rocks are completely soaked with water.
• Waterborne Diseases: Highly contagious infections, such as Cholera and Typhoid, contracted by consuming water contaminated by untreated sewage.
• Wavelength: The spatial distance between two successive corresponding points on a periodic wave, such as from crest to crest.
• Weathering: The mechanical breaking down or chemical decomposition of rocks exposed at Earth's surface by weather elements.
• Weeding: The physical, chemical, or mechanical removal of unwanted plants that compete with target crops for nutrients, sunlight, and water.
• Weaving: A craft technique where long structural strands of reeds, grass, or fibers are interlaced at right angles to manufacture baskets or mats.
• Weight: The gravitational force exerted on an object's mass by a massive body like the Earth.
• White Triangle: Located on the national flag, it represents peace, national stability, and progress moving forward after achieving independence.
• Whole Numbers: A counting number or zero that contains no fractional or decimal parts.
• Wind Vane: A spinning arrow tool placed high up to show the directional origin of blowing wind.
• Word Processor: An application software used for the typing, editing, formatting, structuring, styling, and printing of text-based documents.
• Work Done: The scalar energy transferred to an object when a force applied parallel to displacement moves that object over a distance.

Section X

• Xanthophyll: A yellow accessory pigment found in plant thylakoids that aids in light-harvesting during photosynthesis.
• Xenolith: A rock fragment trapped inside an igneous rock body that is foreign to the igneous mass in which it occurs.
• Xerophyte: A species of plant that has evolved special structural adaptations to survive in extremely dry environments, such as cacti.
• Xylem: A vascular tissue in plants consisting of dead, lignified cells that transports water and dissolved mineral ions upward from the roots to the leaves.

Section Y

• Yeast: A single-celled eukaryotic fungus variety used heavily in educational science experiments to study anaerobic fermentation and carbon dioxide production.
• Yellow stripe: Located on the national flag, it represents the vast mineral wealth (such as gold, chrome, and platinum) contained within the soil.
• Yield: The total volume or weight of agricultural crop harvested per unit of field land area.

Section Z

• ZANLA: The military wing of the ZANU nationalist movement that operated primarily out of Mozambique during the liberation struggle.
• Zero Tillage: A farming method where the soil is left undisturbed by plowing or tilling from harvest to planting, preserving organic structure.
• ZIPRA: The military wing of the ZAPU nationalist movement that operated primarily out of Zambia, utilizing regular and guerrilla strategies.
• Ziwa National Monument: An archaeological site in Nyanga featuring vast ancient agricultural terraces, stone-walled structures, and iron-smelting furnaces.
• Zunde raMambo: A traditional social security framework where a community collectively farms a field dedicated to producing grain reserves for vulnerable members.
• Zygote: The single diploid cell formed by the fusion of two haploid gametes during fertilization, representing the initial stage of a new organism.



Section A

• Abutment: [Building Technology] A solid masonry structure or wall designed to support, anchor, or receive the lateral thrust of an arch, bridge, or retaining wall.
• Acceptance: [Commerce] A formal, binding declaration by the drawee of a bill of exchange confirming their commitment to pay the specified amount at maturity.
• Accommodation Bill: [Commerce / Principles of Accounts] A bill of exchange drawn, accepted, or endorsed without financial consideration, intended purely to act as financial backing to raise money on loan.
• Accounting Equation: [Principles of Accounts] The foundational rule of double-entry bookkeeping stating that total assets must always equal total liabilities plus equity.
• Accrual Concept: [Principles of Accounts] An accounting convention requiring revenues and expenses to be recorded in the specific financial period they occur, regardless of when cash changes hands.
• Acid-Test Ratio: [Principles of Accounts / Commerce] A strict liquidity metric calculated by dividing liquid assets (current assets minus closing inventory) by current liabilities.
• Ancestral Shrines: [Family & Religious Studies] Dedicated geographic locations or physical configurations, such as trees (Chinyakare) or structural platforms, used to commune with familial ancestors (Vadzimu / Amadlozi).
• Aggregate: [Building Technology] Granular materials like sand, gravel, crushed stone, or slag mixed with cement and water to form structural concrete or mortar.
• African Traditional Religion (ATR): [Family & Religious Studies] The indigenous, holistic spiritual belief systems of the African people, centered on a supreme creator god (Mwari / Unkulunkulu), spirit mediums, and ancestors.
• Arch: [Building Technology] A curved, structural masonry arrangement designed to span an open space, supporting its own weight and vertical loads entirely through compression.
• Articles of Association: [Commerce] A statutory legal document outlining the internal regulations, operational rules, voting procedures, and administrative powers of a joint-stock company.
• Assets: [Principles of Accounts] Financial or physical resources owned or controlled by a business entity as a result of past transactions, expected to yield future economic benefits.
• Atonement: [Family & Religious Studies] Religious rituals or liturgical actions performed to reconcile, repair, or purify broken relationships between humans, ancestors, or God.

Section B

• Bad Debts: [Principles of Accounts] The specific portion of trade receivables or customer debts that is deemed entirely uncollectible and written off as a business expense.
• Bank Reconciliation Statement: [Principles of Accounts / Commerce] A financial report compiled to match, adjust, and reconcile differences between the cash book balance and the official bank statement balance.
• Bill of Lading: [Commerce] A binding maritime transport contract and document of title issued by a carrier detailing cargo specifications, destination, and ownership rights.
• Bond: [Building Technology] The deliberate, systematic arrangement of bricks, blocks, or stones in walls to ensure structural stability, distribution of weight, and aesthetic appeal.
• Brick Gauge: [Building Technology] A standardized vertical measurement tool or rule used by masons to ensure consecutive brick courses are laid at identical heights.
• Broker: [Commerce] An independent mercantile agent or intermediary who negotiates commercial transactions, sales, or contracts on behalf of clients in exchange for commission.
• Building Line: [Building Technology] A legally enforced structural boundary line fixed by local planning authorities, behind which all construction must take place.
• Business Entity Concept: [Principles of Accounts] A core accounting convention stating that a business enterprise must be treated as a legal, financial identity separate from its owners.

Section C

• Capital: [Principles of Accounts / Commerce] The total financial resources, machinery, buildings, or net assets contributed by the owners or investors to establish and run an enterprise.
• Capital Expenditure: [Principles of Accounts] Financial outlays made to acquire, improve, or extend the useful life of non-current fixed assets, recorded on the balance sheet.
• Cartage: [Commerce / Principles of Accounts] The financial charge or shipping expense paid for the terrestrial hauling and short-distance delivery of commercial goods.
• Cash Discount: [Principles of Accounts / Commerce] A financial reduction allowed on the invoice price of goods, granted to encourage prompt payment within a specified period.
• Cavity Wall: [Building Technology] A specialized exterior wall configuration consisting of two parallel leaves of masonry separated by a continuous air space, designed to prevent dampness and provide thermal insulation.
• Cement: [Building Technology] A hydraulic binding material composed of calcined limestone and clay, which sets and hardens when mixed with water.
• Certificate of Incorporation: [Commerce] A supreme legal license issued by the Registrar of Companies officially certifying that a business enterprise is duly registered as a corporate body.
• Charterparty: [Commerce] A specialized maritime contract where a shipowner leases an entire vessel, or a substantial portion of it, to a merchant trader for a specific voyage or duration.
• Covenant: [Family & Religious Studies] A solemn, legally or spiritually binding agreement established between two parties, most notably between humanity and a deity in biblical and traditional narratives.
• Closing Inventory: [Principles of Accounts] The calculated total monetary value of unsold commercial goods or raw materials held by an enterprise at the conclusion of a financial period.
• Commandments: [Family & Religious Studies] Divine statutory laws or moral codes issued by a deity to govern human behavior, ethical conduct, and religious obligations (e.g., the Decalogue).
• Commerce: [Commerce] The vast macro-discipline studying all activities involved in the distribution, exchange, marketing, financing, and movement of goods from producers to final consumers.
• Concrete: [Building Technology] A structural composite material manufactured by mixing specific ratios of cement, fine aggregate, coarse aggregate, and water.
• Consignee: [Commerce] The specific person, merchant house, or corporate entity to whom commercial goods are shipped and delivered.
• Copestone: [Building Technology] The top, protective stone or brick course laid along the crest of a wall or parapet, designed to shed rainwater and protect masonry below.
• Course: [Building Technology] A single, continuous horizontal row of bricks, blocks, or stones laid uniformly within a masonry structure.
• Credit Note: [Commerce / Principles of Accounts] A commercial source document issued by a seller to a buyer to reduce the buyer's outstanding account balance, typically due to returned or damaged goods.
• Current Assets: [Principles of Accounts] Cash, bank balances, or short-term resources expected to be converted into cash, sold, or consumed within twelve months.
• Current Liabilities: [Principles of Accounts] Short-term financial obligations or debts that an enterprise must settle within twelve months using current assets.

Section D

• Damp-Proof Course (DPC): [Building Technology] A horizontal layer of impervious material, such as polythene sheet or asphalt, inserted into a wall above ground level to prevent capillary moisture from rising.
• Debenture: [Commerce / Principles of Accounts] A long-term debt instrument or bond issued by a limited company to raise capital, backed by a fixed or floating charge over assets.
• Debit Note: [Commerce / Principles of Accounts] A commercial document sent by a buyer to a seller, or vice versa, formally requesting a financial adjustment or indicating an undercharge.
• Divination: [Family & Religious Studies] The spiritual practice of seeking hidden knowledge, future events, or the will of ancestors and spirits through specialized rituals (e.g., Hakata / Chikuwo).
• Double-Entry Bookkeeping: [Principles of Accounts] The accounting system where every economic transaction is recorded with at least one debit entry and a corresponding credit entry.
• Drawings: [Principles of Accounts] Cash, inventory, or assets taken directly out of a business by the owner for personal use, reducing owner's equity.

Section E

• Endorsement: [Commerce] The act of signing the back of a negotiable instrument, such as a check or bill of exchange, to legally transfer ownership rights to another party.
• Entrepot Trade: [Commerce] A specialized international trade model where goods are imported from one country and subsequently re-exported to another country without being altered or taxed internally.
• Errors of Commission: [Principles of Accounts] A bookkeeping error where a transaction is entered into the correct type of account but under the wrong individual or entity name.
• Errors of Omission: [Principles of Accounts] A fundamental accounting error occurring when a complete transaction is entirely left out of the books of original entry.
• Errors of Principle: [Principles of Accounts] A serious bookkeeping error where a transaction is entered into an entirely wrong class of account, violating accounting principles (e.g., treating capital expenditure as revenue expenditure).
• Eschatology: [Family & Religious Studies] The specific branch of theology and religious studies dealing with ultimate human destiny, death, the end times, and final judgment.
• Ethical Code: [Family & Religious Studies] A structured system of moral principles, values, and behavioral expectations derived from religious doctrines or traditional frameworks (Unhu / Vumunhu).

Section F

• Family: [Family & Religious Studies] The foundational social, economic, and moral institution of human society, responsible for primary socialization, lineage preservation, and spiritual identity.
• Flemish Bond: [Building Technology] A bricklaying pattern consisting of alternating stretchers and headers laid uniformly within every single course.
• Foundation: [Building Technology] The structural base of a building excavated below ground level, designed to receive and safely distribute the total weight of the superstructure into the earth.
• Free On Board (FOB): [Commerce] A standard international shipping term indicating that the seller fulfills their obligation when the goods pass over the ship's rail at the designated port of shipment.

Section G

• Gable: [Building Technology] The triangular portion of an exterior wall enclosed between the sloping ridges of a dual-pitch roof.
• General Ledger: [Principles of Accounts] The master accounting record containing a comprehensive, adjusted collection of all financial accounts used to compile the trial balance.
• Going Concern Concept: [Principles of Accounts] An accounting convention operating under the assumption that a business enterprise will continue operating indefinitely into the foreseeable future.
• Gross Profit: [Principles of Accounts / Commerce] The financial surplus calculated by subtracting the cost of sales directly from net sales revenue.

Section H

• Header: [Building Technology] A brick or masonry unit laid flat with its short end or face exposed on the exterior surface of a wall.
• Honor: [Commerce] Restoring confidence in negotiable instruments by paying or accepting a bill of exchange fully when it becomes legally due.
• Hypothecation: [Commerce] A legal arrangement where property or assets are pledged as security for a loan without transferring physical possession or ownership title to the lender.

Section I

• Imprest System: [Principles of Accounts / Commerce] A systematic method of managing petty cash where a fixed cash float is provided, and regularly replenished by an amount exactly matching total spent receipts.
• Indenture: [Commerce / General Law] A formal, binding legal agreement, contract, or deed executed between two or more parties, historically used for apprenticeships.
• Initiation Rites: [Family & Religious Studies] Structured cultural or religious transition ceremonies marking a person's movement from childhood into full adulthood and tribal responsibility.
• Insulation: [Building Technology] Special construction materials or air spaces integrated into a building's floors, walls, or roofs to prevent heat loss or sound transmission.
• Intangible Assets: [Principles of Accounts] Non-physical, long-term business assets that hold identifiable economic value, such as goodwill, patents, copyrights, and trademarks.
• Insurable Interest: [Commerce] A foundational insurance principle requiring that the person taking out a policy must suffer a direct financial loss if the insured property or life is damaged.

Section J

• Joist: [Building Technology] Parallel horizontal timber, steel, or concrete beams used to support floors, ceilings, or flat roof decks.
• Joint-Stock Company: [Commerce] A large business enterprise owned collectively by shareholders, possessing a separate legal identity and limited liability.
• Journal Proper: [Principles of Accounts] A book of original entry used to record uncommon transactions that cannot fit into specialized books, such as opening entries, corrections, and asset purchases.

Section K

• Kurova Guva / Umbuyiso: [Family & Religious Studies] A traditional Zimbabwean post-burial ritual performed approximately a year after death to bring back the spirit of the deceased into the family fold as a protective ancestor.
• Keying: [Building Technology] The process of raking out mortar joints or roughening a masonry surface to provide a strong mechanical bond for plaster or render.

Section L

• Ledger Account: [Principles of Accounts] A T-shaped visual layout tracking the dynamic increases and decreases of a specific financial item using debit and credit columns.
• Lintel: [Building Technology] A horizontal structural beam constructed from timber, stone, concrete, or steel spanning the top of a door or window opening to support masonry above.
• Limited Liability: [Commerce] A legal condition where shareholders' financial losses in a company are strictly capped at the amount they invested in shares, protecting personal assets.
• Liturgical Rituals: [Family & Religious Studies] Structured, public religious practices or patterns of worship performed by a faith community according to established traditions.

Section M

• Mortar: [Building Technology] A workable paste manufactured by mixing cement or lime with sand and water, used to bind bricks together and seal joints.
• Matching Concept: [Principles of Accounts] An accounting convention dictating that expenses incurred must be matched directly against the revenues earned in the exact same financial period.
• Mercantile Agent: [Commerce] A commercial agent authorized in the customary course of business to buy, sell, consign, or raise loans on goods on behalf of a principal.
• Monotheism: [Family & Religious Studies] The doctrinal religious belief in the existence of only one supreme, omnipotent creator God.
• Mythology: [Family & Religious Studies] A collection of traditional stories or sacred narratives accounting for the origins of the cosmos, humanity, and early spiritual events.

Section N

• Net Profit: [Principles of Accounts / Commerce] The absolute financial surplus remaining after all operating expenses have been deducted from gross profit.
• Non-Current Assets: [Principles of Accounts] Long-term structural resources owned by an enterprise for continuous use over several years rather than for resale, such as machinery, vehicles, and buildings.
• Nosing: [Building Technology] The rounded, projecting edge of a stair tread extending over the riser below, designed to provide safety and extra stepping surface.

Section O

• Operating Expenses: [Principles of Accounts] The continuous operational costs incurred during the running of a business enterprise, such as rent, salaries, utilities, and advertising.
• Original Entry: [Principles of Accounts] The primary stage of bookkeeping where economic transactions are sorted and entered chronologically into specialized journals before being posted to ledgers.
• Overdraft: [Commerce / Principles of Accounts] A flexible bank facility allowing a current account holder to withdraw more money than their account actually holds, up to an agreed limit.

Section P

• Parable: [Family & Religious Studies] A concise, allegorical story used to illustrate a profound spiritual truth, moral lesson, or ethical value in religious teachings.
• Partnership: [Commerce / Principles of Accounts] A business structure formed by two to twenty individuals who agree to contribute capital, share profits, and bear joint liability.
• Parquet Flooring: [Building Technology] A decorative internal flooring surface composed of short timber blocks arranged in geometric, interlocking patterns (e.g., herringbone).
• Petty Cash Book: [Principles of Accounts] A specialized book of original entry tracking small, low-value cash outlays using columns and the imprest system.
• Partition Wall: [Building Technology] A thin, non-load-bearing interior wall designed to divide an internal building space into separate rooms.
• Plastering: [Building Technology] The decorative or protective process of applying layers of mortar paste onto interior wall surfaces to create a smooth, clean finish.
• Polygamy: [Family & Religious Studies / Social Sciences] A marriage structure where an individual has more than one spouse simultaneously, common in traditional systems (Barika).
• Premium: [Commerce] The regular financial payment made by an insured individual or entity to an insurance company to keep a policy active.
• Principles of Accounts: [Principles of Accounts] The systemic vocational science tracking, analyzing, reporting, and balancing financial data for economic enterprises.
• Prophetic Movements: [Family & Religious Studies] Historical or contemporary religious groups led by a charismatic prophet claiming direct divine revelation, driving spiritual and social change.
• Public Limited Company (PLC): [Commerce] A large incorporated business whose shares are listed on a public stock exchange, allowing capital to be raised from the general public.
• Purlin: [Building Technology] Horizontal structural beams spanning across roof trusses to support rafters or roof sheeting.

Section Q

• Quarry Tile: [Building Technology] A durable, unglazed flooring tile manufactured from baked clay, used in heavy-wear internal or external floors.
• Quotation: [Commerce] A commercial source document sent by a seller detailing the specific prices, descriptions, and terms for supplying requested goods.

Section R

• Rafter: [Building Technology] Sloping timber or steel structural beams extending from the ridge down to the wall plate, forming the primary framework of a pitched roof.
• Reconciliation: [Family & Religious Studies] The structural restoration of harmony, peace, and spiritual balance between broken relationships through ceremony, truth-telling, or apology.
• Retained Earnings: [Principles of Accounts] The cumulative net profits kept within a corporate enterprise after dividends have been distributed to shareholders.
• Revenue Expenditure: [Principles of Accounts] Financial outlays made for the daily operation of an enterprise or the direct maintenance of assets, charged as an expense in the income statement.
• Reinforced Concrete: [Building Technology] Concrete containing embedded steel bars or mesh to add tensile strength to its natural compressive strength.
• Riser: [Building Technology] The vertical face or board running between consecutive steps on a staircase.

Section S

• Scaffolding: [Building Technology] A temporary external framework of metal pipes or timber planks built to support workers and materials during the construction, repair, or cleaning of a building.
• Sacraments: [Family & Religious Studies] Holy, symbolic religious rituals recognized as outward signs of inward spiritual grace (e.g., baptism, holy communion).
• Secured Loan: [Commerce / Principles of Accounts] A loan agreement where the borrower pledges specific assets as collateral, minimizing risk for the lender.
• Screed: [Building Technology] A smooth, level layer of fine concrete mortar applied over a rough concrete floor slab to prepare it for floor finishes.
• Sight Bill: [Commerce] A bill of exchange that must be paid instantly upon presentation to the drawee, containing no delayed maturity date.
• Sole Proprietorship: [Commerce / Principles of Accounts] A flexible business entity owned, managed, and financed by one single individual who bears unlimited personal liability.
• Spirit Mediumship: [Family & Religious Studies] The spiritual state where a living individual acts as an active vessel for a spirit (Svikiro / Amadlozi) to speak to communities.
• Statement of Account: [Commerce / Principles of Accounts] A periodic summary sent by a supplier to a customer detailing invoices, payments made, and the remaining balance due.
• Stretcher: [Building Technology] A brick laid flat along its long edge, showing its long face on the exterior surface of a wall.
• Superstructure: [Building Technology] The complete portion of a building constructed safely above ground level, sitting atop the foundation.

Section T

• Totemism: [Family & Religious Studies] A spiritual and social system where a community maintains a sacred link to an animal emblem, preventing intermarriage and preserving environmental wildlife.
• Trade Discount: [Commerce / Principles of Accounts] A direct percentage reduction from the catalog price of goods, allowed by manufacturers to wholesalers or retailers buying in bulk.
• Tread: [Building Technology] The flat, horizontal surface of a stair step upon which a person steps.
• Trial Balance: [Principles of Accounts] A financial statement listing the total debit and credit balances of all ledger accounts to verify arithmetical accuracy before compiling final accounts.
• Truss: [Building Technology] A rigid structural framework of timbers or steel sections arranged in triangles to span wide spaces, notably used in roof construction.
• Trustee: [Commerce / General Law] An individual or entity given legal responsibility to hold, manage, and safeguard assets on behalf of a third party.

Section U

• Unlimited Liability: [Commerce] A legal condition where owners are personally responsible for all business debts, meaning personal assets can be seized to pay creditors.
• Underwriting: [Commerce] The process where a financial institution guarantees to buy any unsold shares during a public share issue, or an insurer accepts risk in exchange for a premium.

Section V

• Vadzimu: [Family & Religious Studies] The benevolent ancestral spirits of a family lineage who act as protective intermediaries between humanity and God (Mwari).
• Vouching: [Principles of Accounts] The auditing process of verifying entries in the books of original entry by checking them against supporting receipts and documents.
• Vibrator: [Building Technology] A mechanical device used to agitate freshly poured concrete, compacting the mix and eliminating trapped air pockets.

Section W

• Wall Plate: [Building Technology] A horizontal timber beam anchored along the top of a masonry wall to receive, level, and anchor the feet of roof rafters or trusses.
• Working Capital: [Principles of Accounts / Commerce] A liquidity measure calculated by subtracting current liabilities from current assets, showing available operational funds.
• Write-off: [Principles of Accounts] The accounting process of removing an asset from the books entirely, converting it into a business expense (e.g., writing off a bad debt).

Section X

• Xenoglossy: [Family & Religious Studies] A religious or spiritual phenomenon where an individual speaks a foreign language they have no prior knowledge of, typically observed during spiritual possession or charismatic worship.

Section Y

• Youth Ministries: [Family & Religious Studies] Specialized organizational divisions inside religious institutions designed to deliver moral guidance and community development to young people.

Section Z

• Zionist Churches: [Family & Religious Studies] A major branch of African Independent Churches (AICs) blending Christian doctrine with traditional African spiritual practices, healing rituals, and distinct holy clothing.



