• Bicuspid Valve: [Advanced Biology] The two-flapped valve located between the left atrium and left ventricle of the heart that prevents the backflow of blood.
• Bile: [Advanced Biology / Combined Science] A green alkaline fluid manufactured by the liver and stored in the gall bladder that emulsifies fats to facilitate digestion.
• Binary Code: [ICT / Computer Systems] A base-2 numbering system consisting entirely of digits 0 and 1, used as the fundamental machine language by digital processors.
• Binary Fission: [Advanced Biology] A primary form of asexual reproduction occurring in prokaryotes where a single cell duplicates its genetic material and splits into two identical cells.
• Binomial Nomenclature: [Advanced Biology] The universal scientific system of naming organisms using two Latin words, the first representing the Genus and the second the species.
• Biocatalyst: [Advanced Biology / Chemistry] A biological macro-molecule, most commonly a protein-based enzyme, that accelerates metabolic chemical reactions within cells.
• Biodegradable: [Combined Science / Chemistry] Substances that can be completely broken down naturally into simple inorganic molecules by soil bacteria and fungi within a short time frame.
• Biodiversity: [Combined Science / Advanced Biology] The total structural variety and abundance of different living species, habitats, and genetic variations present within an ecosystem.
• Biogas: [Combined Science / Agriculture] A clean-burning gas composed mostly of methane, produced by breaking down animal dung and organic waste inside an airtight underground pit.
• Biomagnification: [Combined Science / Chemistry] The progressive increase in the concentration of a non-biodegradable toxic chemical within the tissues of organisms at higher levels of a food chain.
• Biomass: [Combined Science / Biology] The total dry organic weight or mass of living organisms present within a specific trophic level or defined ecological area.
• Biome: [Advanced Biology / Geography] A massive regional ecosystem characterized by distinct climate patterns, soil profiles, and dominant vegetation structures (e.g., tropical rainforest or savannah).
• Biosphere: [Combined Science / Geography] The global ecological system integrating all living beings and their relationships, representing the zone of life on Earth.
• Biotic Factor: [Combined Science / Biology] Any living organism, population interaction, or biological component within an ecosystem that influences other species (e.g., predation or disease).
• Biuret Test: [Combined Science / Biology] A chemical test utilizing sodium hydroxide and copper sulfate reagents to confirm the presence of proteins, turning from blue to violet if peptide bonds are present.
• Bivalve: [Advanced Biology] An aquatic mollusk possessing a compressed body enclosed within a hinged shell consisting of two matching parts (e.g., clams or mussels).
• Blastocyst: [Advanced Biology] An early developmental stage of an embryo consisting of a hollow ball of cells that implants into the uterine lining.
• Bloom's Taxonomy: [General Education] A hierarchical framework classifying educational learning objectives into tiers from simple recall up to synthesis and creation.
• Bohr Effect: [Advanced Biology] A physiological phenomenon where an increase in carbon dioxide concentration or a drop in pH reduces hemoglobin's affinity for oxygen, encouraging oxygen release to tissues.
• Boiling Point: [Chemistry / Combined Science] The exact temperature at which the vapor pressure of a liquid equals the external atmospheric pressure, triggering a state transition into gas.
• Borehole: [Combined Science / Agriculture] A deep, narrow well drilled by machines into the ground to pump clean, filtered underground water up to communities.
• Boyle's Law: [Chemistry / Physics] A gas law stating that the volume of a fixed mass of gas is inversely proportional to its pressure, provided temperature is held constant (\(P_1V_1 = P_2V_2\)).
• British South Africa Company (BSAC): [History / Heritage Studies] A mercantile company established by Cecil John Rhodes through a royal charter in 1889 to colonize, govern, and commercially exploit minerals and land north of the Limpopo River.
• Broadcasting: [Agriculture / Combined Science] An agricultural method of sowing seeds or distributing fertilizers by manually scattering them widely across the surface of a field.
• Broiler: [Agriculture / Combined Science] A domestic fowl variety bred and managed specifically for high-rate meat production, typically reaching market weight within six weeks.
• Bronchus: [Advanced Biology / Combined Science] One of the two primary branches of the trachea that conduct air directly into the left and right lungs.
• Browser: [ICT / Computer Systems] A software application used to open, view, and navigate websites on the World Wide Web.
• Bryophyte: [Advanced Biology] A small, non-vascular land plant family lacking specialized xylem and phloem tissues, absorbing water directly through rhizoids (e.g., mosses).
• Budding: [Agriculture / Advanced Biology] A horticultural technique where a single detached bud from a desired plant is inserted into the stem of a rooted rootstock plant to fuse and grow.
• Buffer Solution: [Chemistry / Advanced Biology] A chemical solution composed of a weak acid and its conjugate base that strongly resists shifts in pH when minor amounts of acid or alkali are introduced.
• Budgeting: [Principles of Accounts / Commerce] Creating a detailed financial plan that balances expected income against estimated expenses for a project, business, or household.
• Burette: [Chemistry / Combined Science] A long, graduated glass tube equipped with a stopcock at the bottom, used to accurately dispense precise volumes of liquid during acid-base titrations.
• Bus Topology: [ICT / Computer Systems] A network architecture where all computer nodes and peripherals are linked sequentially along a single central communication cable.

Section C

• Cadmium Rods: [Combined Science / Physics] High-density neutron-absorbing control rods inserted into nuclear reactors to regulate or arrest the rate of nuclear fission reactions.
• Calorie: [Combined Science / Biology] A unit of measurement used to quantify the energy content of food, defined as the thermal energy needed to raise the temperature of one gram of water by one degree Celsius.
• Calorimeter: [Chemistry / Physics] A thermal laboratory apparatus designed to precisely measure the heat energy absorbed or released during a chemical reaction or physical state change.
• Calvin Cycle: [Advanced Biology] The light-independent stage of photosynthesis occurring in the stroma of chloroplasts, where carbon dioxide is fixed into organic glucose using ATP and NADPH.
• Calyx: [Advanced Biology] The collective term for the outermost whorl of a flower composed of sepals that protect the developing floral bud.
• Cambium: [Advanced Biology] A lateral layer of actively dividing meristematic plant cells responsible for secondary growth, generating new xylem and phloem layers.
• Camouflage: [Combined Science / Biology] A natural coloring or pattern that allows an animal to blend into its surroundings to hide from predators or prey.
• Canine: [Advanced Biology / Combined Science] A pointed, structural tooth located between the incisors and premolars, adapted for tearing tough meat or food matter.
• Capacity: [Mathematics] The maximum volume of liquid or substance a hollow container can hold when completely filled, measured in liters or milliliters.
• Capillary: [Advanced Biology / Combined Science] A microscopic blood vessel with single-cell walls where nutrients, gases, and wastes are exchanged between blood and surrounding tissues.
• Capillary Action: [Combined Science / Physics] The spontaneous movement of a liquid up a narrow tube or porous material driven by the combined forces of adhesion and cohesion.
• Carbohydrate: [Combined Science / Biology] An organic compound composed of carbon, hydrogen, and oxygen that serves as a primary source of metabolic energy for living organisms.
• Carbon Cycle: [Combined Science / Biology] The continuous ecological process where carbon atoms circulate between the atmosphere, living organisms, oceans, and geological deposits.
• Carbon Footprint: [Combined Science / Geography] The total greenhouse gas emissions, primarily carbon dioxide, released into the atmosphere by human activities, industrial processes, or individual lifestyles.
• Carboxylic Acids: [Chemistry] An organic homologous series containing the acidic functional group (\(-COOH\)) attached to a carbon chain (e.g., ethanoic acid).
• Carcinogen: [Advanced Biology / Combined Science] Any physical, chemical, or biological agent that is directly capable of altering cellular DNA and causing cancer in living tissues.
• Cardiac Muscle: [Advanced Biology] A specialized, myogenic involuntary muscle tissue found exclusively within the walls of the heart, adapted for continuous rhythmic contraction without fatigue.
• Cardiovascular Endurance: [Physical Education / Biology] The capacity of the heart, lungs, and blood vessels to deliver oxygen to working muscles during sustained physical activity.
• Carnivore: [Combined Science / Biology] An animal organism that obtains its metabolic energy and nutrients exclusively by hunting and consuming other animals.
• Cartage: [Commerce / Principles of Accounts] The financial charge or shipping expense paid for the terrestrial hauling and short-distance delivery of commercial goods.
• Cartilage: [Advanced Biology] A dense, flexible connective tissue found at joint surfaces, supporting structures like the ears and trachea, reducing friction and absorbing mechanical shock.
• Cash Crop: [Agriculture / Combined Science] An agricultural crop grown primarily for commercial sale to markets or international export rather than for personal family consumption.
• Catabolism: [Advanced Biology] The metabolic pathway where complex organic molecules are broken down into simpler units, releasing chemical energy for cell work.
• Catalyst: [Chemistry / Combined Science] A chemical substance that increases the speed of a chemical reaction by lowering its activation energy without undergoing any permanent chemical change.
• Catalytic Cracking: [Chemistry] The industrial refining process where long-chain heavy petroleum hydrocarbons are broken down into smaller, highly useful alkenes and alkanes using heat and a zeolitic catalyst.
• Cathode: [Chemistry / Combined Science] The negatively charged electrode in an electrical cell toward which cations migrate during electrolysis.
• Cation: [Chemistry / Combined Science] A positively charged ion generated when a metallic atom loses one or more valence electrons.
• Cavity Wall: [Building Technology] A specialized exterior wall configuration consisting of two parallel leaves of masonry separated by a continuous air space, designed to prevent dampness and provide thermal insulation.
• Cell Membrane: [Combined Science / Biology] The flexible semi-permeable lipid bilayer outer boundary of a cell that controls which substances enter and exit.
• Cell Wall: [Combined Science / Biology] A rigid, protective structural layer surrounding the cell membrane of plant cells, fungi, and bacteria, composed primarily of cellulose in plants.
• Cellular Respiration: [Combined Science / Biology] The universal intracellular chemical process where organic nutrient molecules are broken down to release energy in the form of ATP.
• Cellulose: [Advanced Biology / Chemistry] A tough, insoluble complex carbohydrate polysaccharide composed of glucose chains, forming the primary structural component of plant cell walls.
• Cement: [Building Technology] A hydraulic binding material composed of calcined limestone and clay, which sets and hardens when mixed with water.
• Central Business District (CBD): [Combined Science / Geography] The commercial, financial, and retail core of a city characterized by high land values and high-rise offices.
• Central Nervous System (CNS): [Advanced Biology / Combined Science] The primary processing division of the nervous system composed exclusively of the brain and spinal cord.
• Centrifugal Force: [Physics] An apparent outward force experienced by an object moving in a curved or circular path, acting directly away from the center of rotation.
• Centripetal Force: [Combined Science / Physics] A net force that acts continuously on a body moving in a circular path, directed inward toward the center of curvature to maintain circular motion.
• Cereal Crops: [Agriculture / Combined Science] Cultivated grasses grown for their edible grains, forming the backbone of regional food supplies.
• Cervix: [Advanced Biology / Combined Science] The narrow neck or lower opening of the uterus that connects directly into the vagina.
• Charles's Law: [Chemistry / Physics] A gas law stating that the volume of a fixed mass of gas is directly proportional to its absolute temperature, provided pressure is held constant (\(\frac{V_1}{T_1} = \frac{V_2}{T_2}\)).
• Charter: [History / Heritage Studies] A formal written document issued by a monarch or government granting specific rights, monopolies, land privileges, or political authority to an individual or company.
• Chemical Change: [Chemistry / Combined Science] A process in which one or more substances are permanently transformed into entirely new chemical substances with distinct chemical and physical properties.
• Chemical Equation: [Chemistry / Combined Science] A symbolic representation of a chemical reaction using chemical formulas to show the identities and ratios of reactants and products.
• Chemiosmosis: [Advanced Biology] The process where ATP is synthesized as protons move down an electrochemical gradient across a membrane through ATP synthase during respiration or photosynthesis.
• Chemoautotroph: [Advanced Biology] An organism that manufactures its own food using chemical energy extracted from inorganic chemical oxidation processes, independent of sunlight.
• Chief: [Heritage Studies / History] A traditional leader of a rural area responsible for safeguarding culture, resolving local disputes, and allocating communal land.
• Chinyakare: [Heritage Studies / History] The collective term for traditional ways of living, indigenous knowledge systems, folklore, rituals, and customs.
• Chitepo Assassination: [History / Heritage Studies] The targeted car-bomb killing of prominent nationalist leader Herbert Chitepo in Lusaka, Zambia, in 1975.
• Chitin: [Advanced Biology] A tough nitrogenous polysaccharide forming the rigid exoskeleton of arthropods and the structural cell walls of fungi.
• Chlorophyll: [Combined Science / Biology] The green photosynthetic magnesium-bearing pigment found inside chloroplasts that absorbs light energy for photosynthesis.
• Chloroplast: [Combined Science / Biology] An organelle in green plant cells containing chlorophyll where the light-dependent and light-independent stages of photosynthesis take place.
• Choreography: [Fine Arts / Physical Education] The art and practice of designing, arranging, and structuring a sequential series of dance steps, movements, and formations.
• Chorda Tympani: [Advanced Biology] A structural branch of the facial nerve that transmits taste sensations from the anterior two-thirds of the tongue to the brain.
• Chord: [Mathematics] A straight line segment whose endpoints both lie on the outer perimeter or circumference of a circle or curve.
• Chromatid: [Advanced Biology] One of the two identical parallel strands of a duplicated chromosome that are joined together at a central centromere prior to cell division.
• Chromatography: [Chemistry / Combined Science] A separation technique used to divide complex mixtures of solutes based on their varying solubility and rates of migration across a stationary phase.
• Chromosomal Mutation: [Advanced Biology] A structural or numerical modification occurring in an entire chromosome, such as deletions, duplications, or inversions.
• Chromosome: [Advanced Biology / Combined Science] A thread-like structural package of DNA and histone proteins found inside the nucleus of a cell, carrying genetic information in the form of linear genes.
• Chronology: [History] The arrangement of historical events or dates in the exact sequential order of their occurrence over time.
• Chyme: [Advanced Biology] The pulpy, semi-fluid mass of partly digested food mixed with gastric secretions that passes from the stomach into the duodenum.
• Ciliary Muscle: [Advanced Biology / Combined Science] A ring of smooth muscle sheets surrounding the lens of the eye that contracts or relaxes to adjust lens shape for accommodation.
• Circuit: [Combined Science / Physics] A complete, closed loop path through which electric current can flow continuously.
• Circulatory System: [Combined Science / Biology] The systemic organ network composed of the heart, blood vessels, and blood responsible for transporting oxygen, nutrients, and wastes throughout the body.
• Cirrus: [Combined Science / Geography] High-altitude, wispy, feather-like clouds composed entirely of ice crystals, indicating fair weather or an approaching front.
• Citrus: [Agriculture / Combined Science] A genus of evergreen aromatic trees that yield acidic, vitamin C-rich fruits with thick rinds, such as oranges, lemons, and grapefruits.
• Civilization: [History / Heritage Studies] An advanced stage of human social development and organization characterized by urban settlements, specialized labor, governance, and architecture.
• Classification: [Advanced Biology] The systematic taxonomy of grouping living organisms into hierarchical categories based on structural, genetic, and evolutionary similarities.
• Clearing: [Agriculture / Combined Science] The agricultural process of removing trees, vegetation, and rocks from a patch of land to prepare it for crop cultivation.
• Climax Community: [Advanced Biology] The final, stable ecological community that develops at the end stage of ecological succession if left undisturbed by human or natural forces.
• Climate: [Combined Science / Geography] The average, long-term weather patterns and atmospheric conditions of a specific geographic region recorded over a minimum of thirty years.
• Clone: [Advanced Biology] A cell or whole organism that is genetically identical to the parent organism from which it was derived via asexual paths.
• Clotting Factors: [Advanced Biology] A complex cascade of plasma proteins that interact sequentially to convert soluble fibrinogen into insoluble fibrin to seal wounds.
• Cloud Computing: [ICT / Computer Systems] The practice of utilizing a network of remote servers hosted on the internet to store, manage, and process data rather than a local hard drive.
• Coat of Arms: [Heritage Studies / Civics] An official state badge featuring regional animals, shields, agricultural crops, and national land features, symbolizing state authority.
• Codon: [Advanced Biology] A specific sequence of three consecutive nucleotides on an mRNA molecule that codes for a single amino acid during protein synthesis.
• Coefficient of Friction: [Physics] A dimensionless numeric ratio expressing the frictional force resisting movement between two surfaces relative to the normal force pressing them together.
• Cohesion: [Combined Science / Physics] The attractive intermolecular electrostatic force occurring between molecules of the identical substance, generating water surface tension.
• Coitus: [Advanced Biology] The physical reproductive act of copulation or sexual intercourse between a male and female.
• Colloidal Solution: [Chemistry] A chemical mixture where microscopic insoluble particles remain uniformly suspended throughout a liquid medium without settling due to gravity.
• Cognitive Load: [General Education] The total amount of mental effort and information processing memory being used at one time by a student during learning.
• Colonialism: [History / Heritage Studies] A system where a foreign power occupies a country, exploits its resources, and oppresses its native population.
• Combustion: [Chemistry / Combined Science] A high-temperature exothermic chemical reaction between a fuel substance and an oxidant, typically atmospheric oxygen, producing heat, light, and oxides.
• Commercial Agriculture: [Agriculture / Combined Science] Large-scale farming managed with machinery and modern inputs aimed primarily at maximizing crop profits for market sales.
• Community: [Combined Science / Biology] A group of different living species populations sharing and interacting within the same physical environment or habitat.
• Companion Cell: [Advanced Biology] A specialized plant cell located adjacent to a phloem sieve tube element, packed with mitochondria to fuel nutrient translocation.
• Compass: [Combined Science / Physics] A magnetic navigational instrument housing a free-spinning pointer that aligns with Earth's magnetic field to show direction.
• Competitive Inhibition: [Advanced Biology / Chemistry] A form of enzyme inhibition where a fake substrate molecule mimics the real substrate and locks into the active site, blocking catalysis.
• Compiler: [ICT / Computer Systems] A specialized system program that translates source code written in a high-level language into machine code all at once before execution.
• Composition: [Fine Arts / English] An original piece of multi-paragraph creative or formal writing produced by a student, or the arrangement of elements in visual arts.
• Compost: [Agriculture / Combined Science] A rich organic soil amendment composed of layers of decomposed crop residues, leaves, and organic waste left to decay over time.
• Compound: [Chemistry / Combined Science] A chemical substance composed of two or more separate elements bonded together in a fixed weight ratio.
• Compound Microscope: [Advanced Biology / Combined Science] An optical instrument utilizing a multi-lens system (objective and ocular lenses) to generate highly magnified images of microscopic specimens.
• Comprehension: [English / Shona / Ndebele] The active cognitive ability to read a passage of text, absorb its core details, and accurately answer analytical questions about it.
• Concession: [History / Heritage Studies] A lease, grant, or license given by a local ruler or sovereign government to a foreign entity, permitting them to mine resources or trade.
• Condensation: [Combined Science / Physics] The physical phase transition where a gas cools down and turns back into a liquid state.
• Conditional Probability: [Mathematics / Statistics] The mathematical likelihood of an event occurring given that another specific event has already occurred.
• Conduction: [Combined Science / Physics] The transfer of thermal energy or electrical charge through a material via direct molecular contact without any net movement of the material itself.
• Conductor: [Combined Science / Physics] Any material substance, such as copper or aluminum, that possesses a dense pool of free-moving valence electrons, allowing heat or electricity to flow easily.
• Cone Cell: [Advanced Biology / Combined Science] A specialized photoreceptor cell situated within the retina of the human eye responsible for color vision and visual acuity in bright light.
• Coniferous: [Advanced Biology / Geography] A family of evergreen gymnosperm trees that bear cones and possess needle-shaped leaves to limit water loss (e.g., pines).
• Conjunction: [English / Shona / Ndebele] A connecting word used to link phrases, clauses, or sentences together.
• Conjugation: [Advanced Biology] A mechanism of horizontal gene transfer in bacteria involving direct cell-to-cell contact and the transmission of a plasmid link.
• Conservation: [Combined Science / Agriculture] The sustainable management and active protection of natural resources, ecosystems, and wildlife from exploitation or destruction.
• Constitution: [Civics / Heritage Studies] The supreme written law book of a nation that outlines the rights of citizens and guides how the government must run.
• Consumer: [Combined Science / Biology] An organism that cannot manufacture its own food and obtains its energy by feeding on other living organisms or organic matter.
• Contamination: [Combined Science / Chemistry] The introduction of dangerous bacteria, chemicals, or waste materials into clean water, food, or environments.
• Continuous Assessment Learning Activity (CALA): [General Education] A holistic student assessment framework focusing on practical, research-oriented tasks executed alongside formal exams.
• Continuous Variation: [Advanced Biology] A pattern of genetic variation showing a smooth gradient of phenotypes across a spectrum, heavily influenced by multi-gene interactions and environment (e.g., human height).
• Control Experiment: [Combined Science] A scientific experiment setup where all variable conditions are kept perfectly constant except for the single target variable being tested, serving as a baseline for comparison.
• Convection: [Combined Science / Physics] The transfer of thermal energy through a fluid driven by temperature-induced density differences that cause warmer fluids to rise and cooler fluids to sink.
• Convergent Plate Boundary: [Combined Science / Geography] A tectonic boundary where two crustal plates are actively colliding into each other, forming mountain ranges or subduction trenches.
• Coordination: [Physical Education / Biology] The physical ability to execute smooth, efficient, and controlled movements by combining the actions of different muscle groups and senses.
• Copestone: [Building Technology] The top, protective stone or brick course laid along the crest of a wall or parapet, designed to shed rainwater and protect masonry below.
• Corolla: [Advanced Biology] The collective term for the whorl of a flower composed of petals, often brightly colored to attract insect pollinators.
• Corrosion: [Chemistry / Combined Science] The slow, destructive chemical degradation of a metal surface by reacting with environmental oxygen or moisture (e.g., the rusting of iron).
• Corpus Luteum: [Advanced Biology] A temporary yellow endocrine structure formed inside an ovary from a ruptured follicle right after ovulation, secreting progesterone to maintain pregnancy.
• Cotyledon: [Advanced Biology / Combined Science] An embryonic seed leaf found within a plant embryo that stores nutrients to feed the germinating seedling.
• Covalent Bond: [Chemistry / Combined Science] A strong chemical bond formed between non-metal atoms through the mutual sharing of one or more pairs of valence electrons.
• CPU (Central Processing Unit): [ICT / Computer Systems] The main microchip or electronic brain of a computer system that processes data and carries out software instructions.
• Critical Angle: [Combined Science / Physics] The specific angle of incidence inside a denser medium that causes a refracted light ray to bend exactly 90 degrees, skimming along the boundary surface.
• Critical Thinking: [General Education] The mental capacity to analyze facts, evaluate arguments, and solve complex problems logically.
• Crop Rotation: [Agriculture / Combined Science] The systematic practice of growing different types of crops sequentially in the same field plot across successive seasons to maintain soil fertility and break pest cycles.
• Crude Oil: [Chemistry / Combined Science] A complex underground mixture of liquid hydrocarbons formed over millions of years from buried marine remains, refined via fractional distillation.
• Crystalline: [Chemistry] A solid material structure characterized by a highly ordered, repeating three-dimensional geometric lattice of atoms or ions.
• Curriculum Framework: [General Education] An official policy document mapping out the subjects, skills, core competencies, and assessment methods required across schools.
• Currency Conversion: [Mathematics / Commerce] Calculating the comparative value when exchanging money between different national currency systems.
• Current: [Combined Science / Physics] The mathematical rate of flow of electric charge past a specific point in a conductive circuit, measured in Amperes.
• Cuticle: [Advanced Biology / Combined Science] A waxy, water-resistant layer covering the outer epidermis of plant leaves to suppress water transpiration.
• Cytoplasm: [Combined Science / Biology] The jelly-like fluid filling the inside of a cell where most chemical activities and metabolic reactions take place.

Section D

• Dam: [Combined Science / Agriculture] A thick concrete or earthen wall built across a flowing river to block and store water for community use, livestock, or crop irrigation.
• Damp-Proof Course (DPC): [Building Technology] A horizontal layer of impervious material, such as polythene sheet or asphalt, inserted into a wall above ground level to prevent capillary moisture from rising.
• Dariro: [Fine Arts / Heritage Studies] A traditional circular gathering formation used during dances, meetings, and storytelling to signify equality and community unity.
• Database Management System (DBMS): [ICT / Computer Systems] A software suite used to create, maintain, query, organize, and secure structured collections of data files.
• Data Collection: [General Education / Statistics] The systematic gathering of specific facts, measurements, or observations using research instruments.
• Data Encryption: [ICT / Computer Systems] A security practice that encodes digital information using algorithms, making it unreadable to unauthorized parties without a decryption key.
• Data Presentation: [General Education / Mathematics] The process of organizing research findings visually using bar graphs, tally charts, tables, or pictograms.
• Data Security: [ICT / Computer Systems] The practice of protecting digital files, school projects, and personal accounts from unauthorized access, hacking, or corruption.
• Dative Covalent Bond: [Chemistry] A specialized covalent bond where both shared bonding electrons are contributed by only one of the participating atoms.
• Debenture: [Commerce / Principles of Accounts] A long-term debt instrument or bond issued by a limited company to raise capital, backed by a fixed or floating charge over assets.
• Debit Note: [Commerce / Principles of Accounts] A commercial document sent by a buyer to a seller, or vice versa, formally requesting a financial adjustment or indicating an undercharge.
• Deciduous: [Agriculture / Advanced Biology] Trees and shrubs that shed all their leaves annually during the dry winter season to conserve moisture.
• Decimal: [Mathematics] A notation system for writing numbers using a base-10 framework, where a decimal point separates whole units from fractions of a unit.
• Decomposers: [Combined Science / Biology] Heterotrophic organisms, primarily bacteria and fungi, that break down complex organic matter from dead plants and animals into simple inorganic nutrients.
• Deforestation: [Combined Science / Agriculture] The systematic clearing, cutting down, or destruction of natural woodlands or forests without adequate replanting.
• Dehydration: [Advanced Biology / Combined Science] A dangerous condition where the human body loses more water and fluids than it takes in, altering electrolyte balance.
• Deliquescence: [Chemistry] The physical property of a solid substance to absorb moisture from the atmosphere until it dissolves completely to form an aqueous solution.
• Denaturation: [Advanced Biology / Chemistry] A structural transformation where extreme heat or shifts in pH destroy the three-dimensional shape of a protein or enzyme, rendering it permanently non-functional.
• Dendrite: [Advanced Biology] Short, branching extensions of a neuron cell body that act as receptors to pick up electrical incoming signals from adjacent axons.
• Denitrifiers: [Advanced Biology] Specialized anaerobic soil bacteria that convert soil nitrates back into gaseous atmospheric nitrogen, reducing soil nutrients.
• Denominator: [Mathematics] The bottom number in a fraction, indicating the total number of equal parts into which a whole has been divided.
• Density: [Combined Science / Physics] A physical property defined as the mass of a substance per unit volume (\(\rho = \frac{m}{V}\)).
• Depreciation: [Principles of Accounts] The systematic allocation of the cost of a tangible fixed asset over its useful economic life due to wear and tear or obsolescence.
• Dermis: [Advanced Biology] The deep, vascular inner layer of human skin situated directly beneath the outer epidermis, containing sweat glands and hair follicles.
• Desertification: [Combined Science / Geography] The gradual transformation of fertile agricultural or grazing land into dry desert terrain due to drought, deforestation, or land mismanagement.
• Determinant: [Mathematics] A scalar numeric value derived from a square matrix that characterizes its properties, such as whether an inverse matrix exists.
• Deuterium: [Chemistry] A stable isotope of Hydrogen consisting of exactly one proton and one neutron within its nucleus (\({}^{2}H\)).
• Diabetes mellitus: [Advanced Biology / Combined Science] A metabolic disorder characterized by high blood glucose levels caused by a failure of the pancreas to secrete sufficient insulin.
• Dialogue: [English / Literature] A written conversation unfolding between two or more characters in a story, essay, or script.
• Dialysis: [Advanced Biology] A clinical filtration process used to cleanse blood of metabolic wastes in patients suffering from kidney failure.
• Diaphragm: [Advanced Biology / Combined Science] A dome-shaped sheet of skeletal muscle separating the thoracic cavity from the abdomen, contracting to drive lung ventilation.
• Diastole: [Advanced Biology] The phase of the heartbeat cycle where the heart muscle relaxes, allowing the chambers to fill with returning blood.
• Dicotyledon: [Advanced Biology / Combined Science] A class of flowering plants characterized by having seeds containing two embryonic cotyledons, net-veined leaves, and taproot systems.
• Differentiated Learning: [General Education] A teaching method that adapts lessons to match the unique learning styles, speeds, and academic abilities of individual students.
• Differentiation: [Mathematics] A calculus operation that determines the exact rate of change, derivative, or instantaneous gradient of a function at a specific point.
• Differentiation (Biology): [Advanced Biology] The developmental process where unspecialized stem cells undergo structural modifications to become specialized cells with distinct functions.
• Diffusion: [Chemistry / Combined Science] The net movement of particles from a region of higher concentration to a region of lower concentration down a concentration gradient due to random thermal motion.
• Digestion: [Combined Science / Biology] The mechanical and chemical breakdown of large, insoluble food molecules into small, water-soluble molecules that can be absorbed by cells.
• Digital Data: [ICT / Computer Systems] Information that is converted and stored into discrete numeric values, typically binary digits, for electronic processing.
• Digital Literacy: [ICT / Computer Systems] Having the practical skills to confidently navigate computers, use software applications, and evaluate online research safely.
• Dip Tank: [Agriculture / Combined Science] A deep, narrow concrete trench filled with water and liquid chemical acaricides through which cattle are driven to kill disease-carrying ticks.
• Dipole: [Chemistry] A chemical molecule possessing a permanent structural separation of positive and negative electrical charges across its covalent bonds.
• Direct Current (DC): [Combined Science / Physics] An electric current that flows continuously and uniformly in one single direction through an electrical circuit.
• Direct Rule: [History / Heritage Studies] A colonial administrative framework where the occupying European power replaces local indigenous authorities with foreign officials governing under metropolitan laws.
• Direct Speech: [English / Shona / Ndebele] The exact words spoken by a person, enclosed tightly within quotation marks during writing.
• Disaccharide: [Advanced Biology / Chemistry] A double sugar molecule composed of two simple monosaccharides linked together via a glycosidic bond (e.g., sucrose).
• Discontinuous Variation: [Advanced Biology] A pattern of genetic variation where traits fall into distinct, separate categories with no intermediates, controlled by single genes (e.g., ABO blood groups).
• Disinfectant: [Combined Science / Biology] A strong chemical agent applied onto non-living surfaces to destroy pathogenic bacteria and viruses.
• Displacement: [Combined Science / Physics] A vector quantity indicating the straight-line distance and direction from an object's initial reference position to its final position.
• Displacement Reaction: [Chemistry / Combined Science] A chemical reaction where a highly reactive element displaces a less reactive element from its compound solution.
• Distillation: [Chemistry / Combined Science] A thermal separation method that involves boiling a liquid solution and condensing its vapor to separate components based on boiling points.
• Divination: [Family & Religious Studies] The spiritual practice of seeking hidden knowledge, future events, or the will of ancestors and spirits through specialized rituals.
• Divergent Plate Boundary: [Combined Science / Geography] A tectonic boundary where two crustal plates are moving away from each other, creating new crust as magma rises into the gap.
• Division: [Mathematics] The mathematical operation of splitting a number into equal parts or groups to find the quotient.
• DNA (Deoxyribonucleic Acid): [Advanced Biology / Combined Science] The double-helix macro-molecule found inside cell nuclei that carries the primary genetic codes and hereditary instructions for living organisms.
• Domain: [Mathematics] The complete set of all possible independent input values for which a mathematical function is validly defined.
• Dominant Allele: [Advanced Biology / Combined Science] A genetic variant that is physically expressed in an organism's phenotype even if only one copy is inherited alongside a recessive variant.
• Dormancy: [Advanced Biology / Agriculture] A temporary period of suspended growth and metabolic activity in seeds or buds to survive hostile environmental conditions.
• Double Circulatory System: [Advanced Biology / Combined Science] A vascular system where blood passes through the heart twice during one complete circuit of the body, split into pulmonary and systemic loops.
• Double-Entry Bookkeeping: [Principles of Accounts] The accounting system where every economic transaction is recorded with at least one debit entry and a corresponding credit entry.
• Drawings: [Principles of Accounts] Cash, inventory, or assets taken directly out of a business by the owner for personal use, reducing owner's equity.
• Draught Animal: [Agriculture / Combined Science] A domesticated livestock animal, such as an ox or donkey, bred and trained to perform heavy physical labor like plowing fields or pulling carts.
• Drip Irrigation: [Agriculture / Combined Science] A water-conserving irrigation system that delivers slow, precise drops of water directly to the root zone of crops through plastic pipes.
• Drosophila melanogaster: [Advanced Biology] The common fruit fly used extensively as a model organism in genetics research to track inheritance patterns.
• Drought: [Combined Science / Agriculture] A long period of time with little to no rainfall, leading to water shortages, crop failures, and livestock deaths.
• Drought-Resistant Crop: [Agriculture / Combined Science] A hardy crop variety capable of enduring prolonged periods of low soil moisture and erratic rainfall while still producing a stable harvest.
• Duodenum: [Advanced Biology / Combined Science] The initial, C-shaped section of the small intestine where chyme is mixed with pancreatic juices and bile for intense chemical digestion.
• Duties of a Citizen: [Civics / Heritage Studies] Constitutional responsibilities like paying taxes, voting, obeying local laws, and protecting national property.
• Dynasty: [History] A succession of hereditary rulers belonging to the exact same family line who maintain political power across multiple generations.

Section E

• E-Commerce: [Commerce] The buying, selling, trading, or marketing of physical goods and digital services over internet platforms and applications.
• E-Learning: [General Education / ICT] An educational framework utilizing electronic media, internet platforms, digital devices, and software apps to deliver instructional lessons remotely.
• Earthing: [Combined Science / Physics] A safety practice in electrical installations where a low-resistance wire connects the metal chassis of an appliance directly to the ground to prevent shock.
• Echocardiogram: [Advanced Biology] A diagnostic medical ultrasound procedure used to image the structural valves, chambers, and blood flow patterns of the heart.
• Ecology: [Combined Science / Biology] The scientific study of the interactions, relationships, and distributions of organisms relative to each other and their physical environment.
• Ecosystem: [Combined Science / Biology] A distinct ecological unit comprising all interacting biotic living communities and their surrounding abiotic non-living physical components.
• Ectoparasite: [Advanced Biology / Agriculture] A parasitic organism that lives, feeds, and attaches onto the exterior surface or skin of a host animal (e.g., ticks or fleas).
• Effector: [Advanced Biology / Combined Science] A responsive muscle gland or organ that executes a physical action or secretion when stimulated by a motor nerve impulse.
• Efficiency: [Combined Science / Physics] A dimensionless ratio measuring the useful energy output of a machine relative to the total energy input, expressed as a percentage.
• Efflorescence: [Chemistry] The spontaneous loss of water of crystallization from a hydrated crystalline salt when exposed to dry air, leaving a powdery residue on the surface.
• Egestion: [Combined Science / Biology] The biological process of discharging undigested, unabsorbed food residues and solid waste materials from the digestive tract of an animal.
• El Niño: [Combined Science / Geography] A climate pattern characterized by abnormal warming of surface waters in the eastern Pacific Ocean that frequently triggers severe droughts in Southern Africa.
• Elasticity: [Physics] The structural property of a material enabling it to return to its original shape and dimensions after the deformative stretching forces are removed.
• Elder: [Heritage Studies / Family & Religious Studies] A senior member of a community respected for their wisdom, historical knowledge, and role in passing down oral traditions.
• Electrical Conductivity: [Combined Science / Physics] A physical property measuring a material's capability to allow electric current to flow smoothly through its structure.
• Electrical Resistance: [Combined Science / Physics] The structural property of a component or material that opposes the free flow of electric current, measured in Ohms (\(\Omega \)).
• Electrochemical Series: [Chemistry] A tabular arrangement of elements listed in order of their standard electrode potentials, dictating displacement tendencies.
• Electrode: [Chemistry / Combined Science] A solid electrical conductor through which electric current enters or exits an electrolyte solution during electrolysis.
• Electrolysis: [Chemistry / Combined Science] A chemical process that uses direct electrical current to drive a non-spontaneous oxidation-reduction reaction and decompose an electrolyte solution.
• Electrolyte: [Chemistry / Combined Science] A liquid substance, solution, or molten salt compound that contains free-moving ions and conducts electricity through chemical migration.
• Electromagnet: [Combined Science / Physics] A temporary magnet generated by wrapping a conductive wire coil around a soft iron core, operational only while an electric current flows through the coil.
• Electromagnetic Induction: [Combined Science / Physics] The physical generation of an electromotive force or voltage across a conductor when it experiences a shifting magnetic field.
• Electromagnetic Spectrum: [Combined Science / Physics] The complete range of all types of electromagnetic radiation arranged sequentially according to wavelength or frequency.
• Electron: [Chemistry / Combined Science] A stable subatomic particle possessing a negative elementary charge, orbiting the atomic nucleus within discrete energy shells.
• Electron Configuration: [Chemistry / Combined Science] The specific mathematical distribution and arrangement of electrons within an atom's atomic orbitals and energy shells.
• Electronegativity: [Chemistry] A chemical property tracking the relative tendency of an atom to attract a shared pair of bonding electrons within a covalent bond.
• Electrostatics: [Combined Science / Physics] The study of stationary electrical charges, electrical forces, and the electric fields generated around charged objects.
• Element: [Chemistry / Combined Science] A pure chemical substance consisting entirely of one single type of atom that cannot be broken down into simpler substances by chemical means.
• Embryo: [Advanced Biology / Combined Science] An early developmental stage of a multicellular organism resulting from zygote division, prior to full tissue differentiation.
• Empirical Formula: [Chemistry] A chemical formula showing the simplest whole-number ratio of the atoms of each element present in a compound.
• Emulsification: [Advanced Biology / Combined Science] The physical breakdown of massive fat globules into thousands of microscopic droplets, increasing surface area for enzymatic attack.
• Endangered Species: [Combined Science / Biology] Animals or plants at a high risk of going extinct soon due to poaching, habitat loss, or rapid climate changes.
• Endocrine Gland: [Advanced Biology / Combined Science] A ductless gland that manufactures specific hormone molecules and secretes them directly into the bloodstream to regulate distant target organs.
• Endocytosis: [Advanced Biology] A cellular process where a cell membrane invaginates to engulf large external materials or fluids into an intracellular vesicle.
• Endoparasite: [Advanced Biology / Agriculture] A parasitic organism that lives and feeds inside the internal tissues, blood, or organs of a host animal (e.g., tapeworms).
• Endoplasmic Reticulum (ER): [Advanced Biology] A network of continuous membrane-bound tubules and sacs within eukaryotic cells, functional in protein folding (rough ER) and lipid synthesis (smooth ER).
• Endothermic Reaction: [Chemistry / Combined Science] A chemical transformation that absorbs thermal energy from its immediate surroundings, causing the temperature of the system to drop.
• Endotoxin: [Advanced Biology] A toxic lipopolysaccharide compound bound within the outer cell wall of gram-negative bacteria, released only when the cell lyses.
• Endurance: [Physical Education] The physical stamina to keep exercising or playing a sport for a long time without getting overly tired.
• Endosperm: [Advanced Biology] A highly nutritious tissue formed inside the seeds of flowering plants that stores food reserves to sustain the developing plant embryo.
• Energy Level: [Chemistry / Combined Science] Fixed regions of space situated at specific distances from an atomic nucleus where electrons can stably reside.
• Enlightenment: [History] An intellectual and philosophical movement in Europe during the 17th and 18th centuries that emphasized human reason, individual liberty, scientific inquiry, and separation of church and state.
• Enthalpy: [Chemistry] The total heat content or thermal energy stored within a thermodynamic system at constant pressure (\(\Delta H\)).
• Entomology: [Advanced Biology] The scientific study of insects, their anatomical structures, life cycles, and ecological impacts.
• Entrepot Trade: [Commerce] A specialized international trade model where goods are imported from one country and subsequently re-exported to another country without being altered or taxed internally.
• Enzyme: [Combined Science / Biology] A biological macro-molecule catalyst composed of protein that accelerates metabolic chemical reactions inside organisms without being consumed.
• Epidermis: [Advanced Biology / Combined Science] The outermost single protective layer of cells covering leaves, roots, stems, or the external skin of animals.
• Epigeal Germination: [Advanced Biology / Agriculture] A pattern of seed germination where the hypocotyl elongates rapidly, pushing the cotyledons safely above the soil surface.
• Epididymis: [Advanced Biology] A long, coiled tube situated atop the testes where newly manufactured sperm cells are stored and mature.
• Epiglottis: [Advanced Biology / Combined Science] A flexible flap of cartilaginous tissue located at the root of the tongue that folds down during swallowing to block food from entering the trachea.
• Epithelium: [Advanced Biology] A primary animal tissue layer composed of tightly packed cells lining the internal organs, cavities, and external surfaces of the body.
• Equation of State: [Chemistry / Physics] A mathematical formula linking pressure, volume, and absolute temperature for an ideal gas (\(PV = nRT\)).
• Equilibrium: [Chemistry / Physics] A stable state in which opposing forces, mechanical influences, or forward and backward chemical reaction rates are perfectly balanced.
• Equinox: [Combined Science / Geography] The two times each year when the sun crosses the celestial equator, resulting in day and night being of approximately equal length all over the earth.
• Errors of Principle: [Principles of Accounts] A serious bookkeeping error where a transaction is entered into an entirely wrong class of account, violating accounting principles.
• Eschatology: [Family & Religious Studies] The specific branch of theology and religious studies dealing with ultimate human destiny, death, the end times, and final judgment.
• Esterification: [Chemistry] A condensation chemical reaction where an organic alcohol reacts with a carboxylic acid to synthesize an ester and water.
• Estimation: [Mathematics] Rounding numbers to the nearest ten, hundred, or thousand to quickly find an approximate answer.
• Estuary: [Combined Science / Geography] The wide, tidal mouth of a river where its fresh water meets and mixes with the salty water of an ocean.
• Ethical Code: [Family & Religious Studies] A structured system of moral principles, values, and behavioral expectations derived from religious doctrines or traditional frameworks (Unhu / Vumunhu).
• Etiolation: [Advanced Biology / Agriculture] A plant growth condition characterized by long, weak, spindly stems and pale yellow leaves caused by a total lack of sunlight.
• Eukaryote: [Advanced Biology] An organism composed of cells containing a distinct membrane-bound nucleus and specialized intracellular organelles.
• Eutrophication: [Combined Science / Biology] An environmental process where nutrient enrichment from agricultural runoff triggers excessive algae growth in water bodies, depleting dissolved oxygen and killing aquatic life.
• Evaporation: [Combined Science / Physics] The physical surface process where thermal energy converts a liquid substance into an invisible gas or vapor below its boiling point.
• Evolution: [Advanced Biology] The gradual process of genetic change and structural modification occurring in populations across generations driven by natural selection.
• Excretion: [Combined Science / Biology] The biological process of isolating and eliminating toxic metabolic waste products, such as urea and carbon dioxide, from the body fluids of an organism.
• Exocytosis: [Advanced Biology] A cellular transport pathway where membrane-bound secretory vesicles fuse with the cell membrane to discharge contents out of the cell.
• Exoskeleton: [Advanced Biology] A rigid, external structural covering found in arthropods that provides support, anchors muscles, and prevents water loss.
• Exothermic Reaction: [Chemistry / Combined Science] A chemical reaction that releases thermal energy into its surrounding environment, causing a measurable rise in temperature (\(\Delta H < 0\)).
• Expert System: [ICT / Computer Systems] An artificial intelligence software application that mimics the decision-making ability of a human expert within a specific domain.
• Extended Family: [Heritage Studies / Family & Religious Studies] A structural family unit that extends beyond parents and children to encompass aunts, uncles, cousins, grandparents, and in-laws.
• Extinction: [Combined Science / Biology] The total, permanent cessation of a biological species when the final living individual of that population dies

Section E

• Extinction: The permanent cessation of a species' entire population globally, occurring when the final surviving organism of that biological group dies.
• Extinction Coefficient: A chemical and optical measure of how strongly a specific substance or chemical solution absorbs light at a given wavelength per unit concentration.
• Extracellular Digestion: A biological process where digestive enzymes are actively secreted outside of a cell or into a digestive cavity to break down nutrients before absorption.
• Extracellular Matrix: A structural network of extracellular macromolecules, including collagen and glycoproteins, that provides structural and biochemical support to surrounding tissues.
• Extraembryonic Membranes: Specialized protective and metabolic membrane layers—comprising the amnion, chorion, allantois, and yolk sac—that form outside a developing embryo to sustain life.
• Extrapolation: The mathematical or statistical method of estimating an unknown value or trend beyond the known range of observed data points by extending an established functional relationship.
• Extrusion: [Building Technology / Design] A manufacturing process where a material, such as metal or plastic, is pushed through a shaped die to create long, uniform profiles.
• Exudate: A nutrient-rich or waste fluid that has exuded out of its natural containment vessels due to localized inflammation, high hydrostatic pressure, or plant tissue damage.
• Exuviae: The cast-off outer skin, cuticular shell, or exoskeleton left behind by an insect, arachnid, or crustacean after undergoing the process of ecdysis or molting.

Section F

• F1 Generation: The first filial generation of offspring obtained from an experimental cross of distinctly different, homozygous parental organisms.
• F2 Generation: The second filial generation of offspring obtained by interbreeding or self-pollinating individuals belonging to the F1 generation.
• Facilitated Diffusion: A form of passive cellular transport where specific polar molecules or ions cross a biological membrane down a concentration gradient via specialized transport proteins.
• Factor of Safety: The structural ratio of a material's ultimate structural breaking strength to its maximum expected operational working load, used in engineering calculations.
• Fallian Tube / Oviduct: The muscular tube in female mammals lined with ciliated epithelium that transports ova from the ovary to the uterus and serves as the site of fertilization.
• Family: [Syllabus Classification] A structural taxonomic rank in biological classification systems that sits directly below an Order and groups related Genera together.
• Faraday's Constant: A physical constant representing the total electric charge carried by exactly one mole of electrons, valued at approximately 96,485 Coulombs per mole.
• Faraday's Laws of Electrolysis: Two electrochemical rules stating that the mass of a substance altered at an electrode during electrolysis is directly proportional to the electric charge passed, and inversely proportional to its chemical equivalent weight.
• Fascia: A continuous sheet or band of fibrous connective tissue beneath the skin that attaches, stabilizes, encloses, and separates muscles and other internal organs.
• Fascicle: A small localized bundle of muscle fibers, nerves, or plant conductive vessels wrapped tightly within protective structural connective tissue layers.
• Fat: A lipid biomolecule composed of three fatty acid chains chemically bonded to a single glycerol backbone, acting as a high-density metabolic energy store.
• Fatty Acid: A long aliphatic hydrocarbon chain terminating in a carboxylic acid functional group, acting as a core structural building block for complex lipids.
• Fault: A fracture or zone of fractures between two blocks of rock in Earth's crust, along which relative displacement or movement occurs due to tectonic forces.
• Fauna: The collective community of animal species inhabiting a specific geographic region, ecosystem, or historical geological timeframe.
• Feedback Inhibition: A cellular metabolic control mechanism where the accumulation of an end-product acts as an allosteric inhibitor on an enzyme early in the biochemical pathway.
• Fehling's Solution: A chemical reagent containing copper(II) ions used to detect the presence of water-soluble reducing sugars, turning from a deep blue solution to a brick-red precipitate upon heating.
• Feldspar: A widespread group of rock-forming aluminum silicate minerals containing potassium, sodium, or calcium, which forms a major component of Earth's crust.
• Femur: The long bone of the thigh in vertebrate skeletons, articulating with the pelvis at its proximal end and the tibia at its distal end.
• Fermentation: An anaerobic metabolic pathway that breaks down glucose or other organic nutrients to extract chemical energy without using an electron transport chain, yielding ethanol or lactic acid.
• Ferromagnetism: The physical property of certain materials, such as iron, cobalt, and nickel, to exhibit strong magnetic properties and form permanent magnets.
• Fertilization: The biological fusion of haploid male and female gametes to generate a single diploid zygote, initiating the development of a new organism.
• Fibreglass: A reinforced composite material composed of fine woven glass fibers embedded within a liquid plastic resin matrix, used for lightweight, high-strength construction.
• Fibrin: An insoluble, fibrous structural protein formed from fibrinogen during the blood clotting cascade, creating a mesh network that traps blood cells to seal wounds.
• Fibrinogen: A soluble plasma glycoprotein synthesized by the liver that circulates in blood and is converted into structural fibrin during tissue injury.
• Fibula: The outer and thinner of the two long bones in the lower leg of vertebrates, situated parallel to the tibia.
• Field: A physical region in space where an object experiences a non-contact force due to its mass, electric charge, or magnetic orientation.
• Filament: The slender, supportive stalk portion of a plant stamen that bears the pollen-producing anther at its apex.
• Filtration: A physical separation method used to isolate insoluble solid particles from a liquid or gaseous mixture by passing it through a porous barrier.
• Fine Aggregate: Fine sand particles or crushed stone measuring less than 4.75 millimeters in diameter, used in mixing structural mortar, plaster, and concrete.
• First Law of Thermodynamics: A physical law stating that total energy within an isolated thermodynamic system remains completely constant; it can change forms but cannot be created or destroyed.
• Fission: A nuclear reaction where a heavy, unstable atomic nucleus splits into two or more smaller, more stable nuclei, releasing immense kinetic energy and neutrons.
• Fixation: The biological or chemical process of converting a volatile, atmospheric gas into stable, solid organic compounds (e.g., nitrogen fixation by soil bacteria).
• Fixed Action Pattern: An innate, unlearned sequence of highly stereotyped animal behaviors that runs to completion once triggered by a specific sign stimulus.
• Flaccid: A cellular state where a plant cell loses internal turgor pressure due to water moving out via osmosis, causing the plasma membrane to stop pressing against the cell wall.
• Flagellum: A long, hair-like cellular appendage or organelle used by microscopic cells and spermatozoa to generate locomotion or fluid currents.
• Flame Emission Spectroscopy: An analytical chemistry method used to identify and quantify metal ions based on the intensity of specific wavelengths of light emitted when a sample is vaporized in a hot flame.
• Flame Test: A qualitative chemistry procedure where a sample is introduced into a hot flame to identify specific metal ions based on the characteristic color produced.
• Flemish Bond: [Building Technology] A traditional bricklaying pattern consisting of alternating stretchers and headers laid uniformly within every single course along a wall.
• Flexor Muscle: A muscle whose active contraction causes a joint to bend, reducing the physical angle between the two articulating bones.
• Flora: The collective community of plant species naturally growing within a specific geographic region, ecological zone, or historical geological era.
• Flotation: A industrial separation technique where fine mineral particles are separated from waste ore based on differences in surface hydrophobicity using air bubbles in water.
• Flue: A dedicated pipe, duct, or chimney channel built to safely convey exhaust combustion gases and smoke from a furnace or fireplace out of a building.
• Fluid: Any substance that lacks a fixed shape, flows under the application of shear stress, and continuously conforms to the boundaries of its container; includes both liquids and gases.
• Fluid Mosaic Model: The accepted structural model of biological membranes, describing them as a dynamic, fluid bilayer of phospholipids embedded with floating protein molecules.
• Fluorescence: The immediate emission of electromagnetic radiation by a substance that has absorbed light or other radiation of a different, typically shorter, wavelength.
• Flux: The rate of flow of a physical property, fluid volume, or field lines passing perpendicular through a given surface area per unit of time.
• Fly Ash: Fine particulate ash residue carried out of coal-fired power station furnaces by exhaust gases, frequently harvested for use as a pozzolanic additive in concrete manufacturing.
• Focal Point: The exact point on the optical axis where light rays traveling parallel to the axis converge, or appear to diverge from, after passing through a lens or reflecting off a mirror.
• Fodder: Coarse, bulk agricultural crops or dry grasses harvested specifically to feed domesticated livestock animals like cattle and goats.
• Foetus / Fetus: The stage of a developing viviparous vertebrate organism inside the uterus after embryonic structures have organized, showing clear species features.
• Foliation: The structural alignment of platy or elongated mineral grains into parallel layers within a rock, typical of metamorphic rocks exposed to intense directional pressure.
• Follicle: A small, hollow cavity or cellular sac in mammalian anatomy, such as those in the ovary that mature an egg, or those in the skin that generate hair.
• Follicle-Stimulating Hormone (FSH): A gonadotropic glycoprotein hormone synthesized by the anterior pituitary gland that stimulates the maturation of ovarian follicles in females and spermatogenesis in males.
• Food Chain: A linear ecological sequence showing the direct transfer of energy and nutrients as one organism consumes another within an ecosystem.
• Food Web: A complex, interlocking network of multiple food chains that maps out the diverse feeding relationships and energy pathways within an ecological community.
• Footing: [Building Technology] The widened structural base or concrete slab at the lowest point of a foundation wall, designed to distribute structural weight over a broader soil area.
• Force: A vector quantity that represents an influence capable of changing an object's state of rest, uniform motion, direction, or structural shape, measured in Newtons.
• Forensic Science: The systemic application of scientific principles, biochemical testing, and analytical techniques to investigate crimes and provide evidence in legal proceedings.
• Formaldehyde: A colorless, highly pungent gaseous chemical compound (\(HCHO\)), used as a structural precursor for industrial polymers and as a preservative for biological specimens.
• Formwork: [Building Technology] Temporary or permanent structural molds constructed from timber boards, steel sheets, or plastic panels used to hold freshly poured concrete in place until it hardens.
• Fossil: The preserved structural remains, impressions, or traces of an organism that lived in a past geological age, sealed inside sedimentary rock layers.
• Fossil Fuels: High-density hydrocarbon energy sources, primarily coal, oil, and natural gas, formed over millions of years from the buried remains of ancient plants and microorganisms.
• Foucault Current / Eddy Current: Microscopic loops of electric current induced within a solid metallic conductor when it experiences a changing magnetic field, generating internal thermal energy.
• Foundation: [Building Technology] The complete structural base of a building excavated below ground level, designed to receive and safely distribute the total weight of the superstructure into the earth.
• Fractional Crystallization: A chemical refinement process where a mixture of dissolved substances is separated based on differences in solubility by incrementally lowering the temperature of the solution.
• Fractional Distillation: An industrial and laboratory separation technique used to divide a mixture of miscible liquids based on their distinct boiling points using a fractionating column.
• Fracture: [Medical / Materials Science] A partial or complete structural break in a bone, or the clean separation of a solid material into pieces under intense mechanical stress.
• Fragmentation: A form of asexual reproduction or ecological disruption where an organism or habitat breaks into separate pieces, each capable of growing independently.
• Frame Structure: [Building Technology / Engineering] A structural system composed of interconnected beams and columns that forms a rigid skeleton to support a building's loads without relying on heavy load-bearing walls.
• Frasch Process: An industrial extraction method used to harvest deep underground sulfur deposits by pumping superheated water and compressed air down a well to melt and lift liquid sulfur.
• Free On Board (FOB): [Commerce] A standard international shipping term indicating that the seller fulfills their commercial obligation when the goods pass over the ship's rail at the designated port of shipment.
• Free Radical: A highly reactive atom, molecule, or ion that possesses one or more unpaired valence electrons, making it highly unstable chemically.
• Freeze-Thaw Action: A mechanical weathering process where water enters rock fractures, expands as it freezes into ice, exerts internal pressure, and gradually shatters the rock.
• Frequency: The total number of complete wave cycles, oscillations, or physical vibrations that pass a fixed reference point per unit of time, measured in Hertz.
• Friction: A contact force that acts parallel to touching surfaces to oppose the relative sliding, rolling, or motion between them.
• Fructose: A water-soluble six-carbon monosaccharide sugar (\(C_6H_{12}O_6\)) commonly found in fruits and honey, acting as an isomer of glucose.
• Fruit: The mature, seed-bearing ovary structure of a flowering plant developed from a flower following the process of fertilization.
• Fulcrum: The fixed pivot point around which a rigid lever rotates or turns when a force is applied.
• Fullerene: A structural allotrope of carbon where molecules consist of carbon atoms linked into a hollow sphere, ellipsoid, or tube shape (e.g., Buckminsterfullerene).
• Functional Group: A specific spatial arrangement of atoms within an organic molecule that dictates its characteristic chemical reactivity and properties (e.g., \(-OH\), \(-COOH\)).
• Fungicide: A specialized toxic chemical compound or biological agent applied to eradicate or inhibit the growth of fungi and their spores on crops or structures.
• Fungus: A diverse kingdom of heterotrophic, eukaryotic organisms that lack chlorophyll, possess cell walls made of chitin, and absorb nutrients from organic substrates.
• Fuse: A sacrificial electrical safety device containing a thin wire designed to melt and break a circuit when current exceeds a specified limit.
• Fusion: A physical change of state where a solid absorbs thermal energy and transforms into a liquid, or a nuclear process where light atomic nuclei combine to form a heavier nucleus.

Section G

• Gable: [Building Technology] The vertical triangular portion of an exterior wall enclosed between the sloping ridges of a dual-pitch roof.
• Galactose: A six-carbon monosaccharide sugar (\(C_6H_{12}O_6\)) that links with glucose to form lactose, sharing an identical chemical formula but differing structurally.
• Galvanism: The generation of direct electrical current via an electrochemical reaction between different metals separated by an electrolyte solution.
• Galvanizing: The process of applying a protective outer coating of zinc to iron or steel structures to prevent rust through sacrificial protection.
• Galvanometer: A sensitive laboratory instrument used to detect, measure, and determine the directional flow of tiny electric currents within a circuit.
• Gamete: A specialized, haploid reproductive cell—such as a spermatozoon or ovum—that must fuse with another gamete during fertilization to form a diploid zygote.
• Gametogenesis: The biological process of cellular division and maturation by which haploid male and female gametes are generated within gonads.
• Gametophyte: The haploid, gamete-producing phase or multicellular generation in the life cycle of a plant undergoing alternation of generations.
• Gamma Radiation: High-frequency, short-wavelength electromagnetic radiation emitted spontaneously from the unstable nucleus of a decaying radioactive atom.
• Ganglion: A localized cluster of nerve cell bodies or neuronal structures situated outside the central nervous system within the peripheral nervous system.
• Gangue: The commercially worthless rock, sand, clay, or mineral material surrounding a valuable ore deposit inside a mine.
• Gas: A fundamental state of matter where particles possess high kinetic energy and weak intermolecular forces, expanding to fill the volume of any container.
• Gastric Juice: A highly acidic digestive fluid secreted by glands in the stomach wall, containing hydrochloric acid and pepsin to initiate protein breakdown.
• Gastrin: A peptide hormone secreted by stomach cells that stimulates gastric glands to release hydrochloric acid during digestion.
• Gate Valve: [Building Technology / Plumbing] A fluid control valve that opens by lifting a circular or rectangular gate out of the path of a flowing fluid, offering minimal resistance when fully open.
• Gateway: [ICT] A network hardware device or software node that connects two distinct networks utilizing different communication protocols.
• Gauging: [Building Technology] The precise measuring of separate component materials—such as cement, sand, and lime—in specific ratios before mixing mortar or concrete.
• Gay-Lussac's Law: A fundamental gas law stating that the pressure of a fixed mass of gas is directly proportional to its absolute temperature, provided volume remains constant.
• Geiger-Müller Counter: A laboratory instrument used to detect and measure ionizing radiation by tracking electrical pulses generated as gas inside a tube is ionized.
• Gel: A colloidal system where a porous network of solid particles spans and traps a liquid medium, creating a semi-solid material.
• Gene: A specific unit of heredity made up of a DNA sequence that carries instructions for building proteins and passing on traits.
• Gene Mutation: A permanent structural alteration occurring in a single gene's nucleotide sequence, modifying the corresponding protein structure.
• Gene Pool: The complete collection of all alleles and genetic variations present within an interbreeding population of a specific species.
• General Ledger: [Principles of Accounts] The master accounting record containing a comprehensive, adjusted collection of all financial accounts used to compile a trial balance.
• Genetic Code: The universal set of three-nucleotide codon rules by which information encoded in genetic DNA or mRNA is translated into amino acids.
• Genetic Drift: Random fluctuations in allele frequencies within a small population across generations due to chance events rather than natural selection.
• Genetic Engineering: The direct laboratory manipulation of an organism's genome using biotechnology to introduce desirable traits.
• Genetics: The branch of biology dedicated to studying heredity, gene variations, and how traits are passed down through generations.
• Genome: The complete, entire blueprint of genetic material and hereditary instructions packed inside a single cell of an organism.
• Genotype: The complete, underlying genetic constitution of an organism regarding its specific alleles, whether those traits are physically expressed or not.
• Genus: A structural taxonomic rank in biological classification systems that sits directly below a Family and groups related species together.
• Geographical Isolation: An evolutionary mechanism where a physical geographic barrier divides a population, preventing interbreeding and driving speciation.
• Geography: The study of Earth's physical features, atmosphere, human populations, resources, and land use patterns.
• Geological Time Scale: A system of chronological dating that relates geological strata layers to time, used by scientists to map Earth's history.
• Geomorphology: The scientific study of the origin, development, characteristics, and dynamics of landforms and physical topographic features on Earth.
• Geostationary Orbit: A circular orbit directly above Earth's equator where a satellite travels at the exact rotational speed of Earth, remaining fixed over one spot.
• Geothermal Energy: Sustainable thermal energy harvested from heat generated deep within Earth's mantle and crust.
• Geotropism / Gravitropism: The growth movement of a plant in response to the pulling force of gravity, where roots grow downward and shoots grow upward.
• Gestation Period: The precise length of time a viviparous animal embryo or fetus develops inside the uterus from fertilization until birth.
• Gibberellin: A plant hormone group that regulates growth processes, including stem elongation, seed germination, and flowering.
• Giga-: A metric prefix denoting a factor of one billion (\(10^{9}\)), symbolized by the letter G.
• Gimlet: A small hand tool featuring a screw point and a cross handle, used to bore small holes into timber elements without splitting the wood.
• Girdle: A structural skeletal arch in vertebrates, such as the pectoral or pelvic girdle, that anchors limbs to the central axial skeleton.
• Gizzard: A highly muscular organ in the digestive tract of birds, reptiles, and earthworms that grinds down food using swallowed stones or grit.
• Glacier: A massive, slow-moving river of ice formed by the accumulation and compaction of snow over centuries.
• Gland: An organ or cell cluster specialized in manufacturing and secreting specific chemical substances, such as enzymes or hormones.
• Glazing: [Building Technology] The physical process of installing glass panels into window frames, doors, or partitions within a structure.
• Glenoid Cavity: The shallow depression on the scapula bone that articulates with the head of the humerus to form the shoulder joint.
• Global Positioning System (GPS): A satellite-based navigation network used to determine the exact geographical location, speed, and time of a receiver on Earth.
• Global Warming: The long-term rise in Earth's temperature caused by greenhouse gases trapping heat in the atmosphere.
• Glomerular Filtrate: The fluid filtered out of blood capillaries in the glomerulus into Bowman's capsule, containing water, glucose, salts, and urea.
• Glomerulus: A compact network of high-pressure blood capillaries located inside Bowman's capsule that acts as the initial site of blood filtration in the kidney.
• Glucagon: A peptide hormone secreted by the pancreas that stimulates the liver to break down stored glycogen into glucose, raising blood sugar levels.
• Glucoregulation: The homeostatic mechanism by which an organism maintains a stable concentration of glucose within its bloodstream.
• Glucose: A simple sugar molecule that serves as an essential source of metabolic energy for living organisms during cellular respiration.
• Glycerol: A sweet, colorless, viscous three-carbon alcohol molecule (\(C_{3}H_{8}O_{3}\)) that acts as the structural structural foundation for triglycerides and lipids.
• Glycogen: A highly branched polysaccharide that serves as the primary multi-stage storage form of glucose in animals, stored in liver and muscle tissues.
• Glycogenesis: The biochemical process of converting glucose into glycogen for metabolic storage when blood sugar levels are elevated.
• Glycogenolysis: The biochemical breakdown of stored glycogen into glucose molecules to raise blood sugar levels during fasting or exercise.
