• Interstitial Cells: Specialized cells situated in the tissue spaces between seminiferous tubules in the testes that secrete testosterone.
• Intestinal Juice / Succus Entericus: A clear digestive enzyme mixture secreted by glands in the wall of the small intestine to complete nutrient digestion.
• Intramuscular: A medical term indicating that a fluid or vaccine is injected directly into a deep muscle layer.
• Intraspecific Competition: An ecological interaction occurring when individuals belonging to the exact same species compete for limited resources.
• Intravenous: A medical term indicating that a fluid, drug, or clinical treatment is introduced directly into a vein.
• Intrusive Igneous Rock: Igneous rock formed when molten magma cools and solidifies slowly deep inside Earth's crust, forming large crystals (e.g., granite).
• Inulin: A naturally occurring storage polysaccharide carbohydrate isolated from many plant roots, used in food science and diagnostics.
• Inversion: A meteorological state where a layer of warm air traps cooler air below it near the ground, reversing the standard temperature decline with altitude.
• Invertebrate: An overarching biological classification grouping together all animal species that lack a internal vertebral column or backbone.
• Interview Guide: A listed sequence of conversational questions prepared beforehand to ask an elder, local farmer, or community expert.
• Inverted Microscope: A laboratory microscope configuration where the light source and condenser are placed above the stage while the objectives sit below.
• Involucre: A whorl or ring of protective bracts situated directly beneath a compressed flower cluster or inflorescence.
• Involuntary Muscle: Smooth or cardiac muscle tissue that contracts automatically under the direction of the autonomic nervous system without conscious control.
• Involuntary Response: An automatic, immediate biological reaction to an external stimulus executed without conscious thought (e.g., a reflex arc).
• Involute: A geometric curve traced out by the free end of a taut string as it is unwound from a fixed guide cylinder.
• Iodine: A shiny, blue-black non-metallic element (\(I\)) belonging to the halogen family, used as a laboratory stain and to test for starch.
• Iodine Test: A biochemical test where an iodine solution is added to a sample, turning from brown to a deep blue-black color if starch is present.
• Ion: An atom or group of atoms that has gained or lost one or more valence electrons, acquiring a net electrical charge.
• Ion Exchange: A chemical water purification process where unwanted hard ions (like calcium) are swapped for harmless ions (like sodium) inside a resin bed.
• Ionic Bond: An electrostatic binding force formed through the transfer of electrons from a metallic atom to a non-metallic atom, yielding oppositely charged ions.
• Ionic Product of Water: The equilibrium constant (\(K_{w}\)) representing the degree of self-ionization of pure water, valued at approximately \(1.0 \times 10^{-14}\) at room temperature.
• Ionization: The energetic process by which neutral atoms or molecules gain or lose electrons, converting them into charged ions.
• Ionization Energy: The minimum energy required to remove the most loosely bound electron from a isolated gaseous atom in its ground state.
• Ionosphere: A heavily ionized layer of Earth's upper atmosphere that reflects high-frequency radio waves back to Earth, enabling long-distance communication.
• Iridocyte: Specialized reflective skin cells found in many lower vertebrates that contain guanine crystals to produce iridescent coloring.
• Iris: A circular, colored muscular diaphragm in the eye that contracts and dilates to regulate the amount of light entering the pupil.
• Iron: A highly abundant, magnetic transition metal element (\(Fe\)) with an atomic number of 26, widely processed to manufacture steel alloys.
• Iron Ore: Rocks and mineral deposits from which metallic iron can be commercially extracted through chemical reduction inside a blast furnace.
• Irradiation: The process of exposing an object, food crop, or living tissue to controlled doses of ionizing radiation to sterilize it or destroy pathogens.
• Irreversible Reaction: A chemical reaction that proceeds in one direction to completion, where products cannot be converted back into the original reactants.
• Irrigation: Supplying water to dry crop fields using man-made pipes, sprinklers, or channels rather than relying only on rainfall.
• Isatide: A complex nitrogenous organic chemical crystalline compound derived during the structural oxidation of indigo dyes.
• Isentropic Process: A theoretical thermodynamic process occurring at a completely constant entropy value, remaining fully reversible and adiabatic.
• Isitshikitsha: A traditional rhythmic Ndebele dance performed at celebrations, characterized by graceful upper body movements and footwork.
• Islet of Langerhans: Tiny patches of endocrine tissue scattered across the pancreas that contain beta and alpha cells to secrete insulin and glucagon.
• Isoclinic Line: An imaginary line mapped across Earth's surface connecting geographical points that share an identical magnetic dip angle.
• Isoelectronic: A chemical state where different atoms, molecules, or ions possess an identical number of electrons and spatial configuration.
• Isogamy: A form of sexual reproduction involving the fusion of gametes that are completely identical in size, shape, and structure.
• Isolate: The laboratory extraction and separation of a pure strain of an organism or a specific chemical compound from a mixed sample.
• Isomer: Chemical compounds that share an identical molecular formula but possess different structural arrangements of atoms, modifying their properties.
• Isomerism: The occurrence of two or more chemical chemical compounds sharing identical molecular formulas but differing in spatial structure.
• Isomorphism: A crystalline state where completely different chemical substances display identical crystal structures and geometries.
• Isoprene: A volatile, liquid hydrocarbon compound (\(C_{5}H_{8}\)) acting as the core chemical structural monomer for natural rubber and terpene molecules.
• Isothermal Process: A thermodynamic process or change occurring within a system where the absolute temperature remains completely constant throughout.
• Isotonic Solution: A solution that features an identical concentration of solute particles compared to another solution, resulting in zero net movement of water via osmosis.
• Isotope: Nuclei of the same chemical element that possess identical atomic numbers but different mass numbers due to a variation in neutron count.
• Isotopic Dating: A geochronological method tracking the radioactive decay of isotopes to calculate the absolute age of rocks, fossils, or ancient artifacts.
• Isotropic: A materials science property indicating that a material displays completely identical physical and structural properties in all directional axes.


Section J

• January Disease: [Combined Science / Agriculture] A deadly tick-borne livestock disease, scientifically known as Theileriosis, caused by the parasite Theileria parva and transmitted by the brown ear tick, leading to high cattle mortality during the rainy season.
• Jarrah: [Building Technology] A dense, heavy, and highly durable hardwood derived from the Australian eucalyptus tree (Eucalyptus marginata), heavily resistant to rot and termites, used for structural framing, bridges, and sleepers.
• Jaw: [Advanced Biology] Either of the two structural skeletal configurations (maxilla and mandible) forming the framework of the mouth in most vertebrates, holding teeth and enabling chewing.
• Jejunum: [Advanced Biology] The long, highly vascularized middle section of the mammalian small intestine, situated between the duodenum and the ileum, specialized for the absorption of carbohydrates and proteins via enterocytes.
• Jellyfish: [Advanced Biology] A free-swimming marine coelenterate belonging to the phylum Cnidaria, characterized by a gelatinous medusa-shaped body, radial symmetry, and trailing tentacles equipped with stinging cells (nematocysts).
