• Jet Engine: [Physics / Applied Mechanics] A reaction engine that discharges a fast-moving jet of gas backward to generate propulsion based on Newton's third law of motion.
• Jig: [Building Technology / Workshop Practice] A specialized custom-built tool or mechanical device used to hold work in position and guide a cutting tool accurately during manufacturing.
• Joist: [Building Technology] Parallel horizontal timber, steel, or concrete beams used to span open areas to support floors, ceilings, or flat roof decks.
• Joint: [Advanced Biology] The physical point, junction, or articulation where two or more bones meet within a vertebrate skeletal system, classified structurally as fibrous, cartilaginous, or synovial.
• Joint-Stock Company: [Commerce] A large incorporated commercial enterprise owned collectively by shareholders, possessing a distinct legal identity separate from its owners and limited liability.
• Joule: [Physics] The standard SI unit used to measure energy, work done, or heat; equivalent to the work performed by a force of one Newton moving an object through a distance of one meter parallel to the force.
• Joule Heating: [Physics] The physical process where the passage of an electric current through a conductor releases thermal energy due to electrical resistance colliding with moving charge carriers.
• Journal Proper: [Principles of Accounts] A primary book of original entry used to chronologically record uncommon financial transactions that cannot fit into specialized sub-journals, such as opening entries and asset corrections.
• Judiciary System: [Heritage Studies / Civics] The legal branch of a state composed of courts running from traditional primary village courts up to the Supreme Court, responsible for interpreting statutory laws and delivering justice.
• Junction Diode: [Physics] A two-terminal semiconductor device formed by joining a p-type material to an n-type material, allowing electric current to flow easily in one direction (forward bias) while blocking it in the reverse.
• Juvenile Hormone: [Advanced Biology] A specialized insect hormone secreted by the corpora allata that maintains larval or nymphal characteristics, preventing premature metamorphosis during molting cycles.

Section K

• K-Selection: [Advanced Biology] An evolutionary reproductive strategy favored in stable environments where organisms invest heavily in a small number of offspring with high survival rates and long lifespans.
• Karyotype: [Advanced Biology] The complete, systematically organized visual profile showing the standard number, sizes, shapes, and configurations of chromosomes found within a single cell nucleus of an organism.
• Kelvin: [Physics / Chemistry] The baseline SI unit of thermodynamic temperature, where an absolute temperature scale operates with its zero point sitting at absolute zero (-273.15°C).
• Keratin: [Advanced Biology] A tough, insoluble fibrous structural protein that forms the primary chemical constituent of hair, nails, claws, hooves, feathers, and the outer epidermal skin layers of vertebrates.
• Kerosine / Kerosene: [Combined Science / Chemistry] A flammable hydrocarbon liquid mixture obtained via the fractional distillation of petroleum within the temperature range of 150°C to 275°C, processed for jet fuel and heating.
• Ketone: [Chemistry] An organic chemical compound containing a carbonyl functional group (C=O) chemically bonded between two separate carbon atoms within a hydrocarbon chain.
• Keying: [Building Technology] The physical process of raking out fresh mortar joints or systematically roughening a smooth masonry surface to provide a strong mechanical bond for plaster or render coats.
• Kidney: [Advanced Biology / Combined Science] An essential bean-shaped homeostatic organ in vertebrates responsible for filtering metabolic wastes from blood plasma, regulating water balance, and generating urine.
• Kiln: [Building Technology / Ceramics] An insulated thermal chamber or high-temperature furnace built to dry timber elements, bake bricks, or fire molded clay pottery to harden them permanently.
• Kilogram: [Physics] The baseline SI unit used to measure mass, defined by fixing the numerical value of the Planck constant h.
• Kinematic Viscosity: [Physics] A fluid mechanics measure calculated by dividing a fluid's dynamic viscosity by its density, indicating its inherent resistance to flow under gravity.
• Kinematics: [Physics] The branch of classical mechanics focused on describing the motion of points, objects, and bodies over time without considering the specific forces that cause the motion.
• Kinetic Energy: [Physics / Combined Science] The quantitative mechanical energy a physical body possesses by virtue of its mass and active velocity or motion, calculated as \(\frac{1}{2}mv^2\).
• Kinetic Theory of Gases: [Chemistry / Physics] A molecular model stating that gases consist of countless microscopic particles in rapid, random, continuous motion, with gas pressure resulting from elastic collisions against container walls.
• Kingdom: [Advanced Biology] A supreme taxonomic rank in biological classification systems that sits directly below a Domain and divides broad categories of life, such as Plants, Animals, and Fungi.
• Knapsack Sprayer: [Combined Science / Agriculture] A backpack-mounted manual or battery-powered pumping device used by farmers to apply liquid pesticides, herbicides, or foliar fertilizers uniformly over crops.
• Krebs Cycle / Citric Acid Cycle: [Advanced Biology] A multi-stage sequence of aerobic biochemical reactions occurring inside the mitochondrial matrix that oxidizes acetyl-CoA into carbon dioxide, generating ATP, NADH, and FADH₂.
• Kurova Guva / Umbuyiso: [Family & Religious Studies] A traditional post-burial ritual performed approximately a year after death to bring back the spirit of a deceased family member into the household fold as a protective ancestor.
• Kwashiorkor: [Combined Science / Advanced Biology] A severe form of malnutrition caused by a critical deficiency of dietary protein, characterized by edema (swollen belly and limbs), thinning reddish hair, and skin lesions.

Section L

• Lachrymal Gland / Lacrimal Gland: [Advanced Biology] An exocrine gland located above the outer corner of each eye that secretes an aqueous fluid (tears) to lubricate, cleanse, and protect the corneal surface.
