• Lactic Acid: [Combined Science / Chemistry] An organic acid (C₃H₆O₃) produced as an end-product of anaerobic respiration in mammalian muscle tissues or during bacterial fermentation in dairy products.
• Lactose: [Chemistry / Combined Science] A reducing disaccharide sugar (C₁₂H₂₂O₁₁) found in milk, composed of glucose and galactose molecules chemically linked via a glycosidic bond.
• Lake Kariba: [Combined Science / Geography] The world's largest man-made lake by volume, situated on the Zambezi River between Zimbabwe and Zambia, acting as a primary source of regional hydroelectric power and aquaculture.
• Lamination: [Building Technology / Materials Science] The structural manufacturing process of bonding multiple thin layers of a material together with adhesives to maximize overall strength, stability, or resistance.
• Lancaster House Agreement: [Heritage Studies / History] The historic constitutional settlement signed in London in December 1979 that legally terminated the Rhodesian Bush War, established a transitional British authority, and paved the way for Zimbabwe's independent elections in 1980.
• Landlocked Country: [Combined Science / Geography] A country surrounded entirely by land with no direct physical access to an ocean, sharing borders with multiple neighbors (e.g., Zimbabwe bordering Zambia, Mozambique, South Africa, and Botswana).
• Landslide: [Geography / Combined Science] A rapid downward mass wasting event where a large volume of loose soil, rock, and debris moves down a slope driven by gravity, often triggered by heavy rain.
• Lap Joint: [Building Technology / Woodworking] A simple joint configuration manufactured by overlapping two separate structural elements and fastening them together.
• Latent Heat: [Physics / Chemistry] The quantity of thermal energy absorbed or released by a substance during a constant-temperature change of physical state (e.g., melting or boiling).
• Lateral Inversion: [Physics] An optical phenomenon where the left and right sides of an object are reversed when viewed in a plane mirror.
• Latex: [Combined Science / Advanced Biology] A milky, complex organic emulsion fluid secreted by specialized plant cells (laticifers) upon tissue injury, containing proteins, resins, and rubber polymers used in industry.
• Lattice: [Chemistry / Physics] A highly ordered, repeating three-dimensional geometric arrangement of atoms, ions, or molecules within a crystalline solid.
• Law of Conservation of Energy: [Physics / Combined Science] A fundamental physical rule stating that total energy within an isolated thermodynamic system remains completely constant; it can change forms but cannot be created or destroyed.
• Law of Conservation of Mass: [Chemistry] A chemical principle stating that the total mass of substances present at the conclusion of a chemical reaction is identical to the mass of the starting reactants.
• Layer: [Combined Science / Agriculture] A mature female domestic fowl variety managed primarily for long-term, high-volume egg production for markets.
• Leaching: [Combined Science / Geography] The physical and chemical process where soluble mineral ions and nutrients are dissolved and washed down through lower soil horizons by rainwater.
• Lead: [Chemistry] A dense, soft, highly malleable, and toxic post-transition metal element (Pb) with an atomic number of 82, used in batteries and radiation shielding.
• League of Nations: [Heritage Studies / History] An international diplomatic organization founded in 1920 after World War I to maintain global peace through collective security, later replaced by the United Nations.
• Ledger Account: [Principles of Accounts] A T-shaped visual layout tracking the dynamic increases and decreases of a specific financial item using debit and credit columns.
• Legume: [Combined Science / Agriculture] A family of plants (Fabaceae) that harbor nitrogen-fixing bacteria (Rhizobium) within their root nodules, converting atmospheric nitrogen into soil nutrients.
• Lens: [Physics] A transparent optical component manufactured from glass or plastic with curved surfaces that refracts light rays to converge or diverge them.
• Lenz's Law: [Physics] An electromagnetic principle stating that the direction of an induced electric current always generates a magnetic field that opposes the change in magnetic flux that created it.
• Leukocyte: [Advanced Biology] A white blood cell responsible for defending the body against pathogens, viruses, and cellular infections within the human immune system.
• Lever: [Physics / Combined Science] A simple machine consisting of a rigid beam or bar that rotates around a fixed pivot point (fulcrum) to multiply mechanical force.
• Leyden Jar: [Physics] An early prototype electrical component consisting of a glass jar lined inside and out with metal foil, used to store static electricity.
• Liability: [Principles of Accounts] A legal financial obligation or debt owed by a business enterprise to outside individuals or organizations, settled over time through economic outflows.
• Liberation War: [Heritage Studies] The historic armed struggles (First and Second Chimurenga/Umvukela) fought by nationalist movements to overturn colonial minority rule and achieve national sovereignty.
• Ligament: [Advanced Biology] A tough, flexible band of fibrous dense regular connective tissue that bridges bone to bone across a synovial joint, stabilizing the skeletal architecture.
