• Methane: [Chemistry / Combined Science] A colorless, odorless, flammable gas (CH₄), acting as the simplest alkane hydrocarbon and a primary constituent of natural gas and biogas.
• Methanol: [Chemistry] A toxic, volatile, colorless alcohol liquid (CH₃OH) used as an industrial solvent, fuel, and chemical feedstock.
• Methyl Group: [Chemistry] A hydrophobic alkyl functional group consisting of a single carbon atom bonded to three hydrogen atoms (-CH₃).
• Mica: [Geography / Chemistry] A group of silicate minerals characterized by a perfect basal cleavage, allowing them to split into thin, transparent, heat-resistant sheets.
• Micro-nutrient: [Advanced Biology / Agriculture] Essential chemical elements or trace minerals required by living organisms in small amounts for biochemical functions (e.g., Iron, Zinc, Boron).
• Microbiology: [Advanced Biology] The branch of biology dedicated to studying microscopic organisms, such as bacteria, viruses, archaea, fungi, and protozoa.
• Microorganism / Microbe: [Combined Science / Advanced Biology] A microscopic living organism—such as a bacterium, microscopic fungus, or protozoan—that cannot be seen by the naked eye.
• Microscope: [Combined Science / Advanced Biology] A laboratory optical instrument utilizing lenses to produce magnified images of small objects or cells.
• Microvilli: [Advanced Biology] Microscopic, finger-like projections extending from the apical membrane of epithelial cells, increasing surface area for absorption (e.g., inside the ileum).
• Middleveld: [Combined Science / Geography] The intermediate plateau regions flanking both sides of the Highveld in Zimbabwe, sitting at an altitude between 900 and 1,200 meters above sea level.
• Mid-Ocean Ridge: [Geography] An underwater mountain range system formed by plate tectonics along a divergent boundary, where seafloor spreading occurs.
• Migration: [Geography / Advanced Biology] The seasonal or permanent physical movement of human populations, animals, or birds from one geographic location to another.
• Mineral: [Chemistry / Geography] A naturally occurring, inorganic solid substance possessing a definite chemical composition and an ordered internal crystalline structure.
• Mineral Deficiency: [Combined Science / Advanced Biology] A health condition occurring when an organism lacks an adequate supply of an essential mineral element, disrupting physiological functions.
• Mitochondrion: [Advanced Biology] A double-membrane-bound cellular organelle responsible for executing aerobic cellular respiration to generate ATP, acting as the powerhouse of the cell.
• Mitosis: [Advanced Biology] A process of nuclear cell division that yields two genetically identical diploid daughter cells from a single parent cell, ensuring growth and tissue repair.
• Mixture: [Chemistry / Combined Science] A physical combination of two or more substances which are mixed together but not chemically bonded, retaining their individual properties.
• Mode: [Advanced Mathematics] The specific value, number, or category that appears with the highest physical frequency within a collected data set.
• Modem: [ICT] A hardware device (Modulator-Demodulator) that converts digital data from a computer into analog signals for transmission over telephone or cable lines, and vice versa.
• Molarity: [Chemistry] A unit of chemical concentration defined as the number of moles of a specific solute dissolved per liter of liquid solution (mol/dm³).
• Mole: [Chemistry] The standard SI unit for amount of substance, containing exactly 6.022 × 10²³ elementary entities (Avogadro's number).
• Molecular Formula: [Chemistry] A chemical formula that states the exact number and types of atoms present in a single molecule of a compound.
• Molecule: [Chemistry / Combined Science] A neutral group of two or more chemically bonded atoms forming the smallest fundamental unit of a chemical compound.
• Molting / Ecdysis: [Advanced Biology] The biological process where an arthropod or reptile sheds its old exoskeleton or outer skin layer to allow for physical growth.
• Molybdenum: [Chemistry / Advanced Biology] A transition metal element (Mo) with an atomic number of 42, acting as an essential cofactor for plant enzymes involved in nitrogen fixation.
• Moment of a Force: [Physics] A quantitative measure of the turning or rotational effect of a force applied around a fixed pivot or fulcrum point, calculated as force multiplied by perpendicular distance.
• Momentum: [Physics] A vector quantity that measures the quantity of motion an object possesses, calculated as the product of its mass and velocity (p=mv).
• Monochromatic Light: [Physics] Light energy consisting of exactly one single wavelength or color, exhibiting no dispersion when passed through a prism.
• Monocotyledon: [Advanced Biology] A group of flowering plants whose seeds contain exactly one embryonic leaf (cotyledon), characterized by parallel leaf veins and fibrous roots.
• Monohybrid Cross: [Advanced Biology] An experimental genetic cross tracking the inheritance pattern of exactly one single pair of contrasting traits or alleles from parents.
• Monosaccharide: [Chemistry / Advanced Biology] The simplest class of carbohydrate sugars that cannot be hydrolyzed into smaller units, acting as monomers (e.g., glucose, fructose).
• Monotheism: [Family & Religious Studies] The doctrinal religious belief in the existence of only one supreme, omnipotent, and omnipresent creator God.
• Monitor: [ICT] The electronic display screen that shows visual text, images, and user interfaces generated by a computer system.
• Monsoon: [Geography] A seasonal reversing wind pattern accompanied by corresponding changes in atmospheric precipitation, dominant in specific tropical regions.
• Mortar: [Building Technology] A workable paste manufactured by mixing specific ratios of cement, lime, sand, and water, used to bind bricks together and seal joints.
• Mosi-oa-Tunya: [Combined Science / Geography] The indigenous name for Victoria Falls, meaning "The Smoke That Thunders," situated on the Zambezi River.
• Motor Neuron: [Advanced Biology] A specialized neuron that transmits electrical impulses away from the central nervous system to effectors (muscles or glands) to trigger a response.
• Mouse: [ICT] A hand-held hardware pointing device used to move the cursor, select objects, and click on items on a computer display screen.
• Mouth: [Advanced Biology] The anatomical opening through which an animal takes in food and liquids, acting as the initial site of ingestion and mechanical digestion.
• Mucus: [Advanced Biology] A slippery, viscous fluid secreted by mucous membranes and goblet cells to lubricate, moisten, and trap dust or pathogens within body tracts.
• Mulching: [Combined Science / Agriculture] The practice of covering topsoil with organic material like dried grass, leaves, or crop residues to suppress weeds, reduce erosion, and retain soil moisture.
• Multiple: [Advanced Mathematics] The product result obtained by multiplying a given whole number by any other whole number.
• Multiplication: [Advanced Mathematics] The mathematical operation of scaling one number by another, representing repeated addition.
• Municipality: [Heritage Studies / Civics] An urban local government council tasked with delivering clean water, waste disposal, roads, and housing in towns and cities.
• Muscle: [Advanced Biology] A specialized contractile tissue in animals responsible for generating locomotion, mechanical force, and internal movement, divided into skeletal, smooth, and cardiac types.
• Mutation: [Advanced Biology] A permanent, structural alteration occurring in the nucleotide sequence of the genomic DNA of an organism, potentially creating new alleles.
• Mutualism: [Advanced Biology] A symbiotic ecological interaction between two different species where both organisms obtain clear biological benefits.
• Mycelium: [Advanced Biology] The vegetative, thread-like network of branching hyphae that forms the primary body structure of a fungus.
• Mycology: [Advanced Biology] The scientific branch of biology dedicated to studying the characteristics, genetics, and ecology of fungi.
• Myopia / Short-sightedness: [Physics / Combined Science] A vision defect where light rays focus in front of the retina instead of on it, making distant objects look blurry while close objects are clear.
• Myosin: [Advanced Biology] A fibrous structural motor protein that interacts with actin filaments to drive muscle contraction within sarcomeres.
• Mythology: [Family & Religious Studies] A collection of sacred narratives, traditional stories, or allegories accounting for the early origins of the cosmos, humanity, and spiritual dynamics.

Section N

• Naphthalene: [Chemistry] A volatile, white crystalline aromatic hydrocarbon compound (C₁₀H₈) derived from coal tar, possessing a strong odor, used in mothballs and industrial synthesis.
• National Anthem: [Heritage Studies] The patriotic state song titled "Simudzai Mureza wedu weZimbabwe" (Blessed be the Land of Zimbabwe), researched for its historical, civic, and cultural values.
• National Flag: [Heritage Studies] The supreme civic banner of Zimbabwe containing seven horizontal stripes (Green, Yellow, Red, Black) and a white triangle housing the Zimbabwe Bird and a red star.
• Nationalism: [Heritage Studies / History] An ideology and political movement centered on the shared identity, culture, and land rights of a specific group of people, driving their struggle for sovereignty and independence.
• Natural Gas: [Combined Science / Chemistry] A high-density fossil fuel gaseous hydrocarbon mixture located underground, composed primarily of methane.
• Natural Region I: [Combined Science / Geography] High-altitude, mountainous areas in Zimbabwe receiving very high, reliable rainfall (over 1000mm), suited for forestry, tea, coffee, and intensive fruit farming.
• Natural Region II: [Combined Science / Geography] Intensive farming regions located on the high plateau receiving reliable rainfall (750-1000mm), suited for intensive crop cultivation and livestock production.
• Natural Region III: [Combined Science / Geography] Semi-intensive farming regions receiving moderate rainfall (650-750mm) prone to mid-season dry spells, suited for livestock and drought-resistant cash crops.
• Natural Region IV: [Combined Science / Geography] Semi-arid regions receiving low, erratic rainfall (450-650mm), highly prone to drought, suited for extensive livestock ranching and wildlife management.
• Natural Region V: [Combined Science / Geography] Arid, low-lying regions receiving very low, erratic rainfall (below 450mm) and experiencing high temperatures, suited only for extensive cattle ranching and wildlife reserves.
• Natural Selection: [Advanced Biology] The primary evolutionary mechanism proposed by Charles Darwin where organisms better adapted to their environment tend to survive and reproduce more successfully, passing on their traits.
• Neandertal / Neanderthal: [Advanced Biology / History] An extinct species or subspecies of archaic humans (Homo neanderthalensis) who lived in Eurasia until approximately 40,000 years ago, frequently studied in human evolution.
• Neap Tide: [Physics / Geography] A tide of minimum range occurring twice a month when the moon is in its first or third quarter phases, resulting in the gravitational pulls of the sun and moon working at right angles.
• Negative Feedback: [Advanced Biology] A primary homeostatic control mechanism where a change in a physiological variable triggers a response that counteracts and reverses the initial shift to restore balance.
• Nekton: [Advanced Biology] The collective community of active, free-swimming aquatic organisms—such as fish, whales, and squid—capable of moving independently of water currents.
• Nematocyst: [Advanced Biology] A specialized microscopic stinging cell organelle located within cnidocytes of jellyfish and hydras, used to capture prey and defend against predators.
• Nematode: [Advanced Biology] A phylum of unsegmented, cylindrical roundworms (Nematoda) inhabiting soil and water, containing many parasitic species affecting crops and livestock.
• Neocolonialism: [Heritage Studies / Civics] The geopolitical practice of using economic globalization, international trade pressures, corporate investments, and cultural dominance to influence a developing nation instead of direct military control.
• Neon: [Chemistry] A colorless, odorless, exceptionally unreactive noble gas element (Ne) with an atomic number of 10, used in luminous glow signs.
• Nephridiopore: [Advanced Biology] The microscopic external opening or excretory pore through which invertebrates like earthworms discharge metabolic wastes from nephridia.
• Nephridium: [Advanced Biology] A tubular excretory organ found in many invertebrates, such as annelids and mollusks, responsible for removing metabolic wastes and regulating fluid balance.
• Nephron: [Advanced Biology] The microscopic functional filtration unit located within the kidney cortex and medulla, responsible for filtering blood plasma, reabsorbing nutrients, and generating urine.
• Nerve: [Advanced Biology] A cord-like bundle of neuronal axons wrapped in protective connective tissue sheaths that transmits electrical impulses between the central nervous system and body organs.
• Nervous System: [Advanced Biology / Combined Science] The complex regulatory network of cells, tissues, and organs—comprising the brain, spinal cord, and peripheral nerves—that coordinates immediate biological responses to stimuli.
• Net Profit: [Principles of Accounts] The absolute financial surplus remaining after all operating, administrative, and financial expenses have been deducted from gross profit.
• Network: [ICT] A collection of interconnected computers, servers, and peripheral devices linked via communication channels to share data, files, and resources.
• Neural Pathway: [Advanced Biology] The physical route formed by interconnected neurons along which electrical nerve impulses travel through the nervous system.
• Neurolemma / Neurilemma: [Advanced Biology] The delicate outermost nucleated cytoplasmic layer of Schwann cells that encloses the myelin sheath of peripheral nerve fibers, aiding in nerve regeneration.
• Neuromuscular Junction: [Advanced Biology] The specialized chemical synapse formed between the terminal end of a motor neuron and the motor endplate of a muscle fiber, initiating contraction.
• Neuron: [Advanced Biology] A highly specialized cell within the nervous system responsible for generating, receiving, and transmitting electrical nerve impulses, divided into sensory, motor, and relay types.
• Neurotransmitter: [Advanced Biology] A chemical messenger molecule released from synaptic vesicles across a synaptic cleft to transmit signals from one neuron to a target cell.
• Neutralization: [Chemistry / Combined Science] A chemical reaction between an acid and a base that produces a neutral salt and water by combining hydrogen ions and hydroxide ions.
• Neutron: [Chemistry / Physics] A neutral subatomic particle located within the nucleus of an atom, possessing a mass approximately equal to a proton but carrying zero electrical charge.
• Neutron Number: [Chemistry / Physics] The total number of neutrons contained inside the nucleus of an atom, calculated as mass number minus atomic number.
• Neutrophil: [Advanced Biology] A highly abundant type of phagocytic white blood cell characterized by a multi-lobed nucleus, acting as a primary defender during acute bacterial infections.
• Newton: [Physics] The standard SI unit used to measure force; equivalent to the force required to accelerate a mass of one kilogram at a rate of one meter per second squared (1 N = 1 kg⋅m/s²).
• Newton's Laws of Motion: [Physics] Three classical mechanics principles governing the motion of physical bodies, describing inertia (\(1^{\text{st}}\)), force and acceleration (\(2^{\text{nd}}\)), and action and reaction (\(3^{\text{rd}}\)).
• Ngano / Inganekwane: [Languages / Heritage Studies] Traditional fireside folktales told orally by elders to children to pass down historical morals, cultural worldviews, and social values.
• Ngoma: [Heritage Studies / Creative Arts] A traditional drum made from a hollowed wooden log covered with a taut animal skin, forming the rhythmic base of regional music and dances.
• Nhetembo / Izimbongi: [Languages / Creative Arts] Cultural poetry and clan praise poetry spoken aloud to honor family lineages, heroic leaders, historical events, or national identity.
• Niacin / Vitamin B3: [Advanced Biology / Combined Science] An essential water-soluble vitamin required by the body to synthesize coenzymes (NAD and NADP) vital for cellular metabolism, preventing pellagra.
• Niche, Ecological: [Advanced Biology] The specific functional role, position, resource use, and environmental conditions that a species maintains within an ecological ecosystem.
• Nichrome: [Physics / Chemistry] An alloy composed primarily of nickel and chromium, characterized by high electrical resistance and resistance to oxidation at high temperatures, used in heating elements.
• Nickel: [Chemistry] A hard, malleable, silvery-white transition metal element (Ni) with an atomic number of 28, used extensively in stainless steel alloys and electroplating.
• Nictitating Membrane: [Advanced Biology] A translucent or transparent third eyelid present in some animals, such as birds, reptiles, and amphibians, that can be drawn across the eye to protect and moisten it.
• Nidicolous: [Advanced Biology] Offspring or birds that are hatched blind, featherless, and helpless, requiring a prolonged stay in the nest and active parental care.
• Nidifugous: [Advanced Biology] Offspring or birds that are hatched in an advanced developmental state with open eyes and down feathers, capable of leaving the nest almost immediately.
• Ninhydrin: [Chemistry / Advanced Biology] A chemical reagent used to detect the presence of amino acids or proteins, reacting to produce a deep blue or purple color, heavily used in chromatography.
• Niobium: [Chemistry] A soft, ductile, transition metal element (Nb) with an atomic number of 41, used in superconducting alloys and high-strength steels.
• Nitrate: [Chemistry / Combined Science] A polyatomic anion (NO₃⁻) containing nitrogen and oxygen, acting as a highly soluble, primary source of nitrogen for plant growth.
• Nitration: [Chemistry] An organic chemical reaction where a nitro group (-NO₂) is introduced into a chemical compound, typically using a mixture of nitric and sulfuric acids.
• Nitric Acid: [Chemistry] A strong, highly corrosive, suffocating mineral acid (HNO₃) used industrially to manufacture synthetic chemical fertilizers and explosives.
• Nitrification: [Combined Science / Advanced Biology] A two-stage biological process in the nitrogen cycle where soil bacteria convert ammonia into nitrites (Nitrosomonas) and subsequently into nitrates (Nitrobacter).
• Nitrifying Bacteria: [Combined Science / Advanced Biology] Chemoautotrophic soil bacteria responsible for executing the process of nitrification.
• Nitrite: [Chemistry] A polyatomic anion (NO₂⁻) acting as an intermediate compound during the biological process of nitrification.
• Nitro Group: [Chemistry] A highly polar functional group consisting of one nitrogen atom bonded to two oxygen atoms (-NO₂).
• Nitrogen: [Chemistry / Combined Science] A colorless, odorless, diatomic gaseous element (N₂) with an atomic number of 7, making up approximately 78% of Earth's atmosphere, vital for amino acids and proteins.
• Nitrogen Cycle: [Combined Science / Advanced Biology] The continuous global ecological biogeochemical cycle by which nitrogen circulates through the atmosphere, soil, and living organisms via fixation, nitrification, assimilation, and denitrification.
