• Nuclear Pore: [Advanced Biology] Microscopic protein complexes perforating the nuclear envelope that actively regulate the movement of RNA and proteins between the nucleus and the cytoplasm.
• Nucleic Acid: [Chemistry / Advanced Biology] Complex organic macromolecule chains composed of nucleotide monomers, serving as the primary genetic material of living cells, divided into DNA and RNA.
• Nucleolus: [Advanced Biology] A dense, non-membrane-bound structure located inside the cell nucleus, acting as the primary site of ribosome synthesis and assembly.
• Nucleon: [Physics / Chemistry] A collective structural term for either a proton or a neutron located within the nucleus of an atom.
• Nucleoside: [Chemistry / Advanced Biology] A biochemical molecule consisting of a purine or pyrimidine nitrogenous base chemically linked to a five-carbon sugar (ribose or deoxyribose), lacking a phosphate group.
• Nucleosome: [Advanced Biology] The structural unit of eukaryotic chromatin, consisting of a segment of DNA looped around a core complex of eight histone proteins.
• Nucleotide: [Chemistry / Advanced Biology] The structural building block monomer of nucleic acids, composed of a nitrogenous base, a five-carbon sugar, and at least one phosphate group.
• Nucleus: [Advanced Biology / Combined Science] The primary double-membrane-bound cellular organelle housing genetic blueprints (DNA) and coordinating cellular activities like growth and division.
• Nucleus (Atom): [Physics / Chemistry] The dense, positively charged core located at the center of an atom, containing protons and neutrons.
• Nuclide: [Physics / Chemistry] A specific atomic species characterized by the exact composition of its nucleus, defined by its number of protons and neutrons.
• Null Hypothesis: [Advanced Mathematics / Statistics] A statistical statement proposing that no true difference or correlation exists between observed variables, acting as a baseline to be tested for rejection.
• Numerator: [Advanced Mathematics] The top number in a fraction that indicates exactly how many equal parts of a whole are being considered.
• Numerical Aperture: [Physics / Advanced Biology] A dimensionless number measuring the light-gathering capacity and resolution limit of an optical lens system like a microscope objective.
• Nursery Bed: [Combined Science / Agriculture] A small, carefully prepared, sheltered patch of rich soil used to raise delicate seeds and young seedlings under controlled conditions until they are strong enough to be transplanted into fields.
• Nutrient: [Combined Science / Advanced Biology] A chemical substance absorbed by an organism from its environment or diet, required to supply metabolic energy, build tissues, or regulate physiological functions.
• Nutrient Cycle: [Combined Science / Advanced Biology] The continuous ecological movement and exchange of organic and inorganic matter back and forth between living organisms and the physical environment.
• Nyaminyami: [Heritage Studies] The legendary Zambezi River God, historically depicted as a snake-like creature with a fish head, central to the cultural folklore and spiritual heritage of the Tonga people living around Lake Kariba.


Section O

