• Opisthobranch: [Advanced Biology] A diverse subclass of specialized marine gastropod mollusks, including sea slugs, characterized by reduced or absent protective shells.
• Optic Nerve: [Advanced Biology] A bundle of sensory neuronal axons that transmits electrical nerve signals from retinal photoreceptors directly to the visual cortex of the brain.
• Optical Activity: [Chemistry / Physics] The physical property of certain asymmetric chemical compounds or molecules to rotate the plane of polarized light passed through them.
• Optical Density: [Physics] A logarithmic measure of the transmission of light through a medium, dictating how much the speed of light slows down inside a material.
• Optics: [Physics] The structural branch of physics focused on studying the behavior, properties, propagation, and interactions of light waves.
• Order: [Advanced Biology] A structural taxonomic rank in biological classification systems that sits directly below a Class and groups related Families together.
• Ordinate: [Advanced Mathematics] The vertical coordinate of a point in a two-dimensional coordinate system, representing its distance from the horizontal x-axis.
• Ore: [Chemistry / Geography] A naturally occurring solid rock or mineral deposit containing a high enough concentration of a metal to make commercial mining profitable.
• Organ: [Advanced Biology / Combined Science] A structurally distinct group of different biological tissues that work together to perform one or more specific physiological tasks (e.g., heart, kidney, leaf).
• Organelle: [Advanced Biology / Combined Science] A specialized, membrane-bound microscopic structure located inside a cell that performs a specific functional task to sustain life.
• Organic Chemistry: [Chemistry] The scientific branch of chemistry focused on studying the structure, properties, composition, reactions, and synthesis of carbon-containing chemical compounds.
• Organic Compound: [Chemistry] Any chemical compound containing carbon-hydrogen bonds, typical of living or once-living matter.
