• Propyl Group: [Chemistry] A three-carbon alkyl functional group (-C₃H₇) derived by removing one hydrogen atom from a propane molecule chain.
• Protease: [Advanced Biology / Combined Science] A digestive enzyme group that hydrolyzes the peptide bonds linking amino acids in proteins, breaking them down into peptides.
• Protectorate: [Heritage Studies / Civics] An autonomous territory that is formally protected and politically or militarily directed by a more powerful sovereign state.
• Protein: [Chemistry / Advanced Biology] Complex organic macromolecules composed of amino acid chains linked by peptide bonds, vital for structural growth, enzymes, and tissue repair.
• Prothallus: [Advanced Biology] The small, heart-shaped, multicellular gametophyte stage in the life cycle of a fern or lower plant, bearing reproductive organs.
• Prothrombin: [Advanced Biology] A circulating plasma protein synthesized by the liver that is converted into active thrombin during blood clotting cascade.
• Proton: [Chemistry / Physics] A stable, positively charged subatomic particle located within the nucleus of an atom, possessing a mass number of 1.
• Protoplasm: [Advanced Biology] The complete living contents of a cell, combining the internal nucleoplasm and cytoplasm into one living system.
• Protozoan: [Advanced Biology] A diverse group of single-celled, heterotrophic eukaryotic microorganisms that display animal-like behaviors (e.g., Amoeba).
• Proverb: [Languages] A wise, brief traditional saying that conveys a deep life lesson, moral truth, or cultural warning using figurative language.
• Pruning: [Combined Science / Agriculture] The horticultural cutting away of selective dead, overgrown, or diseased plant branches to improve air circulation and encourage fruit growth.
• Pseudopodia: [Advanced Biology] Temporary, fluid-filled extensions of the cytoplasm deployed by cells like Amoeba for locomotion and engulfing food particles.
• Psychrometer: [Geography] A type of hygrometer featuring two thermometers (one dry-bulb, one wet-bulb) used to measure relative atmospheric humidity.
• Ptalin / Salivary Amylase: [Advanced Biology] A digestive enzyme present in saliva that initiates the breakdown of starch into the disaccharide maltose inside the mouth.
• Public Limited Company (PLC): [Commerce] A large incorporated business whose shares are listed on a public stock exchange, allowing capital to be raised from the general public.
• Pulley: [Physics] A simple machine consisting of a grooved wheel holding a taut rope or cable, used to change the direction or magnitude of an effort force.
• Pulmonary Artery: [Advanced Biology] A major blood vessel that carries deoxygenated blood away from the right ventricle of the heart to the lungs for oxygenation.
• Pulmonary Vein: [Advanced Biology] A blood vessel that transports freshly oxygenated blood from the lungs back to the left atrium of the heart.
• Pulse: [Advanced Biology / Combined Science] The rhythmic expansion and contraction of an artery wall felt as blood is pumped through by the heart, tracking heart rate.
• Pumice: [Geography] A highly vesicular, lightweight volcanic igneous rock formed when gas-rich lava cools exceptionally fast, creating a floating porous stone.
• Pump: [Physics / Mechanical Engineering] A mechanical machine used to apply pressure to displace, lift, or circulate fluids or gases through a pipe system.
• Punctuation: [Languages] A system of standard marks used to separate written sentences and make text readable.
• Punnett Square: [Advanced Biology] A grid diagram chart used by geneticists to predict the absolute probability and ratios of genotypes and phenotypes in offspring from a cross.
• Pupa: [Advanced Biology] The inactive, non-feeding developmental stage of an insect undergoing complete metamorphosis, transforming from a larva into an adult inside a cocoon.
• Pupil: [Combined Science / Advanced Biology] The adjustable circular opening in the center of the eye iris through which light rays pass to reach the internal lens and retina.
• Pure Line: [Advanced Biology] A population of organisms that breeds true for a specific trait across generations due to continuous self-pollination or homozygous inbreeding.
• Purlin: [Building Technology] Horizontal structural beams spanning across roof trusses or rafters to support roof sheeting.
• Purine: [Chemistry / Advanced Biology] A double-ringed nitrogenous base compound that forms the structural code for adenine and guanine molecules inside DNA and RNA.
• Pus: [Advanced Biology / Health] A thick, yellowish fluid produced at an infection site, composed of dead white blood cells, cellular debris, and bacteria.
• Pymave / Pyramid: [Advanced Mathematics] A solid three-dimensional geometric shape possessing a polygonal base and triangular outer faces that meet at a single apex point.
• Pyramid of Biomass: [Advanced Biology] A graphical ecological diagram showing the total dry weight or mass of living organic matter present at each trophic level within an ecosystem.
• Pyramid of Energy: [Advanced Biology] An ecological chart showing the absolute total energy content and energy transfer rate recorded at each consecutive trophic level over time.
• Pyramid of Numbers: [Advanced Biology] An ecological chart showing the actual total population count of individual organisms recorded at each successive trophic level.
• Pyrimidine: [Chemistry / Advanced Biology] A single-ringed nitrogenous base compound that forms the structural code for cytosine, thymine, and uracil inside DNA and RNA.
• Pyrites: [Chemistry / Geography] Iron disulfide (FeS₂), a shiny brass-colored mineral deposit commonly called fool's gold, processed to extract sulfur.
• Pyrolysis: [Chemistry] The chemical decomposition of organic materials at high temperatures in the complete absence of oxygen, preventing combustion.
• Pyrometer: [Physics] A non-contact laboratory thermometer instrument used to measure exceptionally high temperatures based on radiant thermal emissions.
• Pyruvic Acid / Pyruvate: [Advanced Biology] A three-carbon organic acid generated as the key end-product of glycolysis in the cytoplasm during cellular respiration.

Section Q

• Quadrant: [Advanced Mathematics] One of the four quarters into which a flat coordinate grid plane is divided by the intersecting x and y axes.
• Quadrant (Ecology): [Advanced Biology] A portable square frame of known area (e.g., 1m²) placed randomly on the ground to count and sample slow-moving plant or insect populations.
• Quadratic Equation: [Advanced Mathematics] A polynomial equation of the second degree, written in the standard form ax² + bx + c = 0.
• Quadrilateral: [Advanced Mathematics] Any flat, two-dimensional geometric polygon that possesses exactly four straight sides and four vertices.
• Qualitative Analysis: [Chemistry] Analytical procedures deployed to identify the specific chemical components or elements present in a sample without measuring amounts.
• Quantitative Analysis: [Chemistry] Analytical tests run to determine the exact amount, concentration, or numeric percentage of elements present in a compound.
• Quantum: [Physics] The absolute minimum discrete amount of a physical property or energy packet involved in an interaction (plural: quanta).
• Quark: [Physics] A fundamental subatomic elementary particle that bonds together via strong interactions to form hadrons like protons and neutrons.
• Quarry: [Geography / Building Technology] An open excavation pit from which building stone, slate, gravel, or limestone is commercially extracted.
• Quarry Tile: [Building Technology] A durable, unglazed flooring tile manufactured from baked clay, used in heavy-wear internal or external floors.
• Quartz: [Geography / Chemistry] A highly abundant crystalline mineral composed of silicon dioxide (SiO₂), known for high hardness and thermal stability.
• Quartzite: [Geography] A hard, non-foliated metamorphic rock formed when pure quartz sandstone is transformed by intense crustal heat and pressure.
• Quaternary Structure: [Advanced Biology / Chemistry] The final structural configuration of a complex protein formed when multiple folded polypeptide chains link together.
• Quicklime: [Chemistry / Building Technology] Calcium oxide (CaO), a white crystalline solid manufactured by roasting limestone in a kiln, reacting violently with water.
• Quotation: [Commerce] A commercial source document sent by a seller detailing the specific prices, descriptions, and terms for supplying requested goods.
• Quotient: [Advanced Mathematics] The final mathematical result or numeric score obtained by dividing one number by another.

Section R

• Radial Symmetry: [Advanced Biology] A geometric body layout where parts are arranged symmetrically around a central axis, allowing the organism to be cut into matching halves along multiple planes (e.g., jellyfish).
• Radian: [Advanced Mathematics] The standard unit of plane angle measurement, defined as the angle subtended at the center of a circle by an arc equal in length to the radius.
• Radiation: [Physics] The emission and propagation of energy through space or a material medium in the form of electromagnetic waves or subatomic particles.
• Radical: [Advanced Mathematics] A mathematical symbol indicating the extraction of a root, such as a square root or cube root.
• Radical (Chemistry): [Chemistry] An atom, molecule, or ion that possesses an unpaired valence electron, making it highly reactive.
• Radicle: [Combined Science / Advanced Biology] The embryonic root structure located inside a seed plant embryo that develops into the primary root system following germination.
• Radioactivity: [Physics / Chemistry] The spontaneous, uncontrolled decay of unstable atomic nuclei, accompanied by the emission of ionizing radiation particles or rays.
• Radiocarbon Dating: [Combined Science / Advanced Biology] A geochronological method tracking the radioactive decay of carbon-14 isotopes to calculate the age of organic materials.
• Radiography: [Physics / Medical] An imaging technique utilizing X-rays or gamma radiation to view the hidden internal structures of an object or body.
• Radiolan: [Advanced Biology] An order of marine protozoans characterized by complex protective siliceous skeletons and fine pseudopodia extensions.
• Radius: [Advanced Mathematics] A straight line segment extending from the absolute center point of a circle or sphere out to its perimeter edge.
• Radius (Anatomy): [Advanced Biology] One of the two long bones in the vertebrate forearm, situated on the thumb side parallel to the ulna.
• Radula: [Advanced Biology] A flexible, tongue-like structural band covered with microscopic chitinous teeth found in mollusks, used to scrape food.
• Rafter: [Building Technology] Sloping timber or steel structural beams extending from the ridge down to the wall plate, forming the primary framework of a pitched roof.
• Rain Gauge: [Combined Science] A marked cylinder tool used to collect and measure the amount of rain that falls over a specific time.
• RAM: [ICT] Volatile primary computer memory that provides temporary, high-speed storage for data and programs currently being processed by the CPU.
• Ramjet: [Physics] A simple compression jet engine that lacks a moving compressor, utilizing the forward speed of the aircraft to compress incoming air.
• Range: [Advanced Mathematics] The numerical difference between the highest and lowest values recorded within a collected data set.
• Range (Functions): [Advanced Mathematics] The complete set of all possible dependent output values that a function can produce from its valid domain inputs.
• Rankine Cycle: [Physics / Thermodynamic Engineering] A theoretical thermodynamic model predicting the performance of steam turbine power systems that convert heat into mechanical work.
• Raoult's Law: [Chemistry] A chemical principle stating that the vapor pressure of an ideal solution is directly proportional to the mole fraction of the solvent added.
• Rare Earth Elements: [Chemistry] A collection of seventeen metallic elements located in the lanthanide series of the periodic table, known for specialized electronic uses.
• Rarefaction: [Physics] A region in a longitudinal sound wave where the particles of the medium are spread furthest apart, exhibiting minimum density and pressure.
• Rate-Determining Step: [Chemistry] The slowest elementary step within a multi-stage chemical reaction pathway, dictating the overall reaction speed.
• Ratio: [Advanced Mathematics] A mathematical comparison of two numeric values showing the relative size of one value to another.
• Ray: [Physics] An idealized narrow beam or straight line representing the directional path along which light energy travels.
• Reactant: [Chemistry] The starting chemical substances that participate and undergo transformation during a chemical reaction, written on the left side of an equation.
• Reaction Rate: [Chemistry] A measure of the speed at which reactants are converted into products during a chemical reaction over time.
• Reagent: [Chemistry] A specific chemical compound or solution added to a system to trigger a chemical reaction or test for the presence of other substances.
• Real Image: [Physics] An optical image formed when light rays actually converge at a point, capable of being projected onto a screen surface.
• Receptor: [Advanced Biology] A specialized cell or protein structure capable of detecting specific external stimuli (such as light or heat) and converting them into nerve signals.
• Recessive Allele: [Advanced Biology] A genetic variant whose phenotypic trait is physically expressed only when an individual inherits two copies (homozygous).
• Reciprocating Engine: [Physics / Mechanical Engineering] A heat engine that utilizes the back-and-forth movement of linear pistons to drive a rotational crankshaft to do work.
• Recombination: [Advanced Biology] The process by which pieces of DNA are broken and combined to produce new combinations of alleles, notable during crossing over in meiosis.
• Recommendations: [General Education] Actionable steps proposed by a student at the conclusion of their research project to solve the stated problem.
• Reconciliation: [Family & Religious Studies] The structural restoration of harmony, peace, and spiritual balance between broken relationships through ceremony or truth-telling.
• Rectifier: [Physics] An electronic circuit component, typically composed of diodes, designed to convert alternating current into direct current.
• Rectilinear Propagation: [Physics] A physical principle stating that light waves travel in perfectly straight lines through a uniform, homogeneous medium.
• Rectum: [Advanced Biology] The terminal muscular section of the mammalian large intestine where undigested waste feces are stored before egestion.
• Red star: [Heritage Studies] Located on the national flag, it represents internationalism, bright future prospects, and state development values.
• Redox Reaction: [Chemistry] A chemical reaction involving the simultaneous transfer of electrons between substances, combining oxidation and reduction.
• Reducing Agent / Reductant: [Chemistry] A chemical reactant that donates electrons to another substance in a redox reaction, causing the other substance to be reduced while being oxidized itself.
• Reducing Sugar: [Chemistry / Combined Science] Any carbohydrate sugar capable of acting as a reducing agent because it possesses a free aldehyde or ketone group (e.g., glucose, maltose).
• Reduction: [Chemistry] A chemical half-reaction characterized by the gain of electrons, a decrease in chemical oxidation state, or the loss of oxygen.
• Referee: [Physical Education] The official on-field judge tasked with enforcing game rules fairly and ensuring player safety during sports.
• Refining: [Chemistry / Metallurgy] The industrial purification process used to remove impurities and foreign traces from extracted metals or crude petroleum.
• Reflecting Telescope: [Physics] An astronomical telescope that utilizes a curved primary mirror to gather and focus incoming light rays to view distant stars.
• Reflection: [Physics] The abrupt rebounding of a wave train, light ray, or sound wave off a boundary surface between two different media back into its original medium.
• Reflex Arc: [Advanced Biology] The neural pathway that controls an immediate, involuntary action or reflex response, connecting a receptor directly to an effector via the spinal cord.
• Reflecting Dynamics: [Physics] Optical behaviors where light rays change direction upon striking a shiny surface, obeying the law that the angle of incidence equals the angle of reflection.
• Reforestation: [Combined Science] The deliberate replanting of trees in forest areas that have been depleted due to logging, clearing, or wildfires.
• Refracting Telescope: [Physics] An astronomical telescope utilizing a series of curved transparent glass lenses to gather and bend light rays to magnify distant objects.
• Refraction: [Physics] The bending or change in direction of a wave ray when it passes obliquely from one medium to another of different optical density due to a shift in wave speed.
• Refraction Index: [Physics] A mathematical ratio measuring how much a ray of light bends when entering a specific medium, calculated as the speed of light in a vacuum divided by speed in the medium.
• Refractory Period: [Advanced Biology] A brief time window following the transmission of a nerve impulse during which a neuron axon membrane cannot generate another action potential.
• Refrigerant: [Physics / Chemistry] A volatile fluid compound cycling through a refrigeration system that absorbs heat through evaporation and releases it through condensation.
• Regeneration: [Advanced Biology] The biological capacity of an organism to replace, repair, or regrow damaged or lost tissues, limbs, or organs.
• Register: [Languages] The choice of vocabulary, structure, and tone a speaker uses based on their social relationship with the listener.
• Regolith: [Geography] A loose layer of fragmented weathered rock debris, sand, and soil covering the solid bedrock layer of Earth's surface.
• Regular Polygon: [Advanced Mathematics] Any flat geometric polygon possessing sides that are all identical in length and interior angles that are equal.
• Reinforced Concrete: [Building Technology] Concrete containing embedded steel bars or mesh to add tensile strength to its natural compressive strength.
• Relative Atomic Mass: [Chemistry] The weighted average mass of an atom of an element compared to exactly one-twelfth of the mass of a carbon-12 atom.
• Relative Density: [Physics] A dimensionless ratio measuring the density of a substance relative to the density of pure water at a specified temperature.
• Relative Humidity: [Geography / Combined Science] A meteorological percentage ratio measuring the actual volume of water vapor in air relative to the maximum volume air could hold at that temperature.
• Relaxation: [Advanced Biology] The physiological process where muscle fibers return to their elongated, resting state following contraction.
• Relay: [Physics / Electronics] An electromagnetic electrical switch component that uses a low-power signal to open or close a separate high-power circuit path.
• Relay Neuron: [Advanced Biology] A specialized neuron located entirely within the central nervous system that bridges and transfers impulses between sensory and motor neurons.
• Remidies / Recommendations: [General Education] Proactive solutions or action steps suggested at the conclusion of a project report to resolve identified problems.
• Renal Artery: [Advanced Biology] A major blood vessel branching off the aorta that delivers high-pressure blood to the kidney for filtration.
• Renal Pelvis: [Advanced Biology] A funnel-shaped cavity in the center of the kidney that collects urine from the collecting ducts, channeling it down into the ureter.
• Renal Vein: [Advanced Biology] A blood vessel that carries filtered, cleansed blood away from the kidney back into the inferior vena cava.
• Renewable Energy: [Combined Science] Clean power harvested from endless natural sources like sunlight, wind, or moving water.
• Rennet / Rennin: [Advanced Biology / Chemistry] A digestive enzyme complex secreted by young ruminants that coagulates milk proteins, transforming liquid milk into solid curds.
• Reparation: [Heritage Studies / Civics] Financial or political compensation provided to repair or amend past historical damages, colonization impacts, or war losses.
• Replication: [Advanced Biology] The biological process where a cell duplicates its double-stranded genomic DNA molecules prior to cell division.
• Repressor Protein: [Advanced Biology] A specialized regulatory protein molecule that binds to a bacterial DNA operator segment, blocking gene transcription.
• Reproduction: [Advanced Biology / Combined Science] The fundamental biological process by which living organisms generate offspring or new individuals, preserving the species.
• Reptile: [Advanced Biology] A class of cold-blooded, amniotic vertebrates characterized by breathing with lungs, laying shelled eggs, and skin covered in dry scales.
• Reservoir: [Combined Science / Geography] A massive man-made lake or storage basin created by building a dam across a river, storing water for community use.
• Residual Volume: [Advanced Biology] The volume of air that remains trapped inside human lungs after a person executes the most forceful exhalation possible.
• Resin: [Advanced Biology / Chemistry] A viscous, hydrocarbon-rich secretion produced by many plants, notably conifers, that hardens when exposed to air, processed to manufacture varnishes.
• Resistance: [Physics] A measure of the opposition a component or material system presents to the free flow of an electric current through it.
• Resistivity: [Physics] An intrinsic material property quantifying how strongly a specific substance opposes the flow of electric current, regardless of shape.
• Resistor: [Physics] A passive electronic circuit component manufactured to provide a specific, known electrical resistance value within a circuit path.
• Resolution: [Advanced Biology / Physics] The capacity of an optical lens system or microscope to separate and distinguish two closely spaced points as distinct images.
• Resonance: [Physics] A physical state occurring when an oscillating system is driven by an external force whose frequency matches the system's natural frequency, causing large amplitudes.
• Respiration: [Advanced Biology / Combined Science] The fundamental biochemical cellular process where organic glucose molecules are systematically broken down to extract chemical energy (ATP).
• Respiratory Surface: [Advanced Biology] A specialized thin, moist, highly vascularized biological boundary membrane across which gas exchange occurs (e.g., alveoli walls).
• Response: [Advanced Biology] The specific physiological or behavioral reaction executed by a living organism in response to a detected external stimulus.
• Resting Potential: [Advanced Biology] The constant electrical potential difference across a neuron axon membrane when it is at rest, typically valued at approximately -70 millivolts.
• Restitution: [Physics] A materials science measure of the elasticity of a collision, tracking how much kinetic energy is retained after objects rebound.
• Restriction Enzyme: [Advanced Biology] A specialized molecular biology enzyme that scans a DNA strand and cuts it at a specific nucleotide target sequence, heavily used in engineering.
• Resuscitation (CPR): [Physical Education] An emergency first-aid procedure combining chest compressions and rescue breaths to help a person whose heart has stopped.
• Retained Earnings: [Principles of Accounts] The cumulative net profits kept within a corporate enterprise after dividends have been distributed to shareholders.
• Retina: [Advanced Biology / Combined Science] The delicate inner layer of the eye containing photoreceptor cells (rods and cones) that capture light rays and convert them into nerve signals.
• Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG): [ICT] An AI system architecture that searches an external database for facts before generating a response to ensure accurate answers.
• Reversible Reaction: [Chemistry] A chemical reaction where the conversion of reactants into products occurs simultaneously with the conversion of products back into reactants.
• Rheostat: [Physics] An electrical circuit component featuring a variable resistor used to manually adjust and control the magnitude of current passing through.
• Rhizome: [Advanced Biology] A thick, horizontal underground plant stem structure that stores resources and shoots out lateral roots and stems (e.g., ginger).
• Rhodium: [Chemistry] A rare, highly unreactive, silvery-white transition metal element (Rh) with an atomic number of 45, processed as an industrial catalyst.
• Rhodopsin: [Advanced Biology] A highly light-sensitive biological pigment molecule located inside the rod cells of the eye retina, vital for night vision.
• Rhombus: [Advanced Mathematics] A flat, two-dimensional geometric parallelogram whose four straight sides are all identical in length.
• Rhythm: [Creative Arts] A strong, regular, repeated pattern of sounds, beats, or movements that forms the structural backbone of music and dance.
• Riant / Reactant: [Chemistry] Chemical substances that enter into and undergo structural changes during a chemical interaction.
• Riboflavin / Vitamin B2: [Advanced Biology] An essential water-soluble vitamin required by organisms to synthesize coenzymes vital for cellular respiration electron chains.
• Ribose: [Chemistry / Advanced Biology] A five-carbon monosaccharide pentose sugar molecule (C₅H₁₀O₅) that forms the structural backbone framework of RNA.
• Ribosome: [Advanced Biology] A tiny cellular structure composed of RNA and proteins responsible for manufacturing proteins by translating mRNA codes.
• Ribozyme: [Advanced Biology] An RNA molecule possessing specialized catalytic enzyme properties, capable of accelerating specific biochemical reactions.
• Rickets: [Combined Science / Advanced Biology] A bone development disease in children caused by a critical lack of vitamin D or calcium, resulting in soft, weak, bowed leg bones.
• Riddle: [Languages] A puzzling question or statement phrased as a word game to test a child's critical thinking and vocabulary.
• Riffle: [Geography / Mining] A sequence of raised structural ridges placed along the base of a mining sluice box to capture heavy mineral grains like gold.
• Rift Valley: [Geography] A steep-sided valley formed on a continental plate when crustal tension forces cause blocks of land to slide downward between parallel faults.
• Right Angle: [Advanced Mathematics] An exact 90-degree angle formed when two perpendicular lines intersect perfectly.
• Right-Angled Triangle: [Advanced Mathematics] Any geometric triangle that contains exactly one internal angle measuring 90 degrees.
• Rigid Body: [Physics] A theoretical solid object whose internal dimensions and shape remain perfectly unchanged when external forces are applied.
• Ring Topology: [ICT] A network layout where all computer nodes are connected sequentially in a continuous closed loop, passing data tokens in one direction.
• Riser: [Building Technology] The vertical face or board running between consecutive steps on a staircase.
• River Basin: [Geography] The total geographic land area drained completely by a main river and all of its associated tributaries.
• River Capture: [Geography] A geomorphological event where a river with intense erosive power cuts backward, breaching a watershed to divert the headwaters of a neighboring river.
• RNA (Ribonucleic Acid): [Chemistry / Advanced Biology] A single-stranded nucleic acid molecule composed of nucleotide monomers containing ribose, responsible for protein synthesis codes.
• Robot: [ICT / Engineering] An automated mechanical machine programmed with algorithms to execute complex physical tasks independently or via sensors.
• Rock: [Geography / Combined Science] A solid, naturally occurring mass composed of a mixture of one or more mineral deposits, forming Earth's crust.
• Rock Art: [Heritage Studies] Ancient paintings or engravings created on natural rock surfaces or cave shelters by early human hunter-gatherers.
• Rock Cycle: [Geography / Combined Science] The continuous geological process by which rocks are slowly transformed across deep time between igneous, sedimentary, and metamorphic states.
• Rod Cell: [Advanced Biology] A highly light-sensitive photoreceptor cell located in the retina of the eye eye, specialized for black-and-white vision in dim light.
• Rodenticide: [Combined Science / Agriculture] A toxic chemical compound or bait deployment used to eradicate or manage destructive rodent populations on farms or granaries.
• Rolling: [Materials Science] An industrial manufacturing process where metal blocks are passed through rotating rollers to reduce thickness and create sheets.
• ROM: [ICT] Non-volatile primary computer memory that contains permanent, unchangeable boot instructions needed to turn on a device.
• Roof Truss: [Building Technology] A rigid structural framework of timbers or steel sections arranged in triangles to span wide open spaces to support roofs.
• Root: [Combined Science / Advanced Biology] The underground vascular organ of a plant that anchors it into the soil while absorbing water and vital mineral ions.
• Root Cap: [Advanced Biology] A protective, thimble-shaped cone of cells covering the delicate growing tip of a plant root, shielding it as it pushes through soil.
• Root Hair: [Combined Science / Advanced Biology] Microscopic, single-cell tubular extensions of root epidermal cells, vastly increasing surface area to absorb soil water via osmosis.
• Root Pressure: [Advanced Biology] The hydrostatic pressure generated within root xylem cells by the active transport of mineral ions, forcing water upward into the stem.
• Root Stock: [Building Technology / Agriculture] A rooted plant stem onto which a separate desired plant graft or bud is chemically fused during grafting.
• Rotary Engine: [Physics / Mechanical Engineering] An internal combustion engine where the cylinders rotate around a fixed central crankshaft, or where a triangular rotor turns inside a chamber.
• Rotation: [Advanced Mathematics] A geometric transformation that turns a flat shape or point around a fixed center reference point by a specific angle.
• Rote Learning: [General Education] A learning technique focusing on memorizing facts through pure repetition rather than building deep understanding.
• Rotor: [Physics / Engineering] The rotating or spinning component located inside an electric motor, generator, alternator, or turbine system.
• Router: [ICT] A network hardware device that forwards data packets between separate computer networks, directing internet traffic.
• Row Planting: [Combined Science / Agriculture] Sowing seeds in straight, measured lines to make weeding, fertilizing, and harvesting easier.
• Rubidium: [Chemistry] A soft, highly reactive, silvery-white alkali metal element (Rb) with an atomic number of 37, reacting violently with water.
• Rudd Concession: [Heritage Studies] A written agreement signed in 1888 between King Lobengula and agents of Cecil Rhodes, granting exclusive mining privileges over all minerals.
• Rumen: [Combined Science / Agriculture] The massive initial fermentation compartment of a ruminant animal's stomach where bacteria and protozoans break down tough plant cellulose.
• Ruminant: [Combined Science / Agriculture] An herbivorous mammal with a specialized four-compartment stomach that allows it to digest tough plant matter.
• Rumination: [Combined Science / Agriculture] The physiological process where a ruminant regurgitates semi-digested fodder (cud) from the rumen back into the mouth to chew it further.
• Runner: [Advanced Biology] A specialized horizontal stem that grows along the soil surface, striking out roots and shoots at nodes to achieve vegetative reproduction (e.g., strawberry).
• Runoff: [Geography / Combined Science] Rainwater that flows over the top surface of the ground into local rivers and dams rather than soaking down into the soil.
• Rural-Urban Migration: [Geography / Combined Science] The movement of people away from rural villages to cities in search of employment and social amenities.
• Rust: [Chemistry / Combined Science] A flaky, reddish-brown iron oxide compound (Fe₂O₃ ⋅ nH₂O) formed when iron or steel structures corrode in the presence of water and oxygen.
• Ruthenium: [Chemistry] A rare, hard transition metal element (Ru) with an atomic number of 44, processed as an industrial alloy to add hardness.
• Rutherford Scattering: [Physics] A physics experiment where alpha particles were fired at thin gold foil, proving that atoms possess a dense, positively charged nucleus.


Section R (Continued)

• Rutherford Scattering: [Physics / Combined Science] The elastic deflection of alpha particles through large angles when fired at a thin gold foil, demonstrating that an atom's positive charge and mass are concentrated in a tiny nucleus.
• Rutherfordium: [Chemistry] A highly radioactive synthetic transition metal element (Rf) with an atomic number of 104, belonging to the transactinide series.

Section S

• Sacraments: [Family & Religious Studies] Sacred, symbolic religious rituals recognized as outward signs of inward spiritual grace, such as baptism or holy communion.
• Sadza: [Heritage Studies / Combined Science] The staple carbohydrate food of Zimbabwe, prepared by boiling ground maize-meal, sorghum, or millet into a thick, dense porridge.
• Salinity: [Combined Science / Geography] A measure of the total concentration of dissolved mineral salts, primarily sodium chloride, contained within a body of water or soil profile.
• Salt: [Chemistry / Combined Science] An ionic compound generated when the hydrogen ion of an acid is replaced completely or partially by a metal ion or ammonium ion.
• San: [Heritage Studies / History] The indigenous hunter-gatherer people of Southern Africa, celebrated for their deep historical connection to the land and rock art across rock shelters.
• Sanitation: [Combined Science / Advanced Biology] The hygienic maintenance of clean living spaces through safe disposal of human waste and trash, preventing the transmission of infectious pathogens.
• Saponification: [Chemistry / Combined Science] The chemical hydrolysis of an ester or fat under alkaline conditions using a strong base to manufacture soap and glycerol.
• Saprophyte: [Advanced Biology] An organism, especially a fungus or bacterium, that feeds on dead and decaying organic matter by secreting extracellular digestive enzymes.
• Sarcomere: [Advanced Biology] The fundamental microscopic structural and functional contractile unit of a myofibril in striated muscle tissue, bounded by Z-lines.
• Saturated Compound: [Chemistry] An organic chemical compound whose carbon atoms are linked entirely by single covalent bonds, containing no double or triple bonds.
• Saturated Solution: [Chemistry] A chemical solution that contains the maximum possible amount of dissolved solute at a specific temperature.
• Savannah: [Combined Science / Geography] A wide, open tropical grassland ecosystem dotted with scattered trees, covering most of Zimbabwe’s landscape.
• Scalar: [Physics / Advanced Mathematics] A physical quantity that is completely defined by its numerical magnitude or size alone, possessing no direction.
• Scaffolding: [Building Technology] A temporary external structural framework of metal pipes or timber planks built to support workers and materials during the construction or repair of a building.
• Scaffolding: [Pedagogy / Educational Psychology] Temporary instructional support structures provided by teachers to help students master complex skills step-by-step.
• Scapula / Shoulder Blade: [Advanced Biology] A flat, triangular bone located on the dorsal aspect of the thoracic skeleton in vertebrates, articulating with the humerus.
• Schistosomiasis / Bilharzia: [Combined Science / Advanced Biology] A chronic parasitic disease caused by tiny flatworms (Schistosoma) that breed inside freshwater snails, entering humans through skin contact with contaminated water.
• Science: [Combined Science] The systematic study of the physical and natural world through structured observation, experimentation, and profiling.
• Sclera: [Advanced Biology] The tough, fibrous, white outermost protective layer of the eye that maintains its shape and protects internal structures.
• Sclerenchyma: [Advanced Biology] A supportive plant tissue composed of dead cells with heavily lignified, thick secondary cell walls, providing mechanical strength.
• Scramble for Africa: [Heritage Studies / History] The rapid, competitive invasion, annexation, division, and colonization of African territories by European empires between 1881 and 1914.
• Screed: [Building Technology] A smooth, level layer of fine concrete mortar applied over a rough concrete floor slab to prepare it for final floor finishes.
• Screw: [Physics] A simple machine consisting of an inclined plane wrapped around a central cylinder, used to apply a large axial force.
